{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.986667+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_027e74df1bc5ffc55891d14409ca226cf862d81c3a71408a1e940ac32a8d46eb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition vp of arXiv:2412.00865, 'On the spectral problem and fractional diffusion limit for Fokker-Planck with(-out) drift '] \item Assume (a1 of the paper)--(a5 of the paper). Let $\alpha:=\frac{2\gamma-d+2}{3}$ for all $\gamma \in (\frac{d}{2},\frac{d+4}{2})$.  Then, the eigenvalue $\mu(\eta)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\mu(\eta) - \mathrm{i} \eta j^\vare_1 = \kappa \eta^{\alpha}\big(1+O(\eta^\alpha)\big) ,
\end{equation}
where we recall that 
\begin{equation}
j^\vare_1 := \left\{\begin{array}{l}  0,   \hspace{3.9cm} \mbox{ if } \quad \displaystyle 2\gamma\in \left(d,d+1\right),  \\
 \\
 \displaystyle \int_{\{|v|  \leqslant \vare^{-\frac{1}{3}}\}} v_1 M^2(v)  \mathrm{d} v , \quad  \ \ \mbox{if } \quad 2\gamma = d+1 ,  \\
 \\
\displaystyle \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} v_1 M^2(v)  \mathrm{d} v ,   \hspace{1.6cm}  \mbox{ if } \quad  2\gamma\in \left(d+1,d+4\right)  ,
\end{array}\right.  
\end{equation}
and $\kappa$ is a positive constant given by
\begin{equation}
\kappa :=  - \int_{\{|s_1| >0\}} s_1 m(s)  \mathrm{Im} H_0(s) \ \mathrm{d}s ,
\end{equation}
and where $H_0$ is the unique solution to  
\begin{equation}
- \frac{1}{m} \nabla_s \cdot \bigg(m^2 \ \nabla_s\bigg(\frac{H_0}{m}\bigg)\bigg) + \mathrm{i} s_1 H_0 = 0 \quad \mbox{ in } \  \mathcal{D}'(\mathbb{R}^d\setminus\{0\})  ,
\end{equation}
satisfying \begin{equation}
\int_{\{|s_1|\geqslant 1\}}|H_0(s)|^2\mathrm{d}s <+\infty \qquad  \mbox{ and } \qquad   H_0(s)\underset{0}{\sim} m(s).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

