{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:02:28.581235+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_042c06441b50d8047bd5a04f3fbd7c6518dfc6cbe1d7b7b0295dc528a8cd4e16","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 6 (Second-order junction expansion) of arXiv:2609.01319, 'Matched Queries for Curvature and Density at Branching Junctions'] Under \cref{ass:jet}, for every compact $\cQ\subset\R^D$ and every fixed integer $m\ge 0$, there are $C_{m,\cQ}<\infty$ and $\sigma_0>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
\norm{\widetilde Q_\sigma-Q_0-\sigma Q_1}_{C^m(\cQ)}
\le C_{m,\cQ}\sigma^{1+\alpha},
\qquad 0<\sigma\le\sigma_0,

\end{equation}
where $\widetilde Q_\sigma$ is the locally rescaled Gaussian convolution after removal of its $z$-independent normalization. Consequently, for $m\ge1$,
\begin{equation}
\norm{F_\sigma-F_0-\sigma G}_{C^{m-1}(\cQ)}
\le C'_{m,\cQ}\sigma^{1+\alpha},

\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
F_0=\nabla\log Q_0,
\qquad
G=\nabla\left(\frac{Q_1}{Q_0}\right).

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.024496+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_04300ac6e455a1db307daaf6beada6f486430ba6a5facb46f767e3eaa6d835bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 (\citeauthor{RP81}, \citeyear{RP81}) of arXiv:2606.06739, 'The relationship between the transition functions of the labeled and unlabeled versions of'] Let $E$ and $E_0$ be complete separable metric spaces, and let $\Phi:E\mapsto E_0$ be Borel measurable and surjective.  Let $\lambda$ be a one-step transition function from $E_0$ to $E$ $($i.e., $y\in E_0\mapsto\lambda(y,\cdot)\in\mathscr{P}(E)$ is Borel measurable$)$ satisfying $\lambda(y,\Phi^{-1}(y))=1$ for all $y\in E_0$.  Let $P(t,x,\cdot)$ be the transition function for a Markov process $X$ in $E$ with initial distribution $\mu:=\int_{E_0}\lambda(y,\cdot)\,\mu_0(dy)$ for some $\mu_0\in\mathscr{P}(E_0)$.  If the condition
\begin{equation}
\int_E P(t,x,\cdot)\,\lambda(y,dx)=\int_E\bigg[\int_{E_0}\lambda(z,\cdot)\,P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(dz))\bigg]\lambda(y,dx),
\end{equation}
holds for every $t>0$ and $y\in E_0$, then $Y:=\Phi\circ X$ is a Markov process in $E_0$ with initial distribution $\mu_0$ and transition function
\begin{equation}
Q(t,y,\cdot):=\int_E P(t,x,\Phi^{-1}(\cdot))\,\lambda(y,dx).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.823937+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_043a7b55dc29d8100596f91cf9f61913fa51c8ca5748ac61f7dd90b4cedf6ec1","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 29 of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] The following conditions are equivalent:
