{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:32:56.095940+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0570ff256ef43b732f5d5e4a2733bb749dcaf0469a3b4782da2ec8cb2dbd2b7f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor corolary:condlaw of arXiv:2411.12717, 'Excursion theory for Markov processes indexed by Lévy trees'] Under $\mathbb{P}_{0,x,0}$ and conditionally on $\tilde{\mathcal{E}}$, the point measures 
$$\mathcal{E}_+(\mathrm{d} s, \mathrm{d}\upvarrho, \mathrm{d} \omega):=\mathbbm{1}_{L_\sigma^* (\upvarrho,\omega)>0}\mathcal{E}(\mathrm{d} s, \mathrm{d}\upvarrho, \mathrm{d} \omega) \quad \text{ and } \quad  \mathcal{E}_0(\mathrm{d} s, \mathrm{d}\upvarrho, \mathrm{d} \omega):=\mathbbm{1}_{L_\sigma^* (\upvarrho,\omega)=0}\mathcal{E}(\mathrm{d} s, \mathrm{d}\upvarrho, \mathrm{d} \omega)$$ are independent, and their conditional distributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[\emph{(i)}]The conditional distribution of $\mathcal{E}_+$ given $\tilde{\mathcal{E}}$ is characterised by the relation: 
     \begin{align}
         \mathbb{E}_{0,x,0}\Big[  g( \, \widetilde{\mathcal{E}} \, ) \exp \big(-  \langle \mathcal{E}_+,f \rangle  \big)      
        \Big]  
        =  \mathbb{E}_{0,x,0}\Big[  g(\, \widetilde{\mathcal{E}} \, ) \prod_{z \in \mathcal{D}_{\widetilde{X}}} \mathbf{N}_x^{*, \Delta \widetilde{X}_z }\Big( \exp \big( -f(z , \rho, W ) \big) \Big)  \Big],  
    \end{align}
    which holds for every non-negative measurable function $f$ on $\mathbb{R}_+\times \mathbb{D}(\mathbb{R}_+, \mathcal{M}_f(\mathbb{R}_+)\times \mathcal{W}_{E})$.
    \item[\emph{(ii)}]  The point measure  $\mathcal{E}_0$ conditionally on $\widetilde{\mathcal{E}}$ is a Poisson point measure  with intensity measure given by 
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathbbm{1}_{[ 0,A_\sigma )}{\mathrm{d}} t \otimes  \mathbf{N}_{x}^*( \, \cdot \cap \{L_\sigma^*=0\}). 
    \end{equation*}
\end{itemize}
Furthermore, the previous statements hold if we replace $\mathbb{P}_{0,x,0}$ by the excursion measure $\mathbb{N}_{x,0}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:28:31.998337+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_057be3af148d4fc49a63ed21384743cb98070ed3527eef4d9374112dd341d8b0","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2512.02661, 'Worst-case mixing estimates for Brownian motion with semipermeable barriers'] For every $\gamma \in (0,1)$ there exists a constant $c>0$ depending only on $\gamma$ such that the following holds.
