{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.682132+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_06f2d664e20d8c205d3cf6404ad62d3c36c8d6deef3b46b6da15ecd45675acd8","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition NEW-d:lip_assum of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] The jointly measurable functions $(b,\sigma) :[0,\infty) \times \mathbb {M}  \times \hat\mathcal{N}_{\mathbb {M} }\to \mathbb{R}^{d'}\times (\mathbb{R}^{d'} \times \mathbb{R}^{d'})$ are said to be \emph{Lipschitz diffusion coefficients} if they satisfy the non-anticipative property, meaning that for all $t \in [0,t_0]$, $x \in {\cal X }$, any finite subset ${\cal X }' \subset {\cal X }$, and for $m_1[x]$, $m_2[x] \in \mathbb {M} $, with $m_1[{\cal X }']:=\sum_{x'\in{\cal X }'}\delta_{m_1[x']}\in\hat \mathcal{N}_{\mathbb {M} }$, and similarly for $m_2[{\cal X }']\in\hat\mathcal{N}_{\mathbb {M} }$, we have: 
\begin{align*}b(t,m_1[x], m_1[{\cal X }']) &= b(t,m_2[x],m_2[{\cal X }']),\\
\sigma(t,m_1[x], m_1[{\cal X }']) &= \sigma(t,m_2[x], m_2[{\cal X }'])\\ \noalign{whenever}
m_1[x,t] &= m_2[x,t],\\
m_1[{\cal X }',t] &= m_2[{\cal X }',t].
\end{align*}
Furthermore, there exists a {\em Lipschitz diffusion constant} $K_t < \infty$ such that:
\begin{align*}
& \sup_{s\in[0,t]}|b(s,m_1[x], m_1[{\cal X }']) - b(s,m_2[x], m_2[{\cal X }])|
+ |\sigma(s,m_1[x],m_1[{\cal X }']) - \sigma(s,m_2[x], m_2[{\cal X }'])| \\
& \qquad \leq K_t \Big( \|m_1[x,t] - m_2[x,t]\| + \frac{1}{|{\cal X }'|}\sum_{x' \in {\cal X }'} \|m_1[x',t] - m_2[x',t]\| \Big),
\end{align*}
with the convention that $\frac{1}{|{\cal X }'|}\sum_{x'\in{\cal X }'}(\dots)=0$ if ${\cal X }'=\emptyset$. We assume, moreover, that  $\sup_{t\in[0,t_0]}K_t<\infty$ and that $K_t$ are increasing in $t$.
Finally, using the zero-function notation $0[x]\in\mathbb {M} $ and $0[{\cal X }']):=\sum_{x'\in{\cal X }'}\delta_{0[x']}$, the coefficients $(b,\sigma)$ satisfy (uniformly in $x$ and ${\cal X }'$)
$$\int_0^{t_0}(|b(s,0[x],0[{\cal X }'])|^2 + |\sigma(s,0[x],0[{\cal X }'])|^2) \mathrm{d} s < K_{t_0}.$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.367958+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_06f61d46579dfe07209593857ada21853a3700079b34aa9e9e123cdbed39ec19","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm mainresult of arXiv:2607.28817, 'Limiting spectral distribution for the adjacency matrix of the Watts--Strogatz random grap'] Let $(K_n)_{n\ge1}$ be a sequence of even integers satisfying $0< K_n < n$ for all sufficiently large values of $n$, and let $(p_n)_{n\ge1}$ be a sequence in $(0,1]$. Let $A_n$ denote the adjacency matrix of $WS_n(K_n, p_n)$. If
\begin{equation}
    p_nK_n\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;\infty
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \frac{K_n}{n}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;0
\end{equation}
then the empirical spectral distribution $\mu_{\frac{A_n}{\sqrt{K_n p_n(2- p_n)}}}$ of the normalized adjacency matrix $\frac{A_n}{\sqrt{K_n p_n(2- p_n)}}$ converges weakly in probability to the semicircle law $\mu_{\mathrm{SC}}$ as $n \to \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.854905+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_06f87fc6ecce4de9b51ac1dfc73a12cdd1ddf093d1b8dd8da636da45dbda695f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem RPD_Uniformity of arXiv:2607.12061, 'Models for species evolution with random deaths'] Let $f^\dagger_c=\frac{qr}{p}$. Then for any $f^\dagger_c\le a<b\le1$,
