Then, BSDE (equation BSDE1.1 of the paper) admits a unique $L^p$ solution $(y_t,z_t)_{t\in[0,\tau]}$ in the space of $H_\tau^p(0;{\mathbb R}^{k}\times{\mathbb R}^{k\times d})$. Moreover, if $|\xi|+\int_0^\tau |g(s,0,0)|{\rm d}s\in L^\infty(\Omega,\mathcal{F}_\tau, \mathbb{P};{\mathbb R})$, then $(y_t)_{t\in[0,\tau]}\in S^\infty({\mathbb R}^k)$.\vspace{0.1cm}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.229439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0790898602030a71aacfe28fec9473546d1fe7d50d1efbab8a17d54a668abe30","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 10 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] There exist $c,C>0$ such that for each $n\in \N$, there exists an event $\mathsf{B}_n$ with
 \begin{equation} 
     \mathbf{P}_n(\mathsf{B}_n) \geq 1-C \exp\big(-cn^{9/8} \big) 
 \end{equation} on which:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)]   The unwrapped patched directed landscape $\Lpat$ is an element of the space of upper semi-continuous functions $ \USC(\SetR,\R \cup \{-\infty\})$.
    \item[(ii)]   The unwrapped patched directed landscape and its unwrapped inherited geodesics satisfy the following decomposition property: If $\bar\pi$ is an unwrapped inherited geodesic from $(x,s)$ to $(y,t)$ for some $(x,s,y,t)\in\SetR$ and $\tilde s\in (s,t)$ then $\bar\pi$ restricted to $[s,\tilde s]$ and $\bar\pi$ restricted to $[\tilde s, t]$ are both inherited geodesics, and 
\begin{equation}
\Lpat(x,s;y,t) = \Lpat(x,s;\bar\pi(\tilde s),\tilde s) + \Lpat(\bar\pi(\tilde s),\tilde s;y,t).
\end{equation} 
\item[(iii)] For every inherited geodesic $\pi \colon [s,t] \rightarrow \R$ in the unwrapped patched directed landscape, 
\begin{equation}
  \int \textup{d}\Lpat\circ \pi  = \Lpat(s,\pi(s);t,\pi(t)) . 
\end{equation}
    \item[(iv)]  For all  $(x,s;y,t)\in \SetR \cap \mathcal{V}_{n}$, we have that 
    \begin{equation}
     \Lpat(x,s;y,t) > - \infty \quad \text{ for all } (x,s;y,t)\in \SetR \cap \mathcal{V}_{n} , 
 \end{equation}
and there are unique rightmost and leftmost inherited geodesics for $ \Lpat$, which we denote by $\pinpt_{x,s;y,t}$ and $\pinmt_{x,s;y,t}$, respectively.
\item[(v)]  The result in \hyperref[eq:Patched2]{(ii)} holds true with $\mathcal{V}_n$ replaced by $\mathcal{D}_{n^{-1}}$ from \eqref{eq:ProbabilityBound} and/or when $\Lpat$ is replaced by $\Lpa$ (in which case we write $\pinp_{x,s;y,t}$ and $\pinm_{x,s;y,t}$ for the rightmost and leftmost wrapped inherited geodesics).
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

