{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.447530+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07e0792a89832813c258171c4281aed210b8852108934d524a80cd53713e8178","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm fixed K 't Hooft master loop equation for surface sum of arXiv:2411.11676, 'Surface sums for lattice Yang--Mills in the large-$N$ limit'] Fix a non-null loop $\ell$ and a plaquette assignment  $K:\mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d}\to\mathbb{N}$. Let $\mathbf{e}$ be a specific edge of the loop $\ell$. Suppose that the  edge $\mathbf{e}$ is a copy of the lattice edge $e\in E_{\mathbb{Z}^d}$. Then
\begin{align*}
	\phi^K(\ell) =&\sum_{\{\ell_1,\ell_2\}\in \mathbb{S}_-(\mathbf{e},\ell)}
	\sum_{K_1+K_2=K}
	\phi^{K_1}(\ell_1)\phi^{K_2}(\ell_2) - \sum_{\{\ell_1,\ell_2\}\in \mathbb{S}_+(\mathbf{e},\ell)}
	\sum_{K_1+K_2=K}\phi^{K_1}(\ell_1)\phi^{K_2}(\ell_2) \\
	&+ \upbeta
	\sum_{p\in \mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d}(e^{-1},K)}
	\phi^{K\setminus p}(\ell \ominus_{\mathbf{e}}p)
	-\upbeta\sum_{q\in \mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d}(e,K)}
	\phi^{K\setminus q}(\ell \oplus_{\mathbf{e}}q),
\end{align*}
where $\mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d}(e,K)$ denotes the collection of plaquettes in $\mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d}$ containing $e$ as one of the four boundary edges (with the correct orientation) and such that $K(p)\geq 1$.

We also used the (more compact) notation $\sum_{K_1+K_2=K}$ to indicate the sum $\sum_{\substack{K_1,K_2:\\K_1+K_2=K}}$, where $K_1+K_2=K$ means that $K_1(p) + K_2(p)=K(p)$ for all $p\in \mathcal{P}_{\mathbb{Z}^d}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.298238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_07fc868045842faf99bb21c33aca2734c2d7b75bf10252851f143e1c83eb55ef","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2606.23242, 'Correction to ``The stepping stone model in a random environment'' - limit theorems for th'] For any measurable bounded function $ f : \Omega^3 \to \R $, there exists a constant $ C > 0 $ such that, for $ \mu $-almost every environment $ \omega \in \Omega $, any bounded set $ A \subset \R^2 $ and for all $ t \geq 0 $,
