{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:55.644794+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08a970bb18ca63e4c8ddcd14c3fd7684fe50a1bdaef60d0d707dcc3260e01177","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 25 of arXiv:2605.24445, 'Matrix concentration inequalities for time-inhomogeneous Markov chains'] \label[definition]{def:sigma}
Let \(t\ge 1\). Since \(\mu_t=\mu_{t-1}P_t\), the Markov operator $P_t$
maps \(L^2(\mu_t)\) to \(L^2(\mu_{t-1})\). Define
the \emph{one-step (second) singular value}
\[
\sigma_t
:=\|P_t\|_{L^2_0(\mu_t)\to L^2_0(\mu_{t-1})}
=\sup\Big\{\|P_t f\|_{2,\mu_{t-1}}:\ \|f\|_{2,\mu_t}=1,\ \mu_t(f)=0\Big\}.
\]
Equivalently, for all \(f\in L^2(\mu_t)\) with \(\mu_t(f)=0\),
\begin{equation}
\|P_t f\|_{2,\mu_{t-1}}\le \sigma_t\,\|f\|_{2,\mu_t}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

