{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:29:08.521182+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08d9a040d6b4317e9f35dcad0ea22d7d2b8be79d7f0fbdc42a87bc2154f4fed5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:SLE-duality of arXiv:2411.19810, 'Radial conformal welding in Liouville quantum gravity'] Let $\kappa'\in(4,8)$, $\kappa=16/\kappa'$,$\rho_1,\rho_2>\frac{\kappa'}{2}-4$, and $\rho_1+\rho_2>\frac{\kappa'}{2}-4$. Let $\alpha = \frac{\kappa'-6-\rho_1'-\rho_2'}{2\sqrt{\kappa'}}$ and $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$. Let $h$ be a GFF on $\mathbb{D}$ with the boundary conditions described in Theorem~(thm:radial-ig of the paper), such that the counterflowline $\eta'$ of $h+\alpha\arg(\cdot)+\beta\log|\cdot|$ has the law radial $\mathrm{SLE}_{\kappa'}^\beta(\rho_1;\rho_2)$ from $-i$ to 0 with force points $(-i)^-;(-i)^+$. Then the left and right boundaries $\eta^L$ and $\eta^R$ of $\eta'$ are the flow lines of  $h+\alpha\arg(\cdot)+\beta\log|\cdot|$ from 0 with angles $\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $-\frac{\pi}{2}$, respectively. In particular, conditioned on $\eta^L$, the curve $\eta^R$ is the union of independent curves in connected components of $\mathbb{D}\backslash\eta^L$, as follows. 
        \begin{itemize}
            \item If $\eta^L$ touches itself, let $\sigma^L$ be the last time when $\eta^L$ touches itself before hitting $\partial\mathbb{D}$, and $D_{\eta^L}$ be the connected component of $\mathbb{D}\backslash\eta^L$ whose boundary contains $\eta^L(\sigma^L)$ and a segment of $\partial\mathbb{D}$. Otherwise let $\sigma^L=0$ and $D_{\eta^L}$ be the connected component of $\mathbb{D}\backslash\eta^L$ with 0 on its boundary. Let $a_{\eta^L}$ and $b_{\eta^L}$ be the first and last points on $\partial D_{\eta^L}\cap\partial \mathbb{D}$ when one views $\partial \mathbb{D}$ as a counterclockwise curve from $(-i)^+$ to $(-i)^-$. Then in $D_{\eta^L}$, $\eta^R$ is an $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(\frac{\kappa}{4}(\rho_1+\rho_2)+\kappa-4,-\frac{\kappa}{4}\rho_1;-\frac{\kappa}{2})$ with force points $(\eta^L(\sigma^L)^-,b_{\eta^L};\eta^L(\sigma^L)^+)$ from $\eta^L(\sigma^L)$ to $a_{\eta^L}$.
            \item In each remaining component whose boundary contains a segment of the counterclockwise boundary arc of $\partial\mathbb{D}$ from $-i$ to $a_{\eta^L}$, $\eta^R$ is a chordal $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(\frac{\kappa}{4}\rho_2+\kappa-4;-\frac{\kappa}{2})$ process from the first to the last boundary point hit by $\eta^L$.
            \item In each remaining component whose boundary does not contains a segment of $\partial\mathbb{D}$, $\eta^R$ is a chordal $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(\frac{\kappa}{4}(\rho_1+\rho_2)+\kappa-4;-\frac{\kappa}{2})$  process from the first to the last boundary point hit by $\eta^L$.
        \end{itemize}
       Similarly, given $\eta^R$, defining $\sigma^R$, $D_{\eta^R}$, $a_{\eta^R}$, $b_{\eta^R}$ as above with $\eta^L$ replaced by $\eta^R$ and counterclockwise replaced by clockwise, the curve $\eta^L$ is $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(-\frac{\kappa}{2};\frac{\kappa}{4}(\rho_1+\rho_2)+\kappa-4,-\frac{\kappa}{4}\rho_1)$ in $D_{\eta^L}$, chordal $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(-\frac{\kappa}{2};\frac{\kappa}{4}\rho_1+\kappa-4)$ in each other connected component of $\mathbb{D}\backslash\eta^R$  whose boundary contains a segment of the  clockwise boundary arc of $\partial\mathbb{D}$ from $(-i)^-$ to $a_{\eta^R}$, and chordal $\mathrm{SLE}_\kappa(-\frac{\kappa}{2};\frac{\kappa}{4}(\rho_1+\rho_2)+\kappa-4)$  process in each other connected component of $\mathbb{D}\backslash\eta^R$  whose boundary does not contain any segment of $\partial\mathbb{D}$. Finally, given $\eta^L$ and $\eta^R$, in each connected component of $\mathbb{D}\backslash(\eta^L\cup\eta^R)$ between $\eta^L$ and $\eta^R$, the law of $\eta'$ is chordal $\mathrm{SLE}_{\kappa'}(\frac{\kappa'}{2}-4;\frac{\kappa'}{2}-4)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.950675+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08db88d786955a2f0231a568d3b71a2934229be7a9be5cc97bc819cb49a43bef","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition P:crt-Feller of arXiv:2412.03521, 'On ergodic properties of stochastic PDEs'] A semigroup $P_t$ on a Hilbert space $H$ is strong Feller if, for all
  $\varphi: H \to \mathbb{R}$ with $\|\varphi\|_\infty$ and
  $\|\nabla\varphi\|_\infty$ finite and for all $t>0$ one has
  \begin{align}
    \left|\nabla P_t\varphi(x)\right| \le C(\|x\|) \, \|\varphi\|_\infty,
  \end{align}
  where $C:\mathbb{R}_+\to\mathbb{R}$ is a fixed nondecreasing function. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:47:40.728703+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_08dbdec2a080f03252604063afb3495f2dcd6cf120cb5c69757bdef749cd97f3","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary coro:m_easy of arXiv:2604.20008, 'Mixing times of Langevin dynamics for \\ spiked matrix models'] Let $\mathsf{m}_{\textsc{e}}=\sqrt{1-\frac{1}{\beta(\lambda_1-\lambda_2)}}$ and let $h(x) = 2H(x)/N$. Then  for any $\varepsilon < \mathsf{m}_{\textsc{e}}$, 
