{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:01:25.575108+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0a61690300f344d19b29f837fa6f0f2ec6ed97aaa84f5806f37b639f4b6098c8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Thm-singular-limit-PF of arXiv:2605.15742, 'Turbulent stretching of FENE dumbbell polymer model via special stochastic scaling and sin'] Assume that $f_{\tau, 0} \to f_0$ in $H_0$ as $\tau \downarrow 0.$ Then the following  hold.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $ f_\tau$ converges to 
        $ \rho_0\otimes \mathbf{M}_0$ in $L^2(0,T;H_0)$, 
     where  $\rho_0(x)=\int_D f_0(x,r) dr$ and $f_\tau$ is the unique solution  to (equation limit-PDE-final-NOCUT-V1***** of the paper). Moreover, the following estimate (convergence rate) holds
              \begin{align}
 \int_0^T  \Vert f_{\tau}(t)-\rho_0\otimes \mathbf{M}_0\Vert_{H_0}^2 dt \leq C_P  \xi\tau (\Vert f_{\tau, 0}\Vert_{H_0}^2+\Vert \rho_0 \Vert^2_{L^2(\mathbb{T}^2)})+2T\Vert f_{\tau,0}-f_0\Vert_{H_0}^2.
          \end{align}
          \item Assume that $f_{\tau,0}$ converges to $ \rho_{0}\otimes\mathbf{M}_0$ in $H_0.$ Then,  $ f_\tau$ converges to 
        $ \rho_0\otimes \mathbf{M}_0$ in $C([0,T];H_0)$, 
     where  $\rho_0(x)=\int_D f_0(x,r) dr$. Moreover, the following estimate (convergence rate) holds
     \begin{align}
         \displaystyle\sup_{t\in [0,T]}\Vert f_{\tau}(t)-\rho_0\otimes \mathbf{M}_0\Vert_{H_0}^2 \leq   \Vert f_{\tau,0}- \rho_{0}\otimes\mathbf{M}_0\Vert_{H_0}^2.
     \end{align}
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.604770+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0a63148e57c17003212d79aeafce4214a3cd97025c4e90f913be16ab07c63bfb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem Propagation of chaos 1 of arXiv:2606.13264, 'Interference Queueing Networks: A Replica Mean-Field Approach in the Symmetric Setting'] Let $T > 0$ and $i \in [1,N]$. Assume that, for all $K$, for all $l \leq K$, and for all $j \in [1,N]$,
