{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.494012+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0b3d55c9d4ed337667bdf0cf1f86392b3c19d447282e635b999f48808c35582d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 (Theorem A.2 in \cite{BW}) of arXiv:2512.02538, 'On the spectral geometry of Liouville quantum gravity'] Let $\nu$ be a nonnegative random measure on $\mathbb{R}_+$, $\nu(t) := \int_0^t \nu(ds)$, and suppose the Laplace transform
\begin{align*}
    \hat{\nu}(\lambda) := \int_0^\infty e^{-\lambda s} \nu(ds)
\end{align*}

\noindent exists almost surely for any $\lambda > 0$. If
\begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{0em}
\item $\rho \in [0, \infty)$ is fixed;
\item $L: (0, \infty) \to (0, \infty)$ is a deterministic slowly varying function at $0$; and 
\item $C_\nu$ is some non-negative (finite) random variable,
\end{itemize}

\noindent then we have:
\begin{align}
    \frac{\lambda^\rho}{L(\lambda^{-1})} \hat{\nu}(\lambda) \xrightarrow[\lambda \to \infty]{p} C_\nu
    \qquad \Rightarrow \qquad 
    \frac{t^{-\rho}}{L(t)} \nu(t) \xrightarrow[t \to 0^+]{p} \frac{C_\nu}{\Gamma(1+\rho)}.
\end{align}

\noindent The same implication also holds when one considers the asymptotics as $\lambda \to 0^+$ and $t \to \infty$ in \eqref{eq:tauberian} (but with $L$ being slowly varying at infinity) instead. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:43:55.658347+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0b438f78a1a70c52bc35a91fee821463febee99d5b56dbd075115291bdaf7a58","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm minimax_sharp of arXiv:2604.07183, 'Stopping on the last success with unknown odds: asymptotic minimax optimality of the plug-'] Fix~$p_0\in(0,\frac12)$. Then, with~$C_\star$ as in~((eq:Cstar of the paper)),
\begin{equation}
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\ \sqrt n\,
\inf_\pi\sup_{p\in[p_0,1)}\bigl(V_n(p)-W_n^\pi(p)\bigr)
=
C_\star
,
\end{equation}
where the infimum is over all (possibly randomized) \mbox{$p$-blind} rules. Consequently, the plug-in rule is asymptotically minimax optimal, with the exact constant. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:36:44.551346+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0b46a8caa9590e73216d45c53f104380b8d174617253ab07c75e1cf6ed801a99","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2604.01978, 'Homogenized Transformers'] Under Assumption \ref{ass:high_order_short}, 
