    then $x_t$ is a strong solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ up to time $\tau$. Conversely, if $x_t$ is a strong solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta,r}$ up to time $\tau$ and (equation eq:mild-vs-strong of the paper) holds, then $x_t$ is a mild solution in $\mathcal{B}_{\theta+1,r}$ up to time $\tau$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.803593+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0c34a6a8001b26e8571193f2795b9a93e72972b081a395a5ff18e0bc659b5b32","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:error-identity of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] Let $v_k = g_k / \|g_k\|_\pi$ be the normalized iterate, and let $s_2 = \operatorname{sign}(c_2)$ where $c_2 = \langle g_0, \phi_2 \rangle_\pi \neq 0$. Then for all $k \ge 0$,
