    In the left side of the second and third bounds, one could interpret the expression using any norm on $d\times d$ matrices.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:36:23.139272+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_0ddfebd741ae0d69746d3c9ce282c7c0577be0525427c84eb1fe5f5d61e53d2b","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition reducible_interval of arXiv:2507.01236, 'Disintegration theorem for multifunctions, \\ with applications to empirical Wasserstein d'] Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{A}, \mu)$ be a probability space with a given cover by measurable sets $B_1, \ldots, B_n$, and let $I \in 2^{[n]}$.  We will say that~$I$ is \emph{reducible} if there exists a nonempty proper subset~$J$ of~$I$ such that
\begin{equation}

\mu\!\left( \left( \bigcup_{i \in J} B_i \right) \cap \left( \bigcup_{i \in I \setminus J} B_i \right) \right) = 0.
\end{equation}
We will say that~$I$ is \emph{irreducible} if it is not reducible.","status":"unverified"}

