{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:49.206708+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1094f4e970695f9ed071a566daca577a02c73f052aba2aa2fabcefb8aad75c8c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2604.27242, 'Statistical Inference for Homogenization Limits Driven by Wiener or Hermite Processes'] Let $X^\varepsilon$ be the solution of \eqref{eq: multiscale system} with \(0<H<1\) and \(G\in L^2(\mathbb{R}, \gamma)\) with Hermite rank \(m\) such that \(H^*(m)\neq 1/2\). Let $T=N \delta$ for any time horizon $T$. If $\delta(\varepsilon)>0$ is such that \(\delta(\varepsilon)\xrightarrow{\varepsilon\rightarrow 0} 0\) and \(\varepsilon/\delta(\varepsilon)\rightarrow 0\) then
\[\mathbb{E}[C_{\varepsilon, \delta}]\rightarrow C^2,\]
where
\[C^2 = \mathbb{E}[X_1^2] = \begin{cases}
\displaystyle\sum_{q=m}^{\infty}c_q^2 q!\int_{\mathbb{R}}\rho(s)^q\,ds, & H^*(m)<1/2,\\[1em]
\displaystyle\frac{c_m^2 m!\,(\sigma^2H(2H-1))^m}{H^*(m)(2H^*(m)-1)}, & H^*(m)>1/2.
\end{cases}\]
Quantitatively,
\begin{equation}

\mathbb{E}[C_{\varepsilon,\delta}] = C^2 +
\begin{cases}
K\,(\varepsilon/\delta)^{1-(2H^*(m)\vee 0)}, & H^*(m)<1/2,\\[0.6em]
K_1(\varepsilon/\delta)^{2H^*(m)-1}+K_2(\varepsilon/\delta)^{2H^*(m)}, & H^*(m)>1/2,
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
for finite constants \(K, K_1, K_2\) depending only on \(H, m, G\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.610224+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1095fc2dc271f4d2d56f1c0d7ac1bd8a0589a097432decc5fa22aaebcc911eae","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm localfbm of arXiv:2606.21049, '\textsc{Locality of rough path lifts}'] Let $H \in (0,1/2]$ and $0 < \eta <H$ with $1/\eta < \lfloor 1/H \rfloor + 1$. Let $X$ be a $d$-dimensional $H$-fBm.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
	\item If $d\geq 2$ and $H \leq 1/4$ there exists no local, square-integrable $\eta$-RPL of $X$.
	\item 
If $H > 1/4$, denoting $\overline{\boldsymbol{X}}$ the canonical lift [CQ02], any local, square-integrable $\eta$-RPL $\boldsymbol{X}$ must satisfy
\begin{equation}
	\boldsymbol{X}^{(2)}_{s,t} = \overline{\boldsymbol{X}}{}^{(2)}_{s,t} + \varphi^{(2)}_{s,t}
\end{equation}
and if $H \in (1/4, 1/3]$ additionally
\begin{equation}
	\boldsymbol{X}^{\alpha\beta\gamma}_{s,t} = \overline{\boldsymbol{X}}{}^{\alpha\beta\gamma}_{s,t} + \DOTSI\upintop\ilimits@_s^t \varphi^{\alpha\beta}_{s,u} \,\mathrm{d} X^\gamma_u + \DOTSI\upintop\ilimits@_s^t \varphi^{\beta\gamma}_{u,t} \,\mathrm{d} X^\alpha_u + \varphi^{\alpha\beta\gamma}_{s,t}
	\;,
\end{equation}
where the integrals are defined as Wiener integrals (e.g.\ $\DOTSI\upintop\ilimits@_s^t \varphi^{\alpha\beta}_{s,u} \,\mathrm{d} X^\gamma_u = \sko(\varphi^{\alpha\beta}_{s,\cdot}\mathbbm 1_{[s,t]}^\gamma)$ and
\[
(0, \varphi^{(2)}, \varphi^{(3)})
\]
is a deterministic $\eta$-H\"older rough path above the zero path. Conversely, given any deterministic $\eta$-rough path $(0, \varphi^{(2)}, \varphi^{(3)})$ above the zero path, the formulas (equation eq:bX_level2 of the paper)-(equation eq:bX_level3 of the paper) define a local, square-integrable, geometric $\eta$-RPL of $X$. (Throughout this statement, we intend $\boldsymbol{X}$ and $\varphi$ to be only considered up to level-$2$ if $\eta \in (\frac 13, \frac 12]$.)
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.773766+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_109d4e97a818393bec4933949f62e0d54bbe5cc29594ae8da3f442df8ed50d16","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:Newton-inequalities of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] Let $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$ be a non-negative and non-zero sequence with finite length $m\geq1$, and let $(e_k)_{0\leq k\leq m}$ be the sequence of the elementary symmetric polynomials in the $(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m)$, or equivalently let $e_k=e_k(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m,0,0,\dots)$ for $0\leq k\leq m$.
	Then the sequence $(e_k)_{0\leq k\leq m}$ satisfies the \emph{ultra-log-concavity} inequality, that is
	\begin{align}
	\frac{e_k^2}{\binom{m}{k}^2}\geq 
		\frac{e_{k-1}}{\binom{m}{k-1}}\cdot
		\frac{e_{k+1}}{\binom{m}{k+1}},
		\qquad k\in\{1,\dots,m-1\}.
	\end{align}
	In particular, ${\mathbf{e}}(\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_m,0,0,\dots)$ is log-concave.
		\footnote{
		This is deduced from the inequality $\binom{m}{k}^2\geq\binom{m}{k-1}\binom{m}{k+1}$ for $0<k<m$, which holds by log-concavity of the binomial coefficients.
		Note that in this paper we require log-concave sequences to have no internal zeros, but we already justified that this is the case for sequences having the form ${\mathbf{e}}({\boldsymbol{\uptheta}})$ with ${\boldsymbol{\uptheta}}$ a summable weight sequence.
		} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.858011+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a5d81861634706283d77e8de44525859b7891f4338d6100b8396a736cfc6ee","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem lem:gen_ibp of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] For all $l \in H_0^1~\cap~H^2$ and $F \in C_b^1(L)$ the following two equations hold
    \begin{align}
        \langle \!\langle F(\beta), \bar{l}_a\delta_0(\beta_a)\rangle \!\rangle &= \int_{L} F \langle l,\ufd{\sigma^{(a)}}\rangle \,d\var{\sigma^{(a)}},\\
        \Big\langle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\langle F(\beta), \int_{[0,1]} \bar{l}_x\delta_0(\beta_x) dx \Big\rangle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\rangle &= \int_{L} F \langle l,n_{\nu}\rangle \,d\| \nu \|.
    \end{align}
	Here, the LHSs are interpreted as discussed in Theorem (thm:ibpf of the paper). Moreover, for all elements $l\in H_0^1~\cap~H^2$ and $G \in (C_b^1)_{H_0^1\cap H^2}$, the two equations
    \begin{align}
        \int_{L} D^*G \rho^a \, d\mu^{\beta} &= \int_{L} \langle G,\ufd{\sigma^{(a)}}\rangle \,d\var{\sigma^{(a)}},\\
        \int_{L} D^*G \rho \, d\mu^{\beta} &= \int_{L} \langle G,n_{\nu}\rangle \,d\| \nu \|
    \end{align}
    hold. Using these results, [RZZ12], Theorem 3.1(iii), directly implies that $\rho^a,\, \rho \in \mathrm{BV}(L, H_0^1)$, $V(\rho^a)=\var{\sigma^{(a)}}(L)$ and $V(\rho)=\| \nu \|(L)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.888936+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a645f18be63c1d85445a77d68812028397f35ff7959cb20732693f55cbe73a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definizione 13 of arXiv:2506.11937, 'Random rotational invariance of integration by parts formulas within a Bismut-type approac'] Given two (trivial) principal bundles $ M \times G$ and $  M' \times  G $, an isomorphism between principal bundles is a diffeomorphism $ F:  M \times G \rightarrow M' \times G$ that preserves the principal bundle structure of $ M \times G$ and $ M' \times G$. In other words, there exists a diffeomorphism $\Phi: M \rightarrow M'$ such that 
\begin{equation} 
 \pi_{M'} \circ F = \Phi \circ \pi_{M}, \ \ \ \ \ \  \ \ \ \ \ \  F \circ R_{M,g} = R_{M',g} \circ F \ \ \ \forall g\in G.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.803358+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a69b91bc565f1ae8459e40bfa6198e8008b789f03a8031405ad29a92f31178","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:variational of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] Fix a degree $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and a target contraction factor $\rho \in (0, \lambda_2)$. Among all polynomials $Q$ of degree at most $m$ satisfying
\begin{equation}
Q(1) = 1, \qquad |Q(\lambda_2)| \le \rho^m,
\end{equation}
the polynomial that \emph{minimizes} the total integrated dissipation
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{D}(Q) := E_0 - E_1^Q = \sum_{i=2}^n |c_i|^2 \bigl(1 - |Q(\lambda_i)|^2\bigr)
\end{equation}
is (up to sign) the rescaled Chebyshev polynomial
\begin{equation}
Q_m^\ast(\lambda) := \frac{T_m(\lambda / \lambda_2)}{T_m(1 / \lambda_2)},
\end{equation}
where $T_m$ is the Chebyshev polynomial of the first kind, defined by $T_m(\cos \theta) = \cos(m\theta)$.

Moreover, this optimal polynomial simultaneously:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
    \item Maximizes the cumulative spectral entropy production $\Sigma(m) := S_{\mathrm{spec}}(1) - S_{\mathrm{spec}}(0)$ after one accelerated step;
    \item Achieves the smallest possible rigidity time $T_{\mathrm{rigid}}(\delta)$ among all degree-$m$ polynomial acceleration schemes.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:58:09.040120+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10a823ade8a28c59db2d133402cde3b91f98a0bb6a93b69242077133547a40d9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2606.26619, 'Ergodicity of stochastic reaction-diffusion equations on unbounded domains driven by space'] Assume that there exists a constant $\beta\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $\sigma(\beta) = 0$. 
