{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.317890+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10aa66fa8189a8d58ac9e8103ec53cf72e12d7973c4d7bf28229a91e68b54301","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mixture-of-vrjps of arXiv:2411.06195, 'Restrictions of some reinforced processes to subgraphs'] \mbox{}\\
  Assume that the graph $G$ is finite without self-loops and partition its vertex set $\Lambda=I\cup J$,
  $I\cap J=\emptyset$, with $|J|\ge 2$. 
  Consider the vrjp $(X,T)$ in exchangeable time scale on $\Lambda$
  starting at $\rho\in J$ with weights $W$. 
  The restrictions $(X^J,T^J)$ and $(X^{J\neq},T^{J\neq})$ to $J$ without or with
  self-loops removed are
  mixtures of vrjps in exchangeable time scale on $J$ with random weights
    \begin{align}

    W^J(\beta_I)=&(W^J_{ij}(\beta_I))_{i,j\in J}:=W_{JJ}+W_{JI}([H_\beta]_{II})^{-1}W_{IJ}
                    \text{ and }\\

    W^{J\neq}(\beta_I):=&\left(W^J_{ij}(\beta_I)1_{\{ i\neq j\}}\right)_{i,j\in J},
  \end{align}
  respectively. They depend on a random
  vector $\beta_I\in{\mathbb R}^I$, where $\beta_{I\cup\{\rho\}}\in{\mathbb R}^{I\cup\{\rho\}}$ is distributed
  according to $\nu_{I\cup\{\rho\}}^{\widehat W}$ with $\widehat W\in{\mathbb R}^{(I\cup\{\rho\})\times(I\cup\{\rho\})}$
  obtained by restricting the parameters $W$ to $I$ and wiring all points
  in $J$ at $\rho$:
  \begin{align}

    \widehat W_{ij}=\widehat W_{ji}=
    \begin{cases}
      W_{ij} &\text{ for }i,j\in I,\\
      \sum_{k\in J}W_{ik} &\text{ for }i\in I, j=\rho,\\
      0 &\text{ for }i=j=\rho.
    \end{cases}
  \end{align}
  In the case of $(X^{J\neq},T^{J\neq})$ this means the
  following for any event $A\subseteq J^{{\mathbb N}_0}\times{\mathbb R}_+^{{\mathbb N}_0}$. 
  \begin{align}
    P_\rho^{W,\Lambda}((X^{J\neq},T^{J\neq})\in A)
    =&\int_{{\mathbb R}^{I\cup\{\rho\}}}
       P_\rho^{W^{J\neq}(\beta_I),J}((X,T)\in A)\,\nu_{I\cup\{\rho\}}^{\widehat W}(d\beta_{I\cup\{\rho\}})\nonumber\\
    =&\int_{{\mathbb R}^\Lambda}
       P_\rho^{W^{J\neq}(\beta_I),J}((X,T)\in A)\,\nu_\Lambda^W(d\beta_\Lambda).

  \end{align}
  The analogous statement holds for $(X^J,T^J)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:04:40.950832+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_10ab4452bf8979307e9ba975c20f1d4866be45f37e22980830c04ef574b4bab5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:asy-Feller of arXiv:2412.03521, 'On ergodic properties of stochastic PDEs'] A Markov transition semigroup $P_t$ on a Polish space $\mathcal{X}$ is called
  asymptotically strong Feller at $x$ if there exists a totally separating
  system of pseudo-metrics $\left\{d_n; n\ge1\right\}$ for $\mathcal{X}$ and a
  sequence $\left\{t_n; n\ge 1\right\}$ with $t_n>0$ such that
  \begin{align*}
    \liminf_{U\in \mathcal{U}_x}\: \limsup_{n\to\infty}\: \sup_{y\in U}\Norm{P_{t_n}(x,\cdot)-P_{t_n}(y,\cdot)}_{d_n}=0,
  \end{align*}
  where $\mathcal{U}_x$ is the collection of all open sets containing $x$. It is
  called asymptotically strong Feller if this property holds at every $x \in
  \mathcal{X}$.","status":"unverified"}

