{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.468714+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_110d1a8e051dc0d8a172ac9a4206315a019b28dd2c55a92a38bcb93339c8de45","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:uppermatrix of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let the QMS $\exp(t \mathcal{L}_\gamma)$ be a lift of $\exp(t \mathcal{L}_O)$ in the sense of (def:2ndlift of the paper). The exponential convergence holds: for $X \in \mathcal{M}$ with $E_{\mathcal{F}}(X) = 0$, 
        \begin{align*}
            \norm{e^{t \mathcal{L}_\gamma} (X)}_{2,\sigma} \le C e^{-\nu t} \lVertX\rVert_{2,\sigma}\,,
        \end{align*}
    with $ C = \mathcal{O}(1)$ and $\nu = \frac{\gamma}{\gamma^2 C_0 + C_1}$,
    where 
    \begin{align*}
        C_0 = \Theta\left(\lambda_O^{-1} \right)\,, \quad C_1 = \Theta\left(
         \left(\lambda_S^{-1} + 1 + \lambda_S^{-1/2}(K_1 + K_2 \lambda_O^{-1/2}) \right)^2
           \right)\,.
    \end{align*}
   The spectral gaps $\lambda_O$ and $\lambda_S$ are given in (equation eq:spgaplado of the paper) and (equation eq:spgaplads of the paper), respectively, and the constants $K_1$ and $K_2$ are such that 
   \begin{align}  
    \norm{(\mathrm{id} - E_S)\mathcal{L}_A^\dag (-\mathcal{L}_S)^{-1} \mathcal{L}_A Y}_{2,\sigma} 
  \le & \left( K_1 \lVert\mathcal{L}_O (Y)\rVert_{2,\sigma} + K_2 \sqrt{\mathcal{E}_{\mathcal{L}_O}(Y)} \right)\,.
\end{align}
If $\gamma = \gamma_{\max} := \sqrt{C_1(T)/C_0(T)}$, we have the maximal convergence rate:
\begin{align} 
    \max_{\gamma > 0} \, \nu(\mathcal{L}_\gamma) \ge \nu(\mathcal{L}_{\gamma_{\max}}) = \Omega\left(\frac{\lambda_O^{1/2}}{ \lambda_S^{-1} + 1 + \lambda_S^{-1/2}(K_1 + K_2 \lambda_O^{-1/2})}\right)\,.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.668188+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_110fc15af739875aaf1e8d42b59307cf38986334dadc7bf4f7380ed0863b737c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 (Negative drift of $c_n$) of arXiv:2607.20838, 'Long-memory Markov chains with power-law intensities'] There exists $x_* > 0$ such that the following statements hold.
