{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.299731+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_115696e3b37a590a9819aa8836730b0fd8feb0c2d5b483e49a052dfb94b6bcff","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 12 (Linear Gaussian smoothed likelihood and force) of arXiv:2608.21709, 'Posterior Information Dynamics of Diffusion Models for Linear Inverse Problems'] Let \(X_0\sim\calN(0,\Sigma)\) and
\(R=AX_0+\varepsilon\), where \(\varepsilon\sim\calN(0,\Gamma)\),
\(\Gamma\succ0\), and \(\varepsilon\) is independent of \(X_0\) and of the
forward noising variable used to form \(X_t\).  Define
\begin{equation}

B_t=a_t^2\Sigma+\Delta_t\Id_d,
\qquad
Q=A\Sigma A^\top+\Gamma.
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}

R\mid X_t=x\sim \calN(M_tx,\Sigma_{R\mid t}),
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}

M_t=a_tA\Sigma B_t^{-1},
\qquad
\Sigma_{R\mid t}=Q-a_t^2A\Sigma B_t^{-1}\Sigma A^\top.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the exact measurement force is
\begin{equation}

g_t(x,r)=M_t^\top\Sigma_{R\mid t}^{-1}(r-M_tx).
\end{equation}
Under the joint law of \((X_t,R)\),
\begin{equation}

\frac1d\E\|g_t(X_t,R)\|^2
=
\frac1d\operatorname{Tr}
\left(M_t^\top\Sigma_{R\mid t}^{-1}M_t\right).
\end{equation}
Moreover, under the VP schedule \eqref{eq:forward-marginal} (so that
\(\Delta_t=1-a_t^2\)), if along a dimension sequence
\(\lambda_{\min}(\Gamma)\ge\gamma_0>0\) and
\(d^{-1}\|A\Sigma\|_F^2\le C_0\), then as \(a_t\to0\),
\begin{equation}

\frac1d\E\|g_t(X_t,R)\|^2=O(a_t^2),
\end{equation}
which is the Gaussian specialization of
Corollary~\ref{cor:general-high-noise-energy}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.937423+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_11619d6b5ee76af6ee39c7e576bbd395bc5a42fcb767fb2f290b4bfa767b019d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 17 of arXiv:2608.02927, 'Thinning Operation via the Poisson-F\"ollmer Process'] Let $X$ have density $f$ with respect to $\pi_{T}$ and $T = \E X > 0.$ Then 
    \begin{displaymath}
        \frac{P_{T-s}f(1)}{P_{T-s}f(0)} \le \frac{T}{T-s}
