{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:42:55.318536+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_12e01c399076cc0518d72d01c4907a09330e0b0409965d7185a13eb90b16b6b9","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 26 (Variational identity for the sub-Gamma class) of arXiv:2609.02398, '\textbf{Convex Order Comparisons for Sub-Gamma Random Variables}'] Fix $a > 0$. For every $p \in (0,1)$ the feasible spreads $\{\nu > 0 : Y_{p,\nu} \in \SG(1,a)\}$ form the interval $(0, \nu^{\SG}_{\max}(p,a)]$: the supremum in \eqref{eqn:sgnumax} is finite and attained; moreover $\nu^{\SG}_{\max}(p,a) = \nu^{\SG}_{\max}(1-p,a)$. For $c > 0$, every $X \in \SG(1,a)$ satisfies $X \lecx c\Lap$ if and only if
\begin{align}
    (1-p)\,\nu^{\SG}_{\max}(p,a) \le c\left(1 + \log\frac{1}{2p}\right)
    \qquad \text{for every } p \in (0, 1/2].

\end{align}
Consequently
\begin{align}
    c^{\SG}(1,a) = \sup_{p \in (0,1/2]} R^{\SG}(p,a),
    \qquad
    R^{\SG}(p,a) \coloneqq \frac{(1-p)\,\nu^{\SG}_{\max}(p,a)}{1 + \log\frac{1}{2p}},

\end{align}
and the infimum in \eqref{eqn:defcSG} is a minimum: $c^{\SG}(1,a)$ is itself admissible.","status":"unverified"}

