{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.459818+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_136b545442dc428859d815fe0c72f1db20d80335d48e9ecb7485b98321937933","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem fn-in-X of arXiv:2601.19145, 'Stochastic Persistence in Infinite Dimensions'] Let $f_0 \coloneqq (W')^{1/2}$ (see (as-W of the paper)) and $f_1,\dots,f_n$, $h_1,\dots,h_n$ be functions satisfying the following. For $i = 1,\dots,n$, some $T > 0$, and all $x \in \mathcal{M}$:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $h_i$ vanishes over $f_i$.
        \item $\mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^t f_i(X_t^x)^2dt\Big] < \infty$ for all $t \geq T$.
        \item
        \begin{equation}
            \begin{aligned}
                 \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^T f_i(X_t^x)dt\Big] &\leq h_i(x) + f_{i-1}(x) + \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^T f_{i-1}(X_t^x) + h_i(X_t^x)dt\Big] \\
                \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^T f_i(X_t^x)^2dt\Big] &\leq h_i(x) + f_{i-1}(x)^2 + \mathbb{E}\Big[\int_0^T f_{i-1}(X_t^x)^2 + h_i(X_t^x)^2dt\Big] \,.
            \end{aligned} 
        \end{equation}
    \end{itemize}
    Then $f_n \in \mathcal{X}$ and $f_n^2 \in \mathcal{Y}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.474777+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_136ea0f355d2a6bd66404787501dea7ab781d3de554559c54f87010db88225df","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:cap-function of arXiv:2607.27613, 'Regularized Bulk Universality versus Bounded-Disorder Nonuniversality\\for Annealed Comple'] \label[lemma]{lem:cap}
Let \(r:\mathbb S^{N-1}(1)\to\mathbb R\) be continuous with \(\left\lVertr\right\rVert_\infty\le M\), and let
\begin{equation}
 F(x)=a x_1^p+r(x),
 \qquad a>2M.
\end{equation}
Choose \(\delta\in(0,1)\) so that
\begin{equation}
 a\bigl[1-(1-\delta)^p\bigr]>2M.
\end{equation}
Then \(F\) attains a local maximum at an interior point of the cap
\[
 \mathcal K_\delta=\{x\in\mathbb S^{N-1}(1):x_1\ge1-\delta\}.
\]
If \(r\) is \(C^1\), this point is a critical point of \(F\) on the sphere.  Its value lies in \([a-M,a+M]\).","status":"unverified"}

