{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.861782+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14752756045762704a2c542ba798ea18b7e265439e6926c9bd6717c4e9c8dae2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 of arXiv:2605.21424, 'A dice game, a multinomial walk, and the inverted Dirichlet distribution'] Let \(m\ge 2\) be an integer and \(n_1,\ldots,n_m\in (0,\infty)\).
Let $(G_1,\dotsc,G_m)$ be a vector of independent random variables with $G_k\sim\operatorname{Gamma}(n_k,1)$, then
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{G_2}{G_1},\dotsc,\frac{G_m}{G_1}\right)\sim \operatorname{ID}(n_2,\dotsc,n_m;n_1)
\end{align*}
and 
\begin{align*}
\left(\frac{G_1}{\sum_{k=1}^m G_k},\dotsc,\frac{G_m}{\sum_{k=1}^m G_k}\right)\sim \operatorname{D}(n_1,\dotsc,n_m),
\end{align*}
where \(\operatorname{D}(n_1,\dotsc,n_m)\) denotes the Dirichlet distribution with parameter set \((n_1,\dotsc,n_m)\) (see, e.g., \cite[Example 6.1]{Fang_et_al_1990} for the definition).

In particular, if $(V_1,\dotsc,V_m)$ is Dirichlet distributed with parameter set $(n_1,\dotsc,n_m)$ then, defining 
\begin{align*}
X_k:=\frac{V_k}{1-V_2-\dotsb-V_m},\quad 2\leq k\leq m,
\end{align*}
we get $(X_2,\dotsc,X_m)\sim \operatorname{ID}(n_2,\dotsc,n_m;n_1)$. 

Conversely, if $(X_2,\dotsc,X_m)\sim \operatorname{ID}(n_2,\dotsc,n_m;n_1)$, then 
\begin{align*}
V:=\Big(\frac{1}{1+X_2+\dotsb+X_m},\frac{X_2}{1+X_2+\dotsb+X_m},\dotsc,\frac{X_m}{1+X_2+\dotsb+X_m}\Big)
\end{align*}
 is Dirichlet distributed with parameter set $(n_1,\dotsc,n_m)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.784411+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_147a3c2ccabefff7034faf4993a4d20a885a624a840b5c4d9064da3be3978d01","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemat 13 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Given a contraction 
