        \| \rho_0\|_{L^2}^2-\|\rho^\kappa_t\|_{L^2}^2 = 2\kappa ^\beta\int_0^t \big\| |\nabla|^\beta \rho^\kappa_s\big\|_{L^2}^2 \dd s = \int_{\T^d} Var\big[ \rho_0((X^\kappa_{0,t})^{-1}(x))\big] \dd x
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:48:25.142609+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14d0b25b9d4424f7bd7d50769e5df70379099048a1f46f3c1ff8760cd441c8ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:wobbling of arXiv:2607.04620, 'The Exact Ville Identity\\ \large From the Absorbing Case to the General Law\\ with an App'] Let $\{M_n,\mathcal{F}_n\}$ be a mean-zero martingale and fix a level $r\ge0$. Let $T_r$ be the first $n\ge1$ with $M_n>r$, and $\infty$ if no such $n$ exists. Suppose
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item (sufficient wobbling)
\[
  \lim_{\varepsilon\downarrow0}\limsup_{k\to\infty}
  \mathbb{P}\left(\sup_{n\ge k}|M_n-M_k|>\varepsilon\right)=1,
\]
\item (bounded expected overshoot) there is $B<\infty$ with $\mathbb{E}[M_n\mid T_r=n]\le r+B$ a.s. for every $n\ge1$.
\end{enumerate}
Then $\mathbb{P}(T_r<\infty)=1$ and $\mathbb{E}[M_{T_r}]\le r+B$; moreover $\limsup_{n\to\infty}M_n=+\infty$ almost surely. Condition (i) ensures the martingale wobbles arbitrarily far out; condition (ii) bounds the overshoot of $M_{T_r}$ beyond $r$. Dropping either condition can make $\mathbb{P}(T_r=\infty)>0$, as the counterexamples of [klass2026beyond] demonstrate. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.279175+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_14d1e116895a1bfd47abc7d97d6aef47a8958ea2281a9673f00dcef937013ef1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 8 (Theorem 1.1 in \cite{Hitczenko_studiamath}) of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Let $\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n}$ be independent symmetric random variables with log-convex tails, i.e., $\log \mathbb{P}\{\vert\xi_{i}\vert\ge t \}$ is a convex function for $t\ge 0$. Then for $r\ge 2$
