{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.986093+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15a7b1be9db8b5b2a5bb994b4316c7766475e07d3785e32d43d5939e67954342","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 18 (Transfer from a represented sequence to its Fock terminal) of arXiv:2608.12492, 'Weak Limits of Wiener Chaos: Primitive--Fock Classification and Hilbert--Stein Extraction'] Fix $Q,d<\infty$.  For every $n$, let $W_n$ be isonormal over $H_n$ and
let
\begin{equation}
 Z_{n,a}=\sum_{q=1}^{Q}I_q^{W_n}(z_{n,a,q}),
 \qquad
 \sup_n\sum_{a,q}q!\norm{z_{n,a,q}}^2<\infty.

\end{equation}
Put
$
 \zeta_{a,q}=[z_{n,a,q}]_{\mathcal U}\in\cH_q
$
and, on the Gaussian space over $\bigoplus_{s\le Q}\cK_s$, define
\begin{equation}
 Z_a^{\mathrm{FF}}
 =\sum_{q=1}^{Q}\mathbb U_q^{-1}\zeta_{a,q}.

\end{equation}
Then the represented packet itself, and not merely some spatial
realization of it, satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \Ulim \E\varphi(Z_n)=\E\varphi(Z^{\mathrm{FF}})

\end{equation}
for every bounded Lipschitz function $\varphi:\R^d\to\R$.

The transfer is compatible with the finite forest register.  More
precisely, let $G$ be such a network with $L$ open legs and put
$x_v=\zeta_{a_v,q_v}$ when its input at $v$ is $z_{n,a_v,q_v}$.  Then
\begin{equation}
 [C_{G,n}((z_{n,a_v,q_v})_{v\in V})]_{\mathcal U}
 =C_G^{\mathrm{FF}}
 \bigl((\mathbb U_{q_v}^{-1}\zeta_{a_v,q_v})_{v\in V}\bigr)
 \quad\text{in }\cT_L^{\mathcal U}.

\end{equation}
For $L=0$ this is the scalar complete-block pairing value; for $L>0$
the unpaired complete primitive blocks form the raw labeled output.  The
assertion is multilinear and therefore also holds for finite linear
combinations of networks.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.102678+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_15a9eaf3692a450cf54ec7f9abd33bee3daa9a2889e7d638fd1807486709dd07","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2608.27374, '\Large \bf Distribution-constrained optimal multiple stopping: the Root-type solution'] Let $X$ and $Y$ be independent standard Brownian motions.
