{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:48:43.520518+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_164852958164bd99aa854759ae78d5b29a28c0d3e2ac313c5bbe00857e06c77b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 4 of arXiv:2608.06646, 'Three-dimensional stochastic wave equation with non-Lipschitz coefficients'] Suppose the initial values $u_0$, $v_0$ and the spatial covariance function $f$ satisfy the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{T:MainTheorem}. Let $u$ be the solution to~\eqref{E:SWE}. Then, there exist constants $c,C>0$ such that for all $T>0$, $p\ge 2$, $x,y\in \R^3$, and $|x-y|\le 1$, $t\in [0,T]$, 
