	$F_f$ in \eqref{distribuzioneComposizioneBernstein}. The jumps are distributed as $Y = \sum_{h=1}^A X_h$, with $A$ being independent of $X_1,X_2,\dots\sim F$ and $M$ and $P\{A = k\} = a'_k,\ k\ge1,$ with $a'_k$ in ($iii$) of Lemma \ref{lemmaEquazioneOperatorialeBernstein}.



	\item[($ii$)] Let $a=0$. The probability law of $M\circ\mathcal{H}_f$ satistfies, for $x\in\mathbb{R}$,	\begin{equation}
		\frac{\dif}{\dif t}p(t, \dif x) = - f\Big(\lambda\big(I-C_F\big)\Big)p(t, \dif x), \ t\ge0,\ \ \ p(0,\dif x) = \delta_0(\dif x).
	\end{equation}
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.128214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1aa5ba436b9337f127afa4cf67a1c4b7ded3f014ff3f1dfe4810e7602755c9e1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:appendix_conv_weak of arXiv:2510.06891, 'Multivariate CLT for L\'evy processes: convergence rates without moment assumptions'] {RaoWeak}}]
Let be $(\bm{\xi}_n)_{n \geqslant 0}$ be a sequence of random vectors in $\mathbb{R}^d$ and $\bm{\xi}$ be an absolutely continuous random vector in $\mathbb{R}^d$. Then the following limits (as $n\to\infty$) are equivalent:
\begin{align}
\bm{\xi}_n \xrightarrow{d} \bm{\xi}, \qquad 
d_\mathscr{K}(\bm{\xi}_n, \bm{\xi} ) \to 0,\qquad
