{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.712216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1c8fec3cd40adf769c25da01112b63778087083070e6286fe1848260c766447c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition defn_Mm of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] We define $m(z)\equiv m_N(z)$ as the unique solution to 
 \begin{equation}
m(z)= \big\langle \left(\Lambda -z- m(z)\right)^{-1} \big\rangle ,
\end{equation}
 such that $\im m(z)>0$ whenever $z\in \mathbb{C}_+$.  
 Then, we define the matrix $M(z)\equiv M_N(z,\Lambda)$ as 
\begin{equation}
M(z):= \left(\Lambda -z- m(z)\right)^{-1}.
 \end{equation}
Since $\Lambda$ is Hermitian, we have that $m(\overline z)= \overline {m(z)}$ and $M(\overline z)=M(z)^*$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.758282+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1c90e2527507a1e0cbc0481358e917a5a8e88bd3768c26aa6d4a3c25cf74dc6f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lemma_localized_eigenvalue_local_law of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] In the setting of (theorem_localized_eigenvector_localization of the paper), the following estimate holds uniformly in $t\in \left[{0,1}\right]$ for some constant $C>0$ that does not depend on $\varepsilon$:

\begin{equation}
        \begin{aligned}
            \mathbb{E}\avg{\left({\im G_t}\right){\widehat{\Lambda}}_t\left({\im G_t}\right){\widehat{\Lambda}}_t}\prec N^{C\varepsilon}N^{-5/3}k^{2/3}\lVert A \rVert_{\text{\rm{HS}}}^2\le N^{-1-2\varepsilon_A+C\varepsilon}
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}

    where $G_t$ is defined by $G_t :=\left({H+t\Lambda-z_t}\right)^{-1}$ with $z_t$ defined in (equation eq:z1t of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

