{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.299856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1d998d557ee8f19639c8131418e71e7b8c50e93161ac0ea8bd89c1175ab9104c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 13 (Gaussian relative-energy decay) of arXiv:2608.21709, 'Posterior Information Dynamics of Diffusion Models for Linear Inverse Problems'] Under Proposition~\ref{thm:linear-gaussian-guidance}, the prior score is
\[
s_t^{\rm prior}(x)=-B_t^{-1}x.
\]
Then, under \(X_t\sim p_t\),
\begin{equation}

\frac1d\E\|s_t^{\rm prior}(X_t)\|^2
=\frac1d\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(B_t^{-1}\right)
=\frac1d\sum_{i=1}^d\frac{1}{a_t^2\lambda_i+\Delta_t},
\end{equation}
where \(\{\lambda_i\}\) are the eigenvalues of \(\Sigma\).  Under the schedule
\eqref{eq:forward-marginal}, \(\Delta_t\to1\) as \(a_t\to0\), so this converges
to \(1\) and the prior-score energy is \(\Theta(1)\); the same conclusion holds
for any VP schedule with \(\Delta_t\to\Delta_\infty>0\).  In fixed
dimension, the likelihood-force energy has the leading-order expansion
\begin{equation}

\frac1d\E\|g_t(X_t,R)\|^2
=\frac{a_t^2}{\Delta_t^2}\,\frac1d\operatorname{Tr}\!\left(\Sigma A^\top Q^{-1}A\Sigma\right)
+O(a_t^4),
\end{equation}
and therefore
\begin{equation}

\rho_t^{\rm global}
:=
\frac{\E\|g_t(X_t,R)\|^2}
{\E\|s_t^{\rm prior}(X_t)\|^2}
=O(a_t^2).
\end{equation}
In the isotropic case \(\Sigma=\sigma_x^2\Id_d\), \(A=\Id_d\),
\(\Gamma=\sigma_y^2\Id_d\),
\(\rho_t^{\rm global}\sim a_t^2\sigma_x^4/(\sigma_x^2+\sigma_y^2)\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.304387+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1da49c2d58345d65cf03fd34cb23f95fc389e1ac559d45caeb1b46b5ed70ee5e","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem DistribXrho of arXiv:2411.14766, 'Axis-Driven Random Walks on $\Z^2$ \\ (transient cases)'] Assume $0<\alpha<1$, there exists $ {\mathbf{c_{_0}}}>0$ such that for $x$ large if $\overline{y}>x$. There exists a function ${\bf c} : \mathbb{N}^2 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}$, such that for any  $\overline{y}-x \leq A x^{\alpha} $, 
