{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.370306+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e2566657370800c8c0b988e687923260be36c8e8c623e7a6b7da3a5e1243cd9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm1 of arXiv:2411.13430, 'Optimal subelliptic super-Poincar\'e and isoperimetric inequalities on stratified Lie grou'] Assume $N$ satisfies in the distributional sense 
    \begin{equation}
        \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^\alpha}{N^\alpha} \lesssim \left\lvert \nabla N \right\rvert \lesssim \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^\alpha}{N^\alpha}, \quad \Delta N \lesssim \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^{2\alpha}}{N^{2\alpha+1}}, \quad \nabla N \cdot \nabla \left\lvert x \right\rvert \lesssim \frac{\left\lvert x \right\rvert^{2\alpha+1}}{N^{2\alpha+1}} 
    \end{equation}
    for some $\alpha > 0$ and where $\left\lvert x \right\rvert$ is the euclidean norm of a generic point $\xi \in \mathbb{R}^n$ written in the form $\xi = (x, x') \in \mathbb{R}^{n_1} \times \mathbb{R}^{n_2}$ with $n_1 \geq 2$. Assume moreover $\left\lvert x \right\rvert$ satisfies again in the distributional sense 
    \begin{equation}
        \nabla \left\lvert x \right\rvert \lesssim 1, \quad \Delta \left\lvert x \right\rvert = \frac{n_1 - 1}{\left\lvert x \right\rvert}, \quad \nabla \cdot x = n_1,
    \end{equation}
    and 
    \begin{equation}
        \left\lvert x \right\rvert \lesssim N, \quad d \lesssim N \lesssim d.
    \end{equation}
    If there exists a local $1$-super-Poincar\'e inequality 
    \begin{equation}
        \int \left\lvert f \right\rvertd\xi \leq \delta \int \left\lvert \nabla f \right\rvertd\xi + \widetilde{\beta}_1(\delta)\left(\int \left\lvert f \right\rvert^{1/2}d\xi\right)^2, \quad \widetilde{\beta}_1(\delta) \lesssim 1 + \delta^{-Q}
    \end{equation}
    for some $Q > 0$ and $f \in W^{1, 2}(\mu)$, and a local $1$-Poincar\'e inequality 
    \begin{equation}
        \int_{B_R} \abs{f - \frac{1}{\xi(B_R)} \int_{B_R} fd\xi} d\xi \lesssim \int_{B_R} \left\lvert \nabla f \right\rvertd\xi
    \end{equation}
    for $f \in W^{1,2}(B_R)$ where $B_R$ is the $d$-ball of radius $R > 0$ centred at the origin, then for each $p \geq \alpha + 1$, the measure $\mu$ defined according to (equation measure of the paper) satisfies the isoperimetric inequality
    \[ I_\mu \gtrsim \mathcal{U}_r, \quad r = \frac{(\alpha + 1)p}{(\alpha + 1) + \alpha p} \]
    with respect to the metric $d$ and where $\mathcal{U}_r(t)$ is the isoperimetric function of the measure $d\nu_r = Z^{-1}e^{-\left\lvert x \right\rvert^r}dx$ with respect to the euclidean metric $\left\lvert \cdot \right\rvert$ on $\mathbb{R}^n$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.143007+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e264387e59d6d7f796b4d40fd6621e92ff863a7db6d514ac53cdb6086a33939","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 9 (\cite{QuanTrig}) of arXiv:2608.27630, 'Oriented planar maps and \\ annular mating of trees'] Fix $W_1,W_2, W_3 \ge \gamma^2/2$, and let $\beta_i= \gamma+\frac{2-W_i}{\gamma}$ if $W_i\neq \frac{\gamma^2}{2}$ and $\beta_i=Q^-$ if $W_i=\frac{\gamma^2}{2}$.
    Consider a distribution $\phi$ sampled from 
    \begin{equation*}
        \prod_{\beta_i\neq Q^-} (Q-\beta_i)^{-1} \LF_{\bH}^{(\beta_1,0), (\beta_2, 1),(\beta_3, \infty)}.
    \end{equation*}
    The infinite measure $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is defined to be the law of $(\bH, \phi, 0, 1, \infty)/\mathord\sim_\gamma$. A sample from $\QT(W_1,W_2,W_3)$ is called a quantum triangle with weights $W_1$, $W_2$ and $W_3$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:48:02.410022+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e27c094b62d56cffe0ab1861f99bcc7ab964ffab1a004fad78200f678763dd3","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lma:trans of arXiv:2608.26564, 'Real ideal points, Conway spheres, and left-orderable Dehn fillings'] Let $\lambda=\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3\ell_4$ be a four-longitude decomposition in positive parabolic normal form along $B$. Then for every sufficiently small $t \neq 0$, 
\begin{equation*}
	\mathrm{trans}(\widetilde\rho_t(\ell_1\ell_2)) = h_1 + h_2 \ \ \ \ \ \mathrm{trans}(\widetilde\rho_t(\ell_1\ell_2\ell_3)) = h_1 + h_2 + h_3
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
	\mathrm{trans}(\widetilde\rho_t(\lambda)) = h_1+h_2+h_3+h_4
\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.681729+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e2fd37c7970323de6ec85ea023f0b24324b8ff820494d1f0884d5829334a014","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition c:cltspindisag of arXiv:2605.28430, 'Limit theory for Lipschitz-localized statistics in random geometric models'] Let \(\mathcal{P}\) be a simple, stationary point process on \(\mathbb{R}^d\) with summable exponential mixing correlations as in Definition (def.A2 of the paper), and assume that the (reduced) Palm intensity function of the ground process \(\mathcal{P}\) is bounded at all orders, as specified in (equation bdedreduced of the paper). Let \(\Psi = \Psi(\cdot)\) be a stabilizing interaction graph on finite windows with respect to \(\mathcal{P}\) as in Definition~(d:stabilizing-graph of the paper), with finite averaged connective constant \(\bar\Delta<\infty\)  defined in Definition~(d:connective-constant-pp of the paper). If, for some $\epsilon>0$,  all sets \( B \in\mathcal{B}_b(r_0,n_0) \)
defined in~(equation e.particular-sets of the paper), and almost every realization \(\mathcal{P} \), the maximum influence \( q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \) as in (equation defq of the paper) of the Gibbs specification~(equation eqn:gibbs of the paper) on $\Psi(\mathcal{P}\cap B)$ with finite spin space $\mathbb{K}$ satisfies the inequality 
\begin{equation} 
q(\Psi(\mathcal{P} \cap B)) \le (\epsilon+\bar{\Delta})^{-1}
\end{equation}
 then the spin configuration \( \{V_x(\Psi(\mathcal{P}_n))\}_{x \in \mathcal{P}_n} \) satisfies the CLT as stated in Theorem~(spinmain of the paper), provided the variance condition there is also met. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:10:08.713277+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e36b81b3ec8482f0199de8c5051f6eb3358545cff3d3bdeb69d68f9ddddf1ab","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition proposition_rho_has_weak_deriv of arXiv:2411.17653, 'Exclusion processes with non-reversible boundary: hydrodynamics and large deviations'] All limit points $\mathbb{Q}$ of the sequence $(\mathbb{Q}_{\nu_N})_{N \geq 1}$ are such that, $\mathbb{Q}$-a.s. there exists  {a} $L^2([0,T],H^1[0,1])$ function, called the weak derivative of $\rho$ and that we denote by $\nabla \rho_s(u)$, such that,  {for every $G$ smooth function, it holds that
\begin{align*}
    \int_0^T \left[\int_0^1 \rho_s(x) \nabla G_s(x) dx - F^+(\rho_t(1)) G(1) - F^-(\rho_t(0)) G(0) \right] ds  = - \int_0^T \int_0^1 \nabla \rho_s(x) G_s(x) dx ds
\end{align*}} and
\begin{align*}
    \int_0^T \int_0^1 \frac{|\nabla \rho_s(u)|^2}{\rho_s(u)[1-\rho_s(u)]} du ds < \infty.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:36:49.912286+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e49a42952a98d492e0a66090b99d933175fe7f44ce8c3143be04ff540403708","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main of arXiv:2609.01557, '$L^\infty$ Variational Approximation of the Aubry Set'] Assume that \(H\in C^\infty(\mathbb R^n\times\mathbb T^n)\) is a periodic Tonelli Hamiltonian.  Let \(u_k\) be the normalized minimizers of (equation eq:Ik of the paper), and let \(u_\infty\) be a uniform subsequential limit. 
If
\begin{equation}
    \mathcal H^1(\mathcal A_M,\delta_M)=0,
\end{equation}
then $u_\infty$ is a critical subsolution that is strict outside $\mathcal A$ and
\[
    \{x\in\mathbb T^n\,:\,u_\infty(x)=u_-(x)\}=D_{u_\infty}=\mathcal A.
\]
Here $u_-(x)$ is given by ((eq:backward of the paper)) with $u=u_\infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:22:08.387682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e49dc082d312028ebd8aeae088d4602660cf2d4804fd661878d468e87bdf1ad","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lm:gaussianZ of arXiv:2607.28817, 'Limiting spectral distribution for the adjacency matrix of the Watts--Strogatz random grap'] Under the additional assumption that $p_n\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\; p\in[0,1)$, we have
    \[
        \mu_{G_n+\frac{{Z}_n^\circ}{\sqrt{p_nK_n}}}-\mu_{G_n+\sqrt{1-p}B_n}\;\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\;0\qquad\mbox{ weakly in probability},
    \]where $B_n$ is an $n\times n$ symmetric random matrix independent of $G_n$ and whose upper-triangular entries are independent and verify, for all $i\le j$,
    \[
        (B_n)_{ij}=\begin{cases}
            \mathcal{N}\left(0,\frac{1}{{K_n}}\right) & \mbox{if }\quad 0 < \min\big(|i-j|,\, n - |i-j|\big) \le \frac{K_n}{2}, \\
            0 & \mbox{otherwise,}
        \end{cases}
    \]
    with $\mathcal{N}\left(0,1\right)$ the standard normal distribution.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:12:13.460548+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e4a5e16d165c1bc05d0eba18c9a663e268207e69d63e562d65dd1111d1dba40","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2608.28307, 'Rigidity of closed $\lambda$-self-expanders'] \cite{okumura1974hypersurfaces}
Let $\lambda_1, \lambda_2, \cdots, \lambda_n, n\geq 3$, be real numbers such that $\sum_i\lambda_i=0$ and $\sum_i\lambda_i^2=\mu^2$. Then
$$\frac{-(n-2)\mu^3}{\sqrt{n(n-1)}}\leq \sum_i\lambda_i^3\leq \frac{(n-2)\mu^3}{\sqrt{n(n-1)}},$$
and equality holds if and only if at least $(n-1)$ of $\lambda_i$ are equal. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:26:26.760214+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1e4a8ce512b2921490a7fc1048887ab812fdb73c05469fe956a9f88aaeb98905","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:BLPP-one-point of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] For $k = 1$, the Fredholm determinant
