{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.196072+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef3266b86c60e364f2ec6701070cf1dd42f3b4044ebdfc54698fdfd6dc87cdf","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 25 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Length of the typical relaying zone.}

Let us consider $H$ a random variable distributed following $\mathcal{L}$. Let us further consider the landscape $\tilde{\landsc} := \landsc\cup \{(0, H)\}$. Then expectation of the length of the zone of the typical eventual relaying $\zone (0, H)$ is 
\[\e[\ell(\zone(0, H))] = \frac{1}{\lambda}\e[T],\]
where $T$ is the hitting time of the cemetery state $(-\infty, -\infty, -\infty)$ for the Markov chain $(\tilde{x}_n, \mathrm{t}_n, H_n)$ started at 
$(0, 0, +\infty)$:
\[\e[T] = \sum_{n\geq 1} \displaystyle\int_{\Half^{n+1}} g_n \delta_{(0, 0)}\displaystyle\bigotimes_{i=1}^{n} (\Leb \otimes \mathcal{L}).\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:52:05.706937+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef4e59af6eb71bbc314d92828f88c6a079216319fd1f0d15ffaaac9ed66398a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem l:1104.1 of arXiv:2602.21277, 'A Limit in Law for the Cover Time and Last Visited Vertex of Wired Planar Domains'] The following holds:
\begin{enumerate}
	\item 
Uniformly on ${\rm D}$,
\begin{equation}
	\psi_n \big(\lfloor {\rm e}^{n} \cdot \rfloor \big) 
	\underset{n \to \infty}\longrightarrow\
		\psi_{\rm D} \,,
\end{equation}
where $\psi_{\rm D}$ is as in~(equation e:1.9p of the paper). As $\psi_{\rm D}$ is a continuous function on $\overline{{\rm D}}$, the functions $\{\psi_n\}$ are uniformly bounded in the supremum norm and asymptotically equicontinuous, in the sense that 
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{\delta \downarrow 0} \limsup_{n\to \infty} \sup_{|x-y| < \delta {\rm e}^n} \big|\psi_n(x) - \psi_n(y) \big| = 0 \,.
\end{equation}
\item With $\overline{h}_{\rm D}$ as in~(equation e:1.7p of the paper),
\begin{equation}
	\overline{h}_n \underset{n \to \infty} \Longrightarrow \overline{h}_{\rm D} \,.
\end{equation}
\item 
With respect to the supremum norm on $\overline{{\rm D}}$, 
\begin{equation}

h^\psi_n(\lfloor {\rm e}^n \cdot \rfloor) \underset{n \to \infty}\Longrightarrow h^\psi_{\rm D}
\quad ; \qquad 
h^\psi_{\rm D} := \overline{h}_{\rm D} \psi_{\rm D} \,.
\end{equation}
The field $h_{\rm D}^\psi$ is Gaussian with mean zero and covariance given by
\begin{equation}
	\mathbb{E} h_{\rm D}^\psi(x)h_{\rm D}^\psi(y) = \frac{\int_{{\rm D} \times {\rm D}} G_{\rm D}(z,x) G_{\rm D}(z', y) {\rm d} z {\rm d} z'}{\int_{{\rm D} \times {\rm D}} G_{\rm D}(z,z') {\rm d} z {\rm d} z'} \,.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:57:16.052690+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef5911ac86d0db62122eed60c7c0e5913b902a14f87b53d68d5e2f727c4b50a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma sublemma of arXiv:2506.10448, 'Hitting probabilities and the Ekstr\"om--Persson conjecture'] For $\omega \in \Omega$ and $n\in \mathbb{N}$, define a measure $\mu_n^{\omega}$ by
\[
\mu_n^{\omega}\coloneqq \sum_{D\in \mathcal{D}_n}\nu(D)a_{n,D}\sum_{k=M_n}^{N_n} 
  \chi_{D\cap (C-V_k)}(\omega_k )\delta_{\omega_k}.
\]
Then $\mu_n^{\omega}\to \nu$ almost surely in the weak-$\ast$ topology.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.965252+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1ef5f6895a4713b66d9e83147e2b9a8198f1cbe1067fc72b4ff0ac31c0d3f2f2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem lem:full_supp_meas_full_supp_cop of arXiv:2411.07113, '\begin{LARGE}On differentiability and mass distributions of topologically typical multivar'] Let $C \in \mathcal{C}_{ar}^d$ and let $\gamma \in \mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{W}_d}$ be its corresponding Williamson measure. Then the following assertions hold
    \begin{itemize}
     \item[(i)] If $\gamma^{abs}$ has full support, then $\mu_C^{abs}$ has full support.
        \item[(ii)] If $\gamma^{dis}$ has full support, then $\mu_C^{dis}$ has full support.
         \item[(iii)] If $\gamma^{sing}$ has full support, then $\mu_C^{sing}$ has full support.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.188479+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1efa602f32e0d26b4b54165164ab1eacb359f910b01525d1fcbbfb53f5f91fb3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 151 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] Assume that $a,b$ are smooth and bounded. Take
$R\leq 1$, smooth $f$, and let $u$ be the classical solution of
\begin{equation} 

\cL u+f=0
\end{equation}
in $C_{R}$ with zero boundary condition
on $\partial' C_{R}$.   Then
\begin{equation} 

|u|\leq NR^{2-\beta }\|I_{C_{R}}f\|_{E_{q,p,\beta }},
\end{equation}
where $N$ depends only on $d,\delta,q ,p ,\beta ,\rho_{a} $. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.710356+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1efdc916a42c7702275e3fe3880eda33bf44a4ffd81c37d4970e37b7b632466a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop mdp y_t of arXiv:2604.10509, 'Nonequilibrium fluctuations and moderate deviations for the occupation time of the SSEP wi'] The sequence of processes $\{\mathcal{Y}_t / a_n, 0 \leq t \leq T\}_{n \geq 1}$ satisfies the large deviation principle with decay rate $a_n^2 $ and with rate function $\mathcal{Q}_T$. Precisely speaking, for any closed set $C$ and any open set $O$ in $D([0,T], \mathcal{D}^\prime (\mathbb T^d))$,
	\begin{align*}
		\limsup_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{a_n^2} \log \mathbb P^n_{\rho_*} \Big( \{\mathcal{Y}_t/a_n, 0 \leq t \leq T\} \in C\Big) \leq - \inf_{\mu \in C} \mathcal{Q}_T (\mu),\\
		\liminf_{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{a_n^2} \log \mathbb P^n_{\rho_*} \Big( \{\mathcal{Y}_t/a_n, 0 \leq t \leq T\} \in O\Big) \geq - \inf_{\mu \in O} \mathcal{Q}_T (\mu).
	\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.072117+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1eff930866b46faa2f6211a3d437ae666add13323e2b797c476473434010f80e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo thm:unif-rsw of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Fix $m>0$, a proper $s$-embedding $\mathcal{S}^{\delta}$ of a graph $(S, (x_e)_{e\in E})$ satisfying {\mbox{\textsc{Unif(}$\delta,r_0,\theta_0$\textsc{)}}}\,, and a collection of masses $(m_{e})_{e\in E}$ all bounded by $m$. Fix a square $\Lambda^{\delta}_{\rho}$  of width $\rho>0$ drawn over $\mathcal{S}^{\delta}$. Consider the FK-Ising model on $\Lambda^{\delta}_{\rho}$, where the coupling constant on the edge $e\in \Lambda^{\delta}_{\rho}$ is $x^{(\delta)}_e=x_e + \delta m_e$, and denote by $\mathbb{P}^{\textnormal{free}}_{\textnormal{FK}}$ the measure with $\textnormal{free}$ boundary conditions on the annulus $\Lambda^{\delta}_{\rho}$. Then, there exists $c(m,r_0,\theta_0)>0$, only depending on $m,r_0,\theta_0$, such that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{P}^{\textnormal{free}}_{\textnormal{FK}} \big( \textnormal{There exist an open circuit in } \mathrm{A}_{\frac{\rho}{2},\rho} \big) > c(m,r_0,\theta_0).		
\end{equation}
A similar estimate holds for the dual model. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.963379+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f01212deaf094b39b82d86edfe6d38e931b2b4a50bec66b516cc029fd7a9263","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 24 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] For every $\mathcal{I}=[t_1,t_2]\subset[0,\infty)$ and $\mathcal{J}=[\underline{a},\overline{a}]\subset(0,1)$ it holds that
    \begin{equation*}
        V_T\to V_\infty
    \end{equation*}
    uniformly on $\mathcal{I}\times\mathcal{J}$ for $T\to\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:19:06.745734+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f063858c532c253841eb63c5e9bf695ca9b84fc369832245a76c33518930c53","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main-circle-endpoint-formula of arXiv:2606.08683, 'Exact Fourier dimensions of dyadic Mandelbrot cascades under minimal integrability'] Assume that \(W\) is in the minimal Kahane--Peyri\`ere regime. Let \(\mu_{\circ}\) be the dyadic Mandelbrot cascade measure on \(\mathbb{S}^{1}\) generated by \(W\). Then, almost surely on \(\{\mu_{\circ}(\mathbb{S}^{1})>0\}\),
\begin{equation}
\dim_{\mathrm F}(\mu_{\circ})=A_{\mathrm{loc}}(W).
\end{equation}
In particular, if \(A_{\mathrm{loc}}(W)>0\), then for every
\(0<\sigma<A_{\mathrm{loc}}(W)\) there is a finite random constant \(C_{\sigma}\)
such that
\begin{equation}
\lvert\widehat{\mu_{\circ}}(\xi)\rvert
\leq
C_{\sigma}\lvert\xi\rvert^{-\sigma/2}
\end{equation}
for all sufficiently large \(\lvert\xi\rvert\), with
\(\xi\in\mathbb{R}^{2}\). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.433034+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f0677ba62d1962ca69db62381c0cb0223c8146607d5d86aa722542b8519a390","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:Jrr-lower of arXiv:2601.07808, 'Supercritical long-range percolation on graphs of polynomial growth: the truncated one-arm'] Let $G$ be a transitive graph of polynomial growth with $d \geq 1$, and suppose that $J : V \times V \to \mathbf{R}_+$ is a transitive kernel with $J(x,y) = \Omega(d_G(x,y)^{-d \alpha})$ with $\alpha > 1$.

     Then there exists $c = c(G,J) > 0$ such that for all $r \in \mathbf{N}$

     \begin{equation}

        J(B(r), B(r)^c) 
        \geq
        c
        r^{
            \max
            \left(
                d(2-\alpha),
                d-1
            \right)  
        }
        \log(r)^{
            \mathbf{1}
            \left(
                \alpha=1+1/d
            \right)
        }.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.225443+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f120909260c031d586fc14038b956b28d0d43ddf899ea3e13d0e9518688202a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor stronger RW to independent RI coupling of arXiv:2411.16398, 'Large Deviations of Cover Time of Tori in Dimensions $d\geq 3$'] Let $\delta > 0, \eta \in (0,1) \text{ and } N \in \mathbb{N}$. Assume that $R \in (N^{\frac{1}{2}}, \frac{N}{1+\delta}]$, $\boldsymbol{F} \subset \mathbb{T}_N$ is $(1+\delta)R$-well separated, and $Q_R(x) \subset Q_\eta$ for all $x \in F$. Then for every $u \geq 1$ and $\rho \in (0,1)$, there exists a probability measure $\widetilde{\mathsf{P}}$ extending $\mathsf{P}$ and carrying independent random interlacements $\left(\mathcal{I}^{(x),u(1\pm\rho)}\right)_{x\in F}$ with the same law as $\mathcal{I}^{u(1\pm\rho)}$, such that
    \begin{multline}

        \widetilde{\mathsf{P}}\Big(\mathcal{I}^{(x), u(1-\rho)} \cap Q_R(x) \subset X[0, uN^d] \cap Q_R(\boldsymbol{x}) \subset \mathcal{I}^{(x), u(1+\rho)} \cap Q_R(x) \text{\quad for all } x \in F \Big) \geq \\
        1-CuN^{3d}\exp\Big(-c\rho^2 \sqrt{uN^{d-2}}\Big)
    \end{multline}
    holds for some constants $c,C \in (0, \infty)$ depending only on $\delta$, $\eta$ and $d$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:51:44.767550+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_1f180728f2c8da95424bdccfd60c7f5ef4dbdeca6672e53a644bf3b7c8413db7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 16 of arXiv:2608.26803, 'On bubbling of Fueter maps'] For a complex manifold $(X, I)$, let 
