{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:32:11.141555+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21a7d507b4d5a9954cc7f89607feb41dabb1c5849b6161e217a69270e6ab2e41","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem lem:8 of arXiv:2510.13572, 'Coalescence in Markov chains'] \mbox{\hfil}
\begin{letlist}
\item
We have that $1\in K(P)$ if and only if $P\in\mathcal{P}_S$ is aperiodic. 
In this case, $k(\mu_{\text{\rm ind}})=1$.
\item
If $P\in\mathcal{P}_S$ has period $d$, then the coalescence classes of $\mu_{\text{\rm ind}}$
are a.s.\ the cyclic classes of $P$ and therefore  $k(\mu_{\text{\rm ind}})=d$.
Hence, $d\in K(P)$, and moreover $d\le k$ for all $k\in K(P)$. 
\end{letlist} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:05:57.784921+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_21b2e51a66ad24610533dce66353ffdce402307cc352a4d3d4552bdb277b54cc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 39 of arXiv:2608.30549, '\vspace{-1cm}Maximally Spread Out Measures and Implications\\{} for Phase Transitions in A'] For $d, n, L_0 \in \N$ and $C_0 \geq 1$, define
  \begin{align*}
