{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:33.747522+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2363d493f8fc36c069c89f04743b1eff4d990d518a40398a80c997226adfe1c6","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 6 (Generalized pivot) of arXiv:2607.28865, 'First-Passage Percolation on Spread-out line graphs: Microscopic Regime'] A vertex $x$ is called a \emph{generalized pivot} if the set
\[
    S_x:=\{x,x+\ell,\ldots,x+(k-1)\ell\}.
\]
satisfies the following two conditions
\begin{enumeratei}
    \item every edge with one endpoint in $S_x$ is light, except no restriction on the two edges $(x-\ell,x)$ and $(x+(k-1)\ell,x+k\ell)$;
    \item every edge crossing over one of the vertices in $S_x$ is heavy.
\end{enumeratei}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:18:13.306578+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_23642882755c3097ca738ab05f75f63bfb87da5562a5c4b16315150ae9cc7fdc","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:a_priori_int_fl of arXiv:2507.15589, 'Tightness of approximations to metrics on non-simple conformal loop ensemble gaskets'] Fix $q>0$, $M,p > 0$. Let $w_1,w_2 \in B(0,\delta/4)$, and let $E$ denote the event that the following hold.
\begin{itemize}
\item Let $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1+\pi}$, $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1}$ (resp.\ $\eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2}$, $\eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2-\pi}$) be the flow lines with respective angles starting at $w_1$ (resp.\ $w_2$) and stopped upon exiting $B(0,3\delta/4)$. Then
\begin{itemize}
\item The right side of $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1}$ intersects the left side of $\eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2}$ with angle difference $\theta_1-\theta_2$, and they do not intersect in any other way.
\item $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1+\pi}$ does not intersect $\eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2}$, $\eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2-\pi}$; and $\eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2-\pi}$ does not intersect $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1+\pi}$, $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1}$.
\item Sample the internal metrics in the regions bounded between $\eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1}, \eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2}$. There exist $x',x,y,y' \in \eta_{w_1}^{\theta_1} \cap \eta_{w_2}^{\theta_2}$ such that $(x,y) \in \intptsapproxbubble{U_{x',y'}}{\epsilon}$ as in~(equation eq:intpts_bubble of the paper) and
