{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:26:02.503663+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2606e4af32b2c1c4f401730ca06dceb99ce0b6b92c4835c4280a7cc79adbc467","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn of arXiv:2608.30031, 'Ziggurats, taut foliations, and contact structures'] For $\bm{x}\in \mathbb{R}^n$ and $1 \leq k \leq n$, we define the standard positive and negative quadrants at $\bm{x}$ in the $k$-first coordinates as
\begin{align*}
    C_k^+(\bm{x}) &\coloneqq [x_1,+\infty)\times\dots\times [x_k,+\infty) \times \{(x_{k+1}, \dots ,x_n)\} , \\
    C_k^-(\bm{x}) &\coloneqq (-\infty, x_1] \times \dots \times (-\infty,  x_k] \times \{(x_{k+1}, \dots ,x_n)\}.
\end{align*}

Let $A\subset \mathbb{R}^n$ be a closed set. A point $\bm{x}\in A$ is an \textbf{upper extremal point} in the $k$ first coordinates if $$C^+_k(\bm{x})\cap A = \{\bm{x}\},$$
and it is a \textbf{lower extremal point} in these coordinates if 
$$C^-_k(\bm{x})\cap A = \{\bm{x}\}.$$","status":"unverified"}

