{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.391856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2721744300a1e1d1145dc1dc5c04690163ab0b1cdc9c04476da95f1d22a334a2","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem t:locality of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] There exist sequences $p_k \in (0, 1)$ and $\zeta_{k} \in \mathbb{R}$ such that, for any $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}$,
  \begin{equation}

    g_k \Big( p_k + \frac{\lambda}{\zeta_k} \Big) \to h_g^{-1}(\lambda),
  \end{equation}
  (contrast this with (eq:near-critical-scaling of the paper)).
  Moreover,
  \begin{enumerate}[\quad a)]
  \item ``The critical threshold is local'':\\
  The sequence $p_{k}$ converges to $p_{l} = p_c(G_1)$ as $k$ grows.

  \item ``Critical exponents are global'':\\
  If $l_k \ll g_k$, then
    \begin{equation}

      \lim_k \frac{1}{l_k + g_k} \log \zeta_{k} = \log \zeta_g.
    \end{equation}
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

