{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:28:49.058023+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_292e54c9303b902c08bc52f3caea826350da6b114557f36144c2df681072345c","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:brownian-fixed-gaussian-block of arXiv:2608.16119, 'Fully nonlinear parabolic equations under a fixed reference diffusion: weighted \texorpdfs'] For the fixed horizon $T$ and parameters $n\geq2$ and $\alpha\geq0$, set
$
 \mathcal H_{n,\alpha}
 :=L^2((0,T),t^\alpha\,\mathrm d t;\mathcal C_n)
$
and define the operator initially on $C_c^\infty((0,T);\mathcal C_n)$ by
\begin{equation}
 \mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}\widehat F
 :=t^{-1}D_\xi^2\operatorname{Sol}_n\widehat F.

\end{equation}
Then $\mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}$ extends uniquely to a bounded operator from
$\mathcal H_{n,\alpha}$ into
$L^2((0,T),t^\alpha\,\mathrm d t;L^2(\gamma_d;\mathsf{S}^d))$.
Under the Brownian pullback, this is the $n$th chaos block of the physical
source-to-Hessian map.  In logarithmic time it is represented by the causal
Volterra operator
\begin{equation*}
 (\operatorname{Volt}_{n,\alpha}\widehat F)(\ell)
 =\sqrt{n(n-1)}\int_0^\ell
   e^{-(n-1+\alpha)(\ell-\ell_0)/2}
   \widehat F(\ell_0)\,\mathrm d\ell_0.
\end{equation*}
Its exact operator norm is
\begin{equation}
 \norm{\mathscr H_{n,\alpha}^{\mathrm{Br}}}
 =\norm{\operatorname{Volt}_{n,\alpha}}_{2\to2}
 =\frac{2\sqrt{n(n-1)}}{n-1+\alpha}.

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.259482+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_293324526e4ab5d258c0c7abd24f24a8c4569b7a5dc9270f42441697a76d621d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemm 15 of arXiv:2609.01105, 'Obstruction to quasi-invariance of Gaussian measures under transport flows on Riemannian M'] Let \(q_0  \in S^0\) be defined for all~\(y\in V_2\) and~\(\eta \in \Rr^d\) by 
\begin{equation}

    q_0(y,\eta) = \omega(\eta)\theta_0^2(\kappa^{-1}(y))\left( \frac{|\eta|^{2\alpha}_{y}}{|\Jac \chi(\chi^{-1}(y))^\top\eta|_{\chi^{-1}(y)}^{2\alpha}}-1\right), 
\end{equation}
where $|\eta|^2_{z}:=g^{ij}(\kappa^{-1}(z))\eta_i\eta_j$, and with $\omega$ which is a radial function such that $\omega = 0$ near $\eta =0$ and $\omega (\eta) = 1$ for $|\eta| \geq C$ for some constant $C>0$. 
There exists~\(\delta > 0\) and~\(r \in S^{-\delta}\) such that
