{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:09.854654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29a38177ea42d1f6209b6c0c0c79b434ec84e2eae18f7820aeb05b5a851910be","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2605.20026, 'Quasihelix properties\\ of selected Volterra Gaussian processes'] Let $\gamma\in[0,1)$. Then for any $0<T_1<T_2$ the following statements hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[$(i)$]
Let $\alpha \in (-1/2, 1/2)$.
Then 
\begin{equation}
\ex\left(U^{(2)}(t)-U^{(2)}(s)\right)^2
\sim C(\alpha)(t-s)^{2\alpha+1}s^{-\gamma},
\quad \text{as } t\downarrow s,
\end{equation}
for any $s\in[T_1,T_2]$,
where
\begin{equation}
C(\alpha) = \int_0^\infty \bigl[(1+z)^\alpha-z^\alpha\bigr]^2\,dz
+ \frac{1}{2\alpha+1}
= \frac{\Gamma(\alpha+1)^2} {\Gamma(2\alpha+2)\cos(\pi\alpha)}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the process $U^{(2)}$ is a local $(\alpha+\frac12,T_1,T_2)$-quasihelix.

\item[$(ii)$]
If $\alpha=1/2$, then the process  $U^{(2)}$ is a local $(1,1-\varepsilon,T_1,T_2)$-generalized quasihelix for any $\varepsilon\in(0,1)$.

\item[$(iii)$]
If $\alpha > 1/2$, then the process $U^{(2)}$ is a local $  (1,T_1,T_2)$-quasihelix.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:38:08.428266+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29a791ce4d6bfb6d2f7919329e070a94816d922c344861637a1553d0b432c7ae","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma unique root of arXiv:2609.02073, 'Existence of closed non-planar $p$-elasticae'] Let $f:[0,\infty)\to \R$ be given as
