{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.283509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_29bb13e07e0344f8b0375b1a03a80d2a7d07907ef5f4c13ad1cc56777f7d64a0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 7 (Finite-prefix certification theorem) of arXiv:2608.21496, '2608.21496'] For integers $N>m\ge1$, the worst-case bounded-reliability identified width in Eq.~\ref{eq:worstwidthdefinition} is exactly
\begin{equation}
W_{m,N}=B_{m,N}.

\end{equation}
Define two complete joint candidate laws $P_\pm$ by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
U_\pm&\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,1),\\
T_\pm\mid U_\pm=u&\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}\{f_\pm(u)\},\\
f_\pm(u)&=\frac{1\pm\sigma_m(u)}2,
\end{aligned}

\end{equation}
and take candidate sequences iid under each law. These worlds satisfy $\theta_n^+=\theta_n^-=1/2$ for every $n\le m$, but
\begin{equation}
\theta_N^\pm=\frac{1\pm B_{m,N}}2.

\end{equation}
At the common evidence $(\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_m)=(1/2,\ldots,1/2)$, the identified set is exactly
\begin{equation}
\left[\frac{1-B_{m,N}}2,\frac{1+B_{m,N}}2\right];

\end{equation}
every interior value is attained by the iid candidate law with $U\sim\mathrm{Unif}(0,1)$ and $T\mid U=u\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}\{\lambda f_+(u)+(1-\lambda)f_-(u)\}$ for some $\lambda\in[0,1]$. Consequently Eq.~\ref{eq:exactfrontier} is the full worst-case identified width, not only a lower bound. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

