with probability at least $1-e^{-cN}$, where $c$ is a positive constant depending on the parameters $\alpha,L,\iota,\epsilon$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:54:34.578892+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ea824f011e9790956dbccde493f305ac4736c4270989fe2fac7f083bc73a597","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 13 of arXiv:2606.14876, 'High-Order Talagrand and Eldan--Gross Inequalities via Besov-Type Variance Functionals'] For all $k\in \Nbb$, $f\in \Sob^k(\Rbb^n,\gamma_n)$, $\kappa\in \Nbb^n$ with $|\kappa|=k$, and $t\geq 0$,
\begin{equation}
    \Dop^\kappa \Pop_t f = \upe^{-t|\kappa|} \Pop_t \Dop^\kappa f,
\end{equation}
and consequently, for all $k\in \Nbb$,
\begin{equation}
    \left|\grad^k \Pop_t f\right| \leq \upe^{-kt}\Pop_t \left|\grad^k f\right|.
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.667363+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2eacd5e3ef0bef73bc38037e5b2a82f3aa74a0d89760d8f91dde28061e94e3f5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop cox of arXiv:2609.00110, 'Phase Transition and Fluctuation Results for First-Passage Percolation on Spread-Out Cycle'] The following statements hold.
\begin{enumeratei}
\item  \textup{(Independent residuals.)} For every deterministic $t\geqslant0$, conditionally on $\mathcal{F}_t^{\rm orig}$,
the family
\begin{align*}
 \{R_e:e\in\mathcal{B}_{\mathfrak{a}_\ell t}\}
\end{align*}
is independent and each variable has the $\mathrm{Exp}(1)$ law.

\item  \textup{(Collision event.)} For every deterministic $s\geqslant0$, with $t=s/\mathfrak{a}_\ell $, on $\Omega_*$,
\begin{align}
 \{\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{col}}>s\}
 =
 \bigcap_{e\in\mathcal{B}_s}
 \left\{
 R_e> L_e(t)^\theta-(L_e^{\mathrm{act}})^\theta
 \right\}.
\end{align}

\item  \textup{(Cox survival.)} For every $s\geqslant0$,
\begin{align*}
 \pr(\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{col}}>s\mid\mathcal{F}_s)=e^{-\gL_s}.
\end{align*}
Consequently,
\begin{align*}
 \pr(\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{col}}>s)=\E e^{-\gL_s}.
\end{align*}

\item  \textup{(Marked form.)}
Let $e_{\mathrm{col}}$ denote the a.s.\ unique bridge realising the collision, and put $\mathcal{B}_\infty:=\bigcup_{s\geqslant0}\mathcal{B}_s$.
For $e\in\mathcal{B}_\infty$, define its individual cumulative hazard by
\begin{align*}
 \gL_e(s)
 :=
 \begin{cases}
 0,
 &s<\mathfrak{a}_\ell\gt_e^{\mathrm{act}},\\
 \left(A_e(s)/\mathfrak{a}_\ell\right)^\theta-(L_e^{\mathrm{act}})^\theta,
 &s\geqslant\mathfrak{a}_\ell\gt_e^{\mathrm{act}}.
 \end{cases}
\end{align*}
Then $\gL_s=\sum_{e\in\mathcal{B}_\infty}\gL_e(s)$.
For every non-negative predictable field $(\Psi(e,s))_{e,s}$, \emph{i.e.,}~$s\mapsto\Psi(e,s)$ is left-continuous and adapted to
$(\mathcal{F}_s)_{s\geqslant0}$,
\begin{align}
 \E\bigl[\Psi(e_{\mathrm{col}},\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{col}})\bigr]
 =
 \E\int_0^\infty
 e^{-\gL_s}\,
 \frac{2\theta}{\mathfrak{a}_\ell^\theta}
 \sum_{e\in\mathcal{B}_s}A_e(s)^{\theta-1}\Psi(e,s)
 \,\mathrm d s .
\end{align}
In words, conditionally on the censored exploration, the collision occurs on the
bridge $e$ in $[s,s+\mathrm d s)$ with rate
$\frac{2\theta}{\mathfrak{a}_\ell^\theta}A_e(s)^{\theta-1}\,\mathrm d s$, thinned by the
survival factor $e^{-\gL_s}$.
\end{enumeratei}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:12:19.528563+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2eb502e08b3e7262d2a46c4df3f92ffe097fe57e297836e0ceb4097790d3a931","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 of arXiv:2608.18557, 'Randomly Permuted Orthogonal Products and Fast Dimension Reduction'] Let \(I\subseteq [d]\) with \(|I|=m\), let $A,B \in O(d)$, and let \(X\subseteq \mathbb S^{d-1}\). We define the \emph{sampled column energy}
	of \(A\) by
	\begin{equation}
		\kappa_I(A)
		:=
		\frac dm
		\max_{k\in[d]}
		\sum_{j\in I}A_{jk}^2
	\end{equation}
	and the \emph{maximal variance} of \(B\) on \(X\) by
	\begin{equation}
		\Theta_B(X)
		:=
		d\sup_{\substack{x\in X\\k\in[d]}}
		\sum_{j=1}^dB_{kj}^2x_j^2.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:04:59.434271+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2eb6a298d872a4f97f5e58562878acb9b46604efa40563bd50f84b27e9b85c67","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem eq:kappa_condition of arXiv:2605.20697, 'Uniform-in-time propagation of chaos for \\ Second-Order Consensus-Based Optimization'] Let the objective function $f$ satisfy (assumption:bounded,assumption:lip of the paper). Assume the parameters are chosen such that the $2q$-th moments decay exponentially with rate $\lambda_{2q} > 0$ (as per (thm:exp-decay-centered-moments of the paper)). 

    For any decay rate $\kappa$ satisfying the condition:
    \begin{equation} 
        \kappa < \frac{\lambda_{2q}}{q},
    \end{equation}
    there exists a finite constant $C' > 0$ (depending on $q, \kappa, \lambda_{2q}$) such that for any threshold $A > 0$ and particle number $J \in \mathbb{N}_+$, the following concentration inequality holds:
    \begin{equation}
        \mathbf P\left[ \sup_{t \geqslant 0} e^{\kappa t} \mathfrak{M}_2(\mu_{X_t^J}) \geqslant \frac{\mathbf{E}[\mathcal{L}_{2q}(\mu_{(Y_0, \hat{Z}_0)})] + A}{c_1} \right] \leqslant \frac{C'}{A^q} J^{-\frac{q}{2}},
    \end{equation}
    where $c_1$ is the coercivity constant from (lem:hypercoercivity of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:06:21.712495+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2eb8f70419d2ec269041a9b6dda6f8928f0204d35fd20304cba08d32c7a7a18a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Maintheorem 3 of arXiv:2607.21374, 'Local structure at the maximum and sharp persistence asymptotics of rough fractional Brown'] There is a probability law \(\mu_H\) on
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal Y
  :=\mathbb R_+^2\times C_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R_+)^2

\end{equation}
equipped with its product topology, with local uniform convergence in each
path coordinate,
such that
\begin{equation}
  \mathcal L_{\widehat{\PP}_a}
  (U_a,V_a,\overline L_a,\overline R_a)
  \Longrightarrow \mu_H.

\end{equation}
Let \((U,V,\overline L,R)\) denote the coordinate random element under
\(\mu_H\).
The left horizon \(U\) is finite almost surely, whereas \(R\) is defined on
the whole half-line.  The path \(\overline L\) is constant on
\([U,\infty)\) almost surely.  Write \(L\) for the restriction of the
constant-extended path \(\overline L\) to \([0,U]\).  Moreover,
\begin{equation}
  J_\infty
  :=\int_0^Ue^{-L(s)}\,\mathrm ds
    +\int_0^\infty e^{-R(s)}\,\mathrm ds

\end{equation}
satisfies \(0<J_\infty<\infty\) almost surely and
\begin{equation}
  J_a\Longrightarrow J_\infty,
  \qquad
  \widehat{\EE}_a[J_a^{-1}]
  \longrightarrow
  D_H:=\EE_{\mu_H}[J_\infty^{-1}]\in(0,\infty).

\end{equation}
Finally, the persistence probability satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{T\to\infty}{T^{1-H}}{\PP\bigl(B_t\leq1\text{ for every }0\leq t\leq T\bigr)} = \mathsf C_H,

\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
  \mathsf C_H
  =\frac{H\,\EE[M]}{\Gamma(1/H)D_H}.

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.602573+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_2ec19cc915839d6dd9e39afe7e2632caa49f9aa78b0b8942f3234ecb9b01d9cb","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 39 of arXiv:2607.07283, 'Multi-time Markov renewal chains and stratified renewal theorems'] Let $d=2$ and fix $x\in\X$. Assume that $\overline H_x(\zerod)=1$ and that

\begin{equation*}
\overline H_x(u+v)=\overline H_x(u)\overline H_x(v),
\qquad u,v\in\N^2.
\end{equation*}

Then

\begin{equation*}
\overline H_x(u_1,u_2)=a_1^{u_1}a_2^{u_2},
\qquad a_i=\overline H_x(e_i).
\end{equation*}

If $0<a_1<1$ and $0<a_2<1$, this function cannot be the rectangular no-renewal survival of a probability distribution on $\N^2$.","status":"unverified"}

