{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.508046+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32b8e7a930fd9058bb6b6b8a65ac852653549d0375bae367168e6f753b9b48a0","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 17 of arXiv:2608.30589, 'Vector-Valued Wavelet Bases as Hilbert $\mathbb{M}_m(\mathbb{R})$-Module Bases: A Construc'] Consider the following classes of wavelet bases:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item A wavelet basis of \(L^2(\mathbb{R})\) associated with an MRA.
    \item A vector-valued wavelet basis of \(L^2(\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{R}^m)\) (\(m \ge 2\)) associated with a VMRA.
    \item An \(m\)-multiwavelet basis of \(L^2(\mathbb{R})\) associated with an MRA.
\end{enumerate}
Then
\[
  \textup{(1)} \;\implies\; \textup{(2)}
  \quad\text{and}\quad
  \textup{(2)} \;\iff\; \textup{(3)}.
\]
In particular, any basis in \textup{(1)} yields bases in \textup{(2)} and \textup{(3)}, and the construction preserves regularity, vanishing moments, and compact support.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:42:57.182688+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32bcda4fd47008e4314afe1d62ccd0118f95521de83ab5e0f673ed40cf98ba34","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:axioms of arXiv:2609.04140, 'The conformally invariant metric on $\CLE_4$ III:\\ uniqueness'] By a (strong) $\CLE_4$ metric, we mean a family of \textbf{deterministic} measurable mappings 
    \begin{equation*}
        D = \left\{D^U \colon \mathscr{LoopEns}(U) \to \BR^{U_\BQ \times U_\BQ},\quad  \Gamma_U \mapsto D_{\Gamma_U}^U\right\}_U,
    \end{equation*}
    where $U \subsetneq \BC$ ranges over all simply connected domains, such that the following conditions are satisfied whenever $U \subsetneq \BC$ is a deterministic simply connected domain and $\Gamma_U$ is a non-nested $\CLE_4$ in $U$:
    \begin{enumerate}[label=(\Roman*), ref=\Roman*]
        \item {\bfseries (Geodesic metric)} The mapping $D_{\Gamma_U}^U$ a.s.\ determines a metric on $\Gamma_U$ such that for each $\SCL_1, \SCL_2 \in \Gamma_U$, there exists an admissible path $P$ (which we shall refer to as a \emph{$D_{\Gamma_U}^U$-geodesic}) connecting $\SCL_1$ and $\SCL_2$ such that $D_{\Gamma_U}^U(\SCL_1, \SCL_2) = \len(P; D_{\Gamma_U}^U)$. Moreover, for all conformal mapping $\phi \colon U \to \BD$, the process $(\phi(\SCB_t(\SCL; D^U_{\Gamma_U})))_{t\ge 0}$ is c\`adl\`ag with respect to the Hausdorff distance, and, for all $t, s\ge 0$, if $\SCB_t(\SCL_1; D^U_{\Gamma_U}) \cap \SCB_s(\SCL_2; D^U_{\Gamma_U}) \neq \emptyset$, then $D^U_{\Gamma_U} (\SCL_1, \SCL_2) \le t+s$. The same is true with a deterministic connected arc (resp.\ two deterministic disjoint connected arcs) of $\partial U$ in place of $\SCL_1$ (resp.\ $\SCL_1$ and $\SCL_2$).
        \item {\bfseries (Locality)} Let $V \subseteq U$ be a deterministic simply connected subdomain. Then, a.s., for each connected component $V_j$ of $V^\star$, 
        \begin{equation*}
            D_{\Gamma_U}^U(\SCL_1, \SCL_2; V_j) = D_{\Gamma_U|_{V_j}}^{V_j}(\SCL_1, \SCL_2), \quad \forall \SCL_1, \SCL_2 \in \Gamma_U|_{V_j}.
        \end{equation*}
        \item {\bfseries (Conformal invariance)} Let $\phi \colon U \to \phi(U)$ be a deterministic conformal mapping. Then, a.s., 
        \begin{equation*}
            D_{\Gamma_U}^U(\SCL_1, \SCL_2) = D_{\phi(\Gamma_U)}^{\phi(U)}(\phi(\SCL_1), \phi(\SCL_2)), \quad \forall \SCL_1, \SCL_2 \in \Gamma_U. 
        \end{equation*}









        \item {\bfseries (Uniform exploration)} The collection $\{(\SCL, D_{\Gamma_U}^U(\SCL, \partial U))\}_{\SCL \in \Gamma_U}$ has the law of a uniform exploration of $\Gamma_U$ (cf.\ [CoInCLEExpl]).
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.471449+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32c6df9479c8a7b0a621b3cbbeb07f5878b9d8cab550f05d3e73da2c4faf5106","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:optimallift of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $\mathcal{L}_O$ be an ergodic QMS generator on a finite-dimensional von Neumann algebra that satisfies $\sigma_o$-KMS detailed balance for a full-rank state $\sigma_o$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ denote its second-order lift as defined in (def:2ndlift of the paper). We say that $\mathcal{L}$ is an \emph{optimal (second-order) lift} of $\mathcal{L}_O$ if the $L^2$ convergence rate of $\mathcal{L}$ satisfies
    \begin{align*}
        \nu(\mathcal{L}) = \Theta(\sqrt{\lambda_O})\,,
    \end{align*}
    where $\lambda_O$ is the spectral gap of $\mathcal{L}_O$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:29:08.731164+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_32cd0118bbec5438636ea26b4b6bca90da0c8730ebbf34082c7051fd21d4ce1a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem apriori of arXiv:2412.00593, 'A new approach to strong convergence II.\\The classical ensembles'] There exist universal constants $C, c>0$ such that 
\begin{equation}

	\mathbf{P}[\|X^N\|>K] \le Cr e^{-cN},
\end{equation}
where $K:=(Cr)^{q_0}\|X_{\rm F}\|$. Moreover, we have
\begin{equation}

	| \mathbf{E}[ \mathop{\textnormal{tr}} h(X^N)]| \le 2\sobnorm{h}{0}{[-K,K]}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}

	|\mathbf{E}[\mathop{\textnormal{tr}}  h(X^N) \cdot 1_{\{\|X^N\|>K \}} ] | 
