{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:15:16.652577+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_346d2c154124cd828ff9086aa7d1cb955e33f02381236b65a5599188c73c63ee","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 of arXiv:2608.30752, '2608.30752'] Let \(u_0\colon\Sigma\to(S,h)\) be an orientation-preserving 
covering map and let
\(Y=(\Sigma,u_0^*h)\).  Then there are constants depending only on \(Y\) such
that
\begin{equation}
  \frac{\eta_Y}{2}e^{2d_{\mathrm T}(X,Y)}
  \le
  E_{u_0}(X)
  \le
  A_Ye^{2d_{\mathrm T}(X,Y)}

\end{equation}
for every \(X\in\mathcal T(\Sigma)\), where
\(
  \eta_Y
  :=
  \min_{[\lambda]\in\mathbb P\mathcal{ML}(\Sigma)}
