{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:09:43.671834+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_349270ef53a3df6b73ffe46318b7e07502f8ff04d2f60e25824d6ec457abee39","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:positive:definite of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] A function \( K: T \times T \to \mathbb{R} \) is called \emph{positive definite} if, for every finite subset \( T_0 \subseteq T \) and every collection of scalars \( (c_t)_{t \in T_0} \subset \mathbb{R} \), it holds that
