{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:52:45.030856+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37da9709447f488286dfa3f15fd1a3316dd539c938f8a9a8eb9be3e8b30af741","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 6 of arXiv:2606.07194, 'Survival Probability of Markov Linear Reccurence Sequence in a Random Environment: Subcrit'] i) The process is strongly subcritical iff for some positive $\delta$ and natural $i$
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} {\bf P}(Y_n>0|Y_0=i) s^n = +\infty
\end{equation}
for some $s: s\rho^+<1$. Moreover, $1/\rho$ is the radius of convergence of series~(\ref{ProbPowerSeries}).\\
ii) Let $1/\rho^*(h)$ be the radius of convergence of the series
\begin{equation}

\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} {\bf E}(Y_n^h|Y_0=i) s^{n},\  h\in (0,\widehat{h}).
\end{equation}
Let $h^*_{++}$ be the solution of the equation
$$
R(h) = \rho,\ R'(h)>0,
$$
where if there's no solution, we say that $h^*_{++}=+\infty$. Then
$$\rho^*(h)=\left\{
\begin{array}{cc}
\rho,& h<h^*_{++},\\
R(h),& h\in [h^*_{++},\widehat{h}).
\end{array}
\right.
$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:39:12.369736+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37dd76b5fe6c13cf2d70d200df33aca6027d6a2d4b302990cadd6f2d0fbe3f47","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ap1:lyapunov1 of arXiv:2412.07710, 'An H-theorem for a conditional McKean-Vlasov process related to interacting diffusions on '] Suppose $f:[0, T]\times \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m$  and $\mu_0 \in \mathcal{P}(\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m)$ satisfy the conditions of Theorem (thm:ap1:wellPosed2 of the paper). Let $g: \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m \rightarrow \ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ be a $C^1$ function such that $\nabla g$ satisfies a linear growth condition. Let $\{\mu_t\}_{t \in [0, T]}$ be the weak solution to (equation eq:ap1:formalPDE of the paper), which exists by (1) of Theorem (thm:ap1:wellPosed2 of the paper). Then for almost every $0 < r < t < T$, we have
\begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        \int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m} \mu_t \big( \log \mu_t + g \big) dx - &\int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m} \mu_r \big( \log \mu_r + g \big) dx = \\
        & - \int_r^t \int_{\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}^m} (\nabla \mu_s + f_s \mu_s) \cdot \bigg(\frac{\nabla \mu_s}{\mu_s}  + \nabla g \bigg) dx ds.
    \end{split}

\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.228918+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37df13a38ef162ef060ddca696fc36b5115500baf7e446670f528fa6eb4b9609","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 6 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] Let $\varepsilon>0$. For all $s<t$, consider the set of points
\begin{equation}
    (z_i)_{i\in \lbr k\rbr} := \{ j\varepsilon \in [s,t] \text{ for some } j\in \N \} 
\end{equation} where $k\in \N_0$ is the number of points, and where we set $z_0=s$ and $z_{k+1}=t$, and $z_{i-1} \leq z_{i}$ for all $i\in \lbr k+1 \rbr$. 
    A geodesic $\pi\in \mathfrak{G}_{(x,s),(y,t)}$ with $(x,s;y,t)\in \R^{4}_{\uparrow}$ is $\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}$\textbf{-good} if for all $i\in \lbr k+1 \rbr$
    \begin{equation}
      \sup_{\ell \in [z_{i-1},z_i]}  \big| \pi(\ell) - \pi(z_{i-1}) \big| \leq \frac{1}{32}  . 
    \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.971558+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37e0353a658df54dc808c8009bcd39634bbc61d415bcaf26d07f9d790cd8da30","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2607.22438, 'A deterministic scalar reduction for the phase transition in two interacting urns'] Let \(z_0\in(1/2,3/4)\) be a zero of \(\Phi_p\), and set
\begin{equation*}
 y_0=q_p(z_0),
 \qquad
 x_0=2z_0-q_p(z_0).
\end{equation*}
Assume \((x_0,y_0)\in\Omega_+\). Then \(q_p'(z_0)\in(0,2)\). Consequently, if \(\Phi_p'(z_0)<0\), then \((x_0,y_0)\) is asymptotically stable and \(\lambda_+(x_0,y_0)<0\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.852348+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_37e10fc89c5d9098c8eeb1097528e7a206f604ebd1588d729b569ae826f8016a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 (Master equation for the eigenvectors) of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] Assume the spectrum $\{\lambda^{(N)}_j\}_{j=1}^N$ is simple.
Let $\lambda^{(N+1)}_i$ be any zero of \eqref{eq:secular}. Then the overlaps satisfy
\begin{subequations}
