{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:37:14.889806+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3cd5fd2caaea969af50c3c953e0ee7e1371a29170ed1355dc81952e44058f05c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2607.11608, '2607.11608'] Let $f$ be a strictly increasing function on $\R_+$
such that   $f(0) = 0$ and its inverse $f^{-1}$ is also strictly increasing.
Then, for any $a, b \ge 0$, we have
\begin{align}
ab \le \int_0^a f(x) dx   +  \int_0^b f^{-1}(x) dx 

\end{align}

\noi
with equality if and only if $b = f(a)$.
In particular, applying \eqref{Young1}
to  $f(x) = e^x - 1$ and $f^{-1}(x) = \log(1+x)$
\textup{(}with $b$ replaced by $b-1$\textup{)}, 
we have 
\begin{align*}
ab \le e^{a} + b \log b - b

\end{align*}

\noi
