{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:50:30.569566+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3dde69fe3e5e5f8a5e882db2a751dbc44147b635f372991605640ebacdfc8bb6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 18 of arXiv:2605.30779, '2605.30779'] Let $l \geq 0$ and $\Theta = (\theta_1, \dots, \theta_l)$. For every \(\varepsilon>0\), there exists a random variable \(\mathcal J' =\mathcal J'(\varepsilon)\) such that almost surely,
\begin{equation} 
\left|
a_j^{\Theta}
+\left(\frac{3\pi j}{2}\right)^{2/3}
