\begin{bmatrix}
\Sigma_1 & \\
& \Sigma_2
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_1^{*} \\
V_2^{*}
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\Sigma_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{k \times k},\;
\Sigma_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{(n-k)\times(n-k)}.
\]
and define $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$ via $\Omega_1=V_1^\star  \Omega$ and $ \Omega_2=V_2^\star  \Omega$. Assuming that $\Omega_1$ has full row rank, the approximation error satisfies
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\left(\mathbf{I}-P_{Y}\right) A\right\|_F^2 \leq\left\|\Sigma_2\right\|_F^2+ \| \Sigma_2 \Omega_2 \Omega_1^{\dagger} \|_F^2,
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.971336+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3de838f077758cccb72fbddaa13fb5405771de1a8cf071f2f6431998bdb298ba","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 9 of arXiv:2607.22438, 'A deterministic scalar reduction for the phase transition in two interacting urns'] Suppose \(z_0\in(1/2,3/4)\) satisfies
