{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.500887+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3eac353244798fc100936950f1105a77fa7285ef0f6d30e80931b7dfd53289be","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition D:geo of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Let $X:\mathbb{Z}^2 \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\cup\{\infty\}$ be a field of random variables that are independent subject to the symmetry condition $X(i,j)=X(j,i)$ for all $i,j$, and distributed as follows.

\textbf{Exponential setting.}
Take real parameters $\alpha$ and $\{\gamma_i\}_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}$. For any $i\in\mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,i)\sim \operatorname{Exp}(\alpha+\gamma_i)$ if $\alpha+\gamma_i>0$, and $X(i,i)=\infty$ otherwise.  
For any $i\neq j\in\mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,j)=X(j,i)\sim \operatorname{Exp}(\gamma_i+\gamma_j)$ if $\gamma_i+\gamma_j>0$, and $X(i,j)=X(j,i)=\infty$ otherwise.

\textbf{Geometric setting.}
Take positive parameters $\alpha$ and $\{\gamma_i\}_{i\in\mathbb{Z}}$. For any $i\in\mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,i)\sim \operatorname{Geo}(\alpha\gamma_i)$ if $\alpha\gamma_i\in(0,1)$, and $X(i,i)=\infty$ otherwise.  
For any $i\neq j\in\mathbb{Z}$, take $X(i,j)=X(j,i)\sim \operatorname{Geo}(\gamma_i\gamma_j)$ if $\gamma_i\gamma_j\in(0,1)$, and $X(i,j)=X(j,i)=\infty$ otherwise.

For any $u\le v$ in $\mathbb{Z}^2$, define the \emph{passage time}
\[
X(u;v)=\max_{\pi}\sum_{(i,j)\in\pi}X(i,j),
\]
where the maximum is taken over all up-right paths $\pi$ from $u$ to $v$ satisfying (equation E:diagonal-condition of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:31:14.255947+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3eba068a3bc0ca5a240b6305614c224a45e639ce1e08af244e7ed2a75fb195cf","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn def:1.1 of arXiv:2606.16237, 'The Backward Stochastic Partial Differential Integral Equations: Solvability and Compariso'] A triple process $(u,q,r)\in S_{\mathbb{F}}^2(0,T;H)\cap M_{\mathbb{F}}^2(0,T;V)\times M_\mathbb{F}^2(0,T;H)\times{M}_\mathscr{P}^{\nu,2}([0,T]\times Z;H)$ is called a solution of BSEE (equation eq:a2 of the paper) if for any $\eta\in V$ and all $t\in[0,T]$, it holds that
\begin{eqnarray*}
(u(t),\eta)_H &=& (\varphi,\eta)_H + \int_t^T \Big[ \langle A(s)u(s),\eta\rangle + \langle f(s,u(s),q(s),r(s,\cdot)),\eta\rangle \nonumber\\
&& + (g(s,u(s),q(s),r(s,\cdot)),\eta)_H \Big]ds - \int_t^T (q(s), \eta)_H dW_s \nonumber\\
&& - \int_t^T \int_Z (r(s,e),\eta)_H \tilde{\mu}(de,ds)\ \ \ \text{a.s.}
\end{eqnarray*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:17:42.309311+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_3ec0caea7143d5c4377e510d7724a15e820cc37c8784938993679531fb166c0f","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 98 of arXiv:2510.22155, 'Random walks in space-time random media in all spatial dimensions: the full subcritical fl'] Fix $k\in \mathbb N$ and $q\ge 2$ and a decreasing function $F:[0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ such that $\sum_{\ell=0}^\infty \ell^{d-1}F(\ell)^{1/4}<\infty$. Let $\br\in \Ek$ be centered and $\delta$-repulsive.

