{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:38:11.735509+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_41b9d8f737a1dea12817682b5a339a27ae6ae7e9cec517f2f9e013a6c792ccda","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def_moving_Lambda of arXiv:2504.00512, 'Localization-delocalization transition for a random block matrix model at the edge'] Suppose $\Lambda_t:[a, b] \rightarrow \mathbb{C}^{D N \times D N}$ is a continuous map such that $\Lambda_t$ satisfies (main_assm of the paper) throughout the evolution. 
     We define $m_t(z)$ as the unique solution to 
 \begin{equation}\nonumber
m_t(z)= \big\langle \left(\Lambda_t -z- m_t(z)\right)^{-1} \big\rangle ,
\end{equation}
such that $\im m_t(z)>0$ whenever $z\in \mathbb{C}_+$.  

Then, we define $M_t=M_t\left({z,\Lambda_t}\right)$ as
\begin{equation}\nonumber
        \begin{aligned}
            M_t(z)=
            \left(\Lambda_t-z-m_t(z)\right)^{-1},
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
noting that $m_t(z)=\left\langle M_t(z)\right\rangle$. 

The associated probability density is given by
    \begin{equation}\nonumber
        \begin{aligned}
            \rho_t(E)=\frac{1}{\pi} \lim _{\eta \downarrow 0} \operatorname{Im} m_t(E+i \eta).
        \end{aligned}
    \end{equation}
We denote the support of $\rho_t$ by $\left[E_t^{-}, E_t^{+}\right]$, where $E_t^{\pm}$ represent the spectral edges. For $z=E+\mathrm{i} \eta$, we also define $ \kappa_t\equiv \kappa_t(E):=|E_t^{+}-E|\wedge |E-E_t^{-}|$. Finally, we define the quantiles $\gamma_k\left({t}\right)$ of $\rho_t$ as in (equation eq:gammak of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

