{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.537719+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_45c71f3abf801bb8d1dd1079272138a61370963b3255f296c5d8a3f2422b01e1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 13 of arXiv:2603.28226, '2603.28226'] An atomic filtration $(\mathcal F_n)_{n\ge1}$ is called $\alpha$-regular if there exists $\alpha\in(0,1]$ such that for every $n\ge2$, every parent atom $P\in\mathcal A_{n-1}$, and every child atom $C\in\mathcal A_n$ with $C\subseteq P$,
