{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.675633+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46c17d5be73a42edd37aa1a7cd1ec5e8050b35037f30c9dce82fd03ac7a04f46","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 4 (Sparre Andersen's formula: Probabilistic version) of arXiv:2608.16752, 'Absorption Probabilities for Random Convex Hulls: Distribution-Freeness via the Wall-Cross'] Let $n\geq1$. Let $\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n$ be real-valued,
possibly dependent random variables with partial sums $S_k:= \xi_1+\dots+\xi_k$, $1\leq k\leq n$, and suppose that the following conditions are satisfied:
\begin{itemize}
\item [(i)]  \emph{Symmetric exchangeability:} For every permutation $\sigma$ of the set $\{1,\dots,n\}$ and every choice of signs $\eps_1,\dots,\eps_n\in \{-1,+1\}$, the following equality in distribution holds:
    $$
    (\xi_1,\dots,\xi_n) \stackrel{d}{=}(\eps_1 \xi_{\sigma(1)}, \dots, \eps_n \xi_{\sigma(n)}).
    $$
\item[(ii)] \emph{No partial-sum atoms at $0$:}
For every $1\leq i \leq n$, we have $\pr[S_i = 0] = 0$.
\end{itemize}
Then
    \begin{align*}
        \pr(S_1 > 0, S_2 > 0, \dots, S_n > 0) = \frac{1}{2^{2n}}
        \binom{2n}{n}.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:51:30.067244+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46dfdfa5ffe98d618faf289917790081d75b0f8772a715494b399ccf7e1515cc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 12 ($a+b=1$, Stieltjes transform) of arXiv:2606.00781, '2606.00781'] If $a+b=1$, then the generalized Stieltjes transform $S_b$ of the stationary distribution $\mu$ is given by
   \begin{equation} 
      S_b(t)\ =\ \frac{2}{1+(1+t)^b},\qquad t\ge 0.
   \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:23:05.295881+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_46e00a6448fc487d95743a2c94806d6018114984cb531f1abaeef8354131cb82","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 11 of arXiv:2608.21701, 'Superdiffusivity of random walks on the three-dimensional randomly oriented Manhattan latt'] There exists $C_\eps<\infty$ such that, for every $m,n\ge1$,
\begin{align}

&\frac{n}{\sqrt{z_{2m}(n)}} + \frac{n}{\sqrt{z_{2m+1}(n)}} +\frac{m(1+\log z_{2m+1}(n))}{z_{2m+1}(n)}+\frac{m(1+\log z_{2m}(n))}{z_{2m}(n)}
\le C_\eps K_2^{-1/2}\varepsilon_m.
\end{align}
Let $(c_m)_{m\ge1}$ be the sequence defined by
\eqref{eq:def.cm}. Then there
