       \psi_{i_N}(\dots \psi_{i_2}(\psi_{i_1}(\mathbb T))\dots)\stackrel{d}{=}\mathbb T(v;\tau_{i_N}(\dots\tau_{i_2}(\tau_{i_1}(\pi))\dots)). \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation} 
    \end{align*}
    In particular, letting $N \to \infty$ above, for the case when $I$ is infinite and $I=\{i_1,i_2,\dots\}$ is some enumeration of it, 
    \begin{align*}
        \psi_\infty(\mathbb T):=\dots \psi_{i_3}(\psi_{i_2}(\psi_{i_1}(\mathbb T)))\dots\stackrel{d}{=}\mathbb T(v;\tau_\infty(\pi)). \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation} 
    \end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:36:12.191952+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4889e40ee1d180d6d65c99565f442201a8238ae3149ef3bbdd0b20062e549df6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 2 of arXiv:2609.03924, 'Beyond normal fluctuations in local laws for Wigner matrices'] Let $W$ be a Wigner matrix satisfying Assumptions~\ref{ass:momass} and~\ref{ass:support}. Fix (small) $\delta, \epsilon>0$, (large) $C>0$ and consider the bulk spectral domain
$\mathcal{D}=\mathcal{D}(\delta,\epsilon,C)$. Let
\begin{equation*}
X:= N\eta\langle G(z)-m(z)\rangle
\end{equation*}
be the natural rescaling of $\langle G(z)-m(z)\rangle$. The following results hold for any fixed $p,q\in \N\cup\{0\}$ with $p+q\ge 3$, uniformly in $z=E+\ii\eta\in\mathcal{D}$.

\emph{(i)} Both for $\beta=1$ and $\beta=2$ it holds that
\begin{equation}
\kappa_{p,q}\left(X\right) = \frac{\eta^{p+q}}{N^{(p+q-2)/2}}K_{p,q}(z)\kappa_{p+q}^{\mathrm{d}} + \frac{N^\xi}{N\eta} \mathcal{O}\left( \frac{1}{N^{(p+q-3)/2}}+ \frac{1}{(N\eta)^{p+q-3}}\right),

\end{equation}
for any fixed $\xi>0$. Here $\kappa^{\dif}_{p+q}:=\kappa_{p+q}(\chi_{\rm{d}})$ is the cumulant of a normalized diagonal entry $w_{aa}$ of $W$, and the function $K_{p,q}$ is explicitly given by
\begin{equation}
K_{p,q}(z) := \left( - \frac{m^2}{1-m^2}\right)^p \left(  - \frac{\overline{m}^2}{1-\overline{m}^2}\right)^q, 
\qquad m= m(z).

\end{equation}

\emph{(ii)} In the complex case ($\beta=2$) we further have
\begin{equation}
\left\vert\kappa_{p,q}\left(X\right)\right\vert \lesssim \frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\bm{1}_{p+q=3} + \frac{1}{N},

\end{equation} 
while in the real case ($\beta=1$) it holds that
\begin{equation}
\kappa_{2,2}\left(X\right) = \frac{5\ii}{2} \left(-\frac{m}{1-m^2}\right)^3 \frac{1}{(N\eta)^3} + \mathcal{O}\left(\frac{1}{(N\eta)^4}+\eta\right).

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.712413+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_4890175a4fe3cb59b855e0ca9349f2de005d626788864ef7c8c5f265377948c5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 13 (Exact spectral decomposition and crossover boundary) of arXiv:2609.02728, 'Momentum in large-batch training: Polyak enlarges the critical batch size, Nesterov improv'] For each coordinate $j$, the characteristic roots of the Polyak recursion are given by
\[
    y_{j, \pm} = \frac{(1+\rho-\eta\lambda_j) \pm \sqrt{\Delta_j}}{2},
\]
where the discriminant is $\Delta_j := (1+\rho-\eta\lambda_j)^2 - 4\rho$. 

The critical transition $\Delta_j = 0$ corresponds exactly to the spectral threshold $\eta\lambda_j = (1-\sqrt{\rho})^2$, which is asymptotically equivalent to $\eta\lambda_j \eqsim (1-\rho)^2$. This threshold classifies the individual eigen-directions into:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Overdamped Modes} ($\eta\lambda_j < (1-\sqrt{\rho})^2$, i.e., $\Delta_j > 0$): The roots are strictly real.
    \item \textbf{Underdamped Modes} ($\eta\lambda_j > (1-\sqrt{\rho})^2$, i.e., $\Delta_j < 0$): The roots are complex conjugates.
\end{enumerate}
In the mixed-damping regime, the boundary index $J_{\max}$ separating the underdamped modes from the overdamped modes is defined as the crossover index satisfying $\eta\lambda_{J_{\max}} \eqsim (1-\rho)^2$:
\begin{equation}

    J_{\max} \eqsim \left(\frac{\eta}{(1-\rho)^2}\right)^{1/\beta}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, based on the maximum eigenvalue $\lambda_{\max}$ of the population Hessian $\bH$, the algorithm globally operates in one of two regimes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Fully Overdamped Regime} ($\eta\lambda_{\max} \lesssim (1-\rho)^2$): All spectral modes are overdamped.
    \item \textbf{Mixed-Damping Regime} ($\eta\lambda_{\max} \gtrsim (1-\rho)^2$): The spectrum bifurcates into high-curvature underdamped modes ($j \le J_{\max}$) and flat overdamped modes ($j > J_{\max}$).
\end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:20:15.486148+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48930d4f38aa0202984b6bcf3488c5c256d26d1b72b7dea121216dacf09f98be","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 76 of arXiv:2509.01797, 'Relation between Wick powers and excursion clusters of the 2D GFF'] Let $n\geq 1$. A.s., the Wick power $:\Phi^{n}:$ can be decomposed
\begin{equation}

:\Phi^{n}:~=\psi^{\rm gen}_{n,0}
+
\sum_{0\leq k <\lfloor n/2\rfloor}
(-1)^{k}\dfrac{n!}{2^{k} k! (n-2k)!}
V_{A^{\rm gen, +}_{0}}^{k}
\,
:\Phi_{D\setminus A^{\rm gen, +}_{0}}^{n-2k}:\,.
\end{equation}
Fix $\eta>0$. 
Then a.s.,
\begin{multline*}
\E\big[\Vert :\Phi^{n}:\Vert^{2}_{H^{-\eta}(\C)}\big\vert \mathcal{F}_{0}^{\rm gen, +}\big]
=
\Vert \psi^{\rm gen}_{n,0}\Vert^{2}_{H^{-\eta}(\C)}
\\+
\E\Big[
\Big\Vert
\sum_{0\leq k <\lfloor n/2\rfloor}
(-1)^{k}\dfrac{n!}{2^{k} k! (n-2k)!}
V_{ A^{\rm gen, +}_{0}}^{k}:\Phi_{D\setminus A^{\rm gen, +}_{0}}^{n-2k}:
\Big\Vert^{2}_{H^{-\eta}(\C)}
\Big\vert \mathcal{F}_{0}^{\rm gen, +}
\Big] .
\end{multline*}
In particular,
\begin{multline*}
\E\big[\Vert :\Phi^{n}:\Vert^{2}_{H^{-\eta}(\C)}\big]
=
\E\big[\Vert \psi^{\rm gen}_{n,0}\Vert^{2}_{H^{-\eta}(\C)}\big]
\\
+
\E\Big[
\Big\Vert
\sum_{0\leq k <\lfloor n/2\rfloor}
(-1)^{k}\dfrac{n!}{2^{k} k! (n-2k)!}
V_{ A^{\rm gen, +}_{0}}^{k}:\Phi_{D\setminus A^{\rm gen, +}_{0}}^{n-2k}:
\Big\Vert^{2}_{H^{-\eta}(\C)}
\Big].
\end{multline*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:01:39.422970+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_48971cc75662b96448a407890ca9a161148e2e77236f65632281f70a503af4aa","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:shift-cumulants of arXiv:2607.22156, 'A random matrix approach to lamplighter groups'] Let $\epsilon_1,\ldots,\epsilon_n\in\{1,-1\}$ and $b_1,\ldots,b_n\in\mathcal{B}_0$. Put $p_j=\epsilon_1+\cdots+\epsilon_j$. Then
