{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:57:20.676304+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51beacf59e6f6c4efbde4c1f9c3da94de033579b553425de9c0ff2fe9e779ff2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theo:wsobo of arXiv:2411.10235, '\textbf{Smooth transport map via diffusion process}'] For $\phi\in C^2(\mathbb{R}^d)$ satisfying $\phi(x)=a\|x\|^{1+s}$ for $\|x\|\geq K,$ with $ K,a,s>0$, define the measure $\mu$ having a density $d\mu(x) = \exp(-\phi(x))dx$ with respect to the $d$-dimensional Lebesgue measure. Then, for all $k\in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$ and $f:\mathbb{R}^d\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ with $\|f\|_{L_{\mu}^2}=1$, we have
\begin{align*}
    \int |f(x)|^2|\log(|f(x)|)|^{2sk/(s+1)}d\mu(x)\leq C \sum_{|\alpha|=0}^k\|D^\alpha f\|^2_{L_{\mu}^2}.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.338774+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51c22022aa2179c00344d42f08e63fc1db153f39c20a0e2f0d30440cb9c89263","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 16 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] Assume that $(G, H)$ is an inhomogeneous graph system, with a constant subsampling parameter $s \in (0, 1]$, where the mother graph $G^\uparrow$ follows the stochastic block model. If $V_i, V_j$ are communities, denote $\alpha_i = \frac{|V_i|}{n}$ the size of community $i$ and $\frac{q_{ij}}{n}$ the edge probability from $V_{i}$ to $V_j$. We then define $\lambda = \lambda_n$ to be the Perron-Frobenius eigenvalue of the matrix 
    \begin{equation}
        Q D = (q_{ij})_{1 \leq i,j \leq m} \cdot \diag(\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_m).
    \end{equation}
    Assume that for any $i, j$, $q_{ij} > 0$ is a constant, independent from $n$. Then: 
       \begin{itemize}
        \item if $\ds\operatorname*{pliminf}_{n  \to +\infty} (s^2 \lambda_n) > 1$, then $\ds\operatorname*{pliminf}_{n \to +\infty}ov(\hat{\pi}, \pi^*) > 0$: partial alignment is feasible.
        \item if $\ds\operatorname*{plimsup}_{n  \to +\infty} (s^2 \lambda_n) < 1$, then for any estimator $\hat{\pi}'$, $ \ds\operatorname*{plimsup}_{n \to +\infty}ov(\hat{\pi}', \pi^*) = 0$: partial alignment is intractable.
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:27:14.875607+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51c34b90ff666216f0c217ce9e0c156c821f8fcadcf5ce4acf44f21b2c85f119","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.23462, '2608.23462'] Let $\beta>0$ and assume that $(\hat Y^x_t)_{t \ge 0}$ is $2$-dissipative with constant $\beta$. Suppose that $f$ is $L_f$-Lipschitz and differentiable in $y$ with $D_y f$ bounded and jointly continuous on $\R^{d_1+d_2}$. Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{___lem_DyFlow}, $\Phi$ is differentiable in $y$, its derivative is jointly continuous on $\R^{d_1+d_2}$, and
    \begin{align*}
        |D_y \Phi (x,y)| \leq \frac{L_f}{\beta} 
    \end{align*}
    for all $x \in \R^{d_1}$ and $y \in \R^{d_2}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.767196+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51d9c15d5597278782d4dfc62564f11409cc0f048404c1869ff8d57824aaa996","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 37 of arXiv:2512.04807, 'Existence and uniqueness of the canonical Brownian motion in non-simple conformal loop ens'] Consider the setup described in Section~\ref{subsec:tightness_setup}, and for each $\epsilon \in (0,1]$ let $\rmetapprox{\epsilon}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\Gamma} = (\rmetapproxres{\epsilon}{V}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\Gamma})_{V \in \metregions}$ be as defined in Section~\ref{se:rmet_approx_scheme}. Let $\median{\epsilon} > 0$ be as defined Section~\ref{se:approx_scheme_general_def}.
Let $V \in \metregions$ be chosen according to some probability distribution given $\Gamma$, and for each $\epsilon$ let $V_\epsilon \in \metregions$ be such that $V_\epsilon \supseteq V$.

Then each sequence $(\epsilon_n)$ with $\epsilon_n \to 0$ contains a subsequence $(\epsilon_{n_m})$ along which we have the weak convergence of the laws of $\median{\epsilon}^{-1} \rmetapproxres{\epsilon}{V_{\epsilon}}{\cdot}{\cdot}{\Gamma}\big|_{(\ol{V} \cap \Upsilon_\Gamma) \times (\ol{V} \cap \Upsilon_\Gamma)}$ in the topology described in Section~\ref{subsec:tightness_setup}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:05:37.779989+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51da2ce9e79e7356622ed4cc1a710a2459a1d1a961347e17a184eb4120824021","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:gauge-link of arXiv:2608.01253, 'The pseudo-quantum representation of \\finite reversible Markov chains'] Let $\mathcal{E}:=\operatorname{diag}\bigl(i^{\,n-k}\bigr)_{k=0}^{n}$, a fixed unitary diagonal
matrix independent of $t$, and let $\lvert \psi_\theta\rangle:=e^{-i\thetaJ_x}\lvert n\rangle$ be
the register coherent state of the imaginary slice. Then

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{E}\,J_x\,\mathcal{E}^{-1}=J_y,\qquad \mathcal{E}\lvert n\rangle=\lvert n\rangle,\qquad
\mathcal{E}\ket{\psi_{\theta}}=e^{-i\thetaJ_y}\lvert n\rangle,

\end{equation}

so $\ket{\sqrt{\pi_t}}=\mathcal{E}\ket{\psi_{\theta(t)}}$ and
$\sigma^{\mathrm{FR}}_t=\mathcal{E}\,\ketbra{\psi_{\theta(t)}}{\psi_{\theta(t)}}\,\mathcal{E}^{*}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.845092+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51e3c49c727723e76e1e10eaa3d190d086c7702c43ba0e1d9925f4377de56f12","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi 10 of arXiv:2511.10535, 'Eigenvalues of Brownian Motions on $\mathrm{GL}(N,\mathbb{C})$'] Let $(\A,\tau)$ be a $W^\ast$-probability space, and let $\A_1,\dots,\A_n$ be $*$-subalgebras of $\A$, sharing the same unit $1_\A$. They are said to be {\bf freely independent} or just {\bf free} if, for all $k$, for all $a_i\in\A_{j_i}$ such that $j_1\neq j_2$, $j_2\neq j_3$, \dots , $j_{k-1}\neq j_k$:
        \begin{equation}

            \tau\Big( (a_1-\tau(a_1))(a_2-\tau(a_2))\dots (a_k-\tau(a_k)) \Big) = 0.
        \end{equation}
        I.e.\ products of centered elements from adjacent-alternating algebras have trace $0$.
		Families of non-commutative random variables are said to be free if the $*$-subalgebras they generate are free.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.853152+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51eae51f3082f01e58afc44c13a1b2aedea7022e9e6f8bc273975048326c2dea","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:smoothedIbPF of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] (Smoothed Versions of the IbPFs) For all $F \in C_b^1(L)$ and $h \in H_0^1 \cap H^2$ we have the IbPF
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{E}_{\rho^{a,\varepsilon}\mu^{\beta}}\left[ \partial_h F \right] + \mathbb{E}_{\rho^{a,\varepsilon}\mu^{\beta}}\left[ F \, (h'',\cdot)_{L} \right]
		&= - \int_{\mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R})} F(\beta(\omega)) \, \bar{h}_a \gamma_{\varepsilon}(\delta_a^{\mathrm{ext}}(\beta(\omega))) \, d\mu(\omega)\\
		&= - \langle \!\langle F(\beta), \bar{h}_a \gamma_{\varepsilon}(\beta_a) \rangle \!\rangle,\nonumber
	\end{align}
	where $\langle \!\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle \!\rangle$ denotes the Hida dual pairing, see Subsection (sect:wna of the paper), which extends the canonical scalar product on $L^2(\mu)$. Further, the following IbPF holds:
	\begin{align}
		\mathbb{E}_{\rho^{\varepsilon}\mu^{\beta}}\left[ \partial_h F \right]
		+ \mathbb{E}_{\rho^{\varepsilon}\mu^{\beta}}\left[ F \, (h'', \cdot)_{L} \right]
		&= - \int_{\mathcal{S}'(\mathbb{R})} F(\beta(\omega)) \, \int_{(0,1)} \bar{h}_x \gamma_{\varepsilon}(\delta_x^{\mathrm{ext}}(\beta(\omega))) \,dx \, d\mu(\omega)\\
		&= - \Big\langle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\langle F(\beta), \int_{(0,1)} \bar{h}_x \gamma_{\varepsilon}(\beta_x) \, dx \Big\rangle \mspace{-7mu} \Big\rangle. \nonumber
	\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.659734+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51ec3b2073748f01e93116d4582faf932b68ff4e5d25b3c61498b1d93bef28c5","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:characteristic of arXiv:2608.28004, 'Hausdorff Dimension of the Set of Extreme Points\\ of a Random Countable Stable Zonotope'] For \(t\in u^\perp\),
\begin{equation}
    \bigl|\mathbb E e^{i\langle t,Y(u,v)\rangle}\bigr|
    =
    \exp\{-c_\alpha\Psi_{u,v}(t)\},
    \qquad
    \Psi_{u,v}(t)
    \mathrel{\overset{\mathrm{def}}{=}}
    \int_{S^{d-1}}
    |\langle t,x\rangle|^\alpha\,|a_{u,v}(x)|\,\sigma(\mathop{}\!\mathrm d x),
\end{equation}
where
\(c_\alpha=\alpha\int_0^\infty(1-\cos s)s^{-1-\alpha}\mathop{}\!\mathrm d s>0\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.591993+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51ed10448554e56fe95507b4321a6a87c6c1e7c4082cac0bec6b8cf2d8d95329","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition WMStoBous of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] [[Zbl1548.60081]]
The combination $\left\{ {\left( {{\Omega } ,\mathcal{F}_t,\mathbb{P}} \right),{u }\left( {t,x} \right),\mathfrak{p}{\left( {t , x} \right)},\mathfrak{W}\left( {t,x} \right)} \right\}$ is said to be the martingale solution to Eq.(equation 2DEEUGE1 of the paper), if it satisfies the following conditions.
\par
\noindent$(\romannumeral 1)$. The Wiener process $\mathfrak{W} \left( {t} \right)$ generates right-continuous filtration ${{\left\{ {{\mathcal{F}_t}} \right\}}_{t \in \left[ {0,T} \right]}}$, and $\left( {{\Omega } ,\mathcal{F}_t,\mathbb{P}} \right)$ is the complete probability space for any ${t \in \left[ {0,T} \right]}$;
\par
\noindent$(\romannumeral 2)$. There exists a stopping time ${\mathfrak{t}_{\mathfrak{S}}}$ such that for any $\psi \left( x \right) \in {C^\infty }\left( {{\mathbb{T}^2}} \right) $ and $t \in \left[ {0,T \wedge {\mathfrak{t}_{\mathfrak{S}}}} \right]$,
\begin{equation*}
{\left\langle {u\left( t \right) - u\left( 0 \right),\psi } \right\rangle _{{\mathbb{T}^2}}} + \int_0^t {{{\left\langle {\left( {u\left( s \right) \cdot \nabla } \right)u\left( s \right) + \nabla \mathfrak{p} \left( s \right),\psi} \right\rangle }_{{\mathbb{T}^2}}}} \mathrm{d}s = {\left\langle {\mathfrak{W}\left( t \right),\psi } \right\rangle _{{\mathbb{T}^2}}} ;
\end{equation*}
\par
\noindent$(\romannumeral 3)$. The process ${u}\left( {t} \right)$ is $\mathcal{F}_t$-adopted and satisfies ${{u^\nu }\left( {t,x} \right)} \in {L^\infty }\left( {\left[ {0, T \wedge {\mathfrak{t}_{\mathfrak{S}}}} \right];{\mathcal{H}}} \right)$;
\par
\noindent$(\romannumeral 4)$. The process ${\left\langle {\mathfrak{W}\left( t \right),\psi } \right\rangle _{{\mathbb{T}^2}}}$ is a martingale.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:26:26.760002+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_51efa688badbb47753cc191c32a4ac230233d7aa15a9ca7df116b26e9fccd29e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:BLPP-multipoint of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] The dressed observable $\mathcal{A}_n$ satisfies
