{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:51:12.726722+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_526fd99cf4b5823bf953b45e19fdd3c6506703895f1567783bd7c7624576846c","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 8 of arXiv:2605.02002, 'Glauber dynamics for random field Ising models on bounded degree graphs and MLSI'] Consider $\mu$ a probability distribution on $\Omega$. Then a localization scheme having $\mu$ as target distribution is made up of a pair of continuous time processes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The noising process $(X_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$. This is a Markov process such that $X_0\sim\mu$ and $X_1$ follows a Dirac measure.

    \item A denoising process $(Y_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$. This process is defined as the reversal of $(X_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$: $Y_\theta=X_{1-\theta}$. Thus $Y_0$ is a Dirac measure and $Y_1\sim\mu$. 
\end{itemize}
Since $X_t$ is a time reversal of $Y_t$, we use the denoising process $(Y_t)_{t\in[0,1]}$ to represent the localization scheme and call it the localization process.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.358381+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5273f3b02ff25d1916d292afc93176e33069b9be8f2c6d5c8da9b0074d6a5a4d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2511.09517, 'From Cannings model to Brownian motion conditioned on local time profile'] \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*), ref=\roman*]
    \item For any continuous profile \(\ell\) with \(h(\ell)<\infty\), the local time profile of \(W^\ell\) is \(\ell\); that is, a.s. for every non-negative measurable \(f\) on \(\mathbb{R}_+\),
