{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:11:09.327200+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_53202039b255770549c41f3a5a155b68779d44b7d1f74b2fdd5aac8f9f28d242","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop gbf of arXiv:2411.06571, 'Moment-based approach for two erratic KPZ scaling limits'] Consider a two-variable process $(G_{s,t})_{-\infty <s\le t<\infty}$ with $s,t\in \Bbb Q$, defined on some complete probability space, and satisfying 
\begin{enumerate}
        \item $G_{s_j,t_j}$ are independent if $(s_j,t_j)$ are disjoint intervals.

        \item $G_{s,t}G_{t,u}= G_{s,u}$ whenever $s<t<u$.

        \item For $n=1,2,3,4,5,6$, we have that $\Bbb E\big[ G_{s,t}^n \big] = e^{\frac{n^2}2(t-s)}.$
\end{enumerate}
These three conditions uniquely determine the finite-dimensional distributions of the process $(G_{s,t})_{s<t}$ as being the same as those of $(e^{B(t)-B(s)})_{s<t}$  for a standard Brownian motion $B$, and moreover $B$ can be defined on the same space so that the relation holds pathwise on $\Bbb Q^2$.","status":"unverified"}

