{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:31:02.826645+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_544b066971ae85845e06bbcc8916697ad5f6d478c3da1d67f69ecf206476e8d0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:popularity-higher-k of arXiv:2609.01690, 'Iterated-sumset spectra: The complete exponent law and its rank geometry'] Fix $k\geq3$ and $h\geq2$.  There are explicit positive rational
constants $\kappa_{k,r}$, $2\leq r\leq h$, such that
as $q\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
 F^{(k)}_{h,M_{h,k}-M_{h-r,k}}(q)
 =\kappa_{k,r}q^{k-1}+O_{h,k}(q^{k-2}),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
 F^{(k)}_{h,M_{h,k}}(q)
 =\binom qk-
 \left(\sum_{r=2}^h\kappa_{k,r}\right)q^{k-1}
 +O_{h,k}(q^{k-2}).
\end{equation}
Every other attainable cardinality has frequency $O_{h,k}(q^{k-2})$.
Thus the $h$ values
\begin{equation}
 \{M_{h,k}\}\cup
 \{M_{h,k}-M_{h-r,k}:2\leq r\leq h\}
\end{equation}
are exactly the popular values.  In decreasing order, their consecutive
gaps are
\[
 \binom{j+k-2}{k-2}\qquad(0\leq j\leq h-2).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:21:56.726838+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_544eb3fdde9223a5367ea66e02b9f1225e971adf12a863a1dfe000374be0d6f6","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma le:tightness_bdry_single_loop of arXiv:2507.15589, 'Tightness of approximations to metrics on non-simple conformal loop ensemble gaskets'] There exists $\zeta > 0$ such that the following is true. Fix $0 < r < 1$ and $c_1 > 0$. For $\delta > 0$, let $E_\delta$ be the event that there exists a loop $\mathcal {L} \in \Gamma$ and two non-overlapping segments $\ell_1,\ell_2 \subseteq \mathcal {L}$ such that the left boundaries of $\ell_1,\ell_2$ intersect and there are four points $\widehat{x},x,y,\widehat{y}$ on their intersection in exactly this order such that
\begin{itemize}
\item $\abs{x-\widehat{x}} \ge \delta^{c_1}$, $\abs{y-\widehat{y}} \ge \delta^{c_1}$,
\item if $U_{x,y}$ is the region bounded between the left boundaries of $\ell_1,\ell_2$ from $x$ to $y$, then $U_{x,y} \subseteq B(0,r)$, $\mathrm{diam}(U_{x,y}) \le \delta$, and $D_\epsilon^{\partial U_{x,y}} \ge \delta^\zeta+\epsilon^{a_0}$.
\end{itemize}
Then $\mathbf{P}[E_\delta] = o^\infty(\delta)$.","status":"unverified"}

