{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.390884+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_545bd11b069924de396882daa68ca755411ae97046e423adc25447591ff303ec","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition p:benjamini_schramm_marks of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] The distribution $\bar{G}_\infty = (G_\infty, E_\infty) \in \mathcal{G}$ has the following properties:
  \begin{enumerate}[\quad a)]
    \item The address of the edge root $E_\infty$ is an infinite i.i.d.\ sequence of random variables $(e_j)_{j \in \mathbb{Z}_-}$, each $e_j$ being chosen uniformly in $\mathcal{E}_1$.
    \item For each $k$, $G_\infty$ is can be tiled by copies of $G_k$. That is, $G_\infty$ is an edge-disjoint union of subgraphs $G_k^w$ isomorphic to $G_k$, where $w \in \sigma^k(\mathcal{E}_{1}^{\mathbb{Z}_-})$. Furthermore, tilings for different levels are compatible, in the sense that, for $k > k'$, $G_k^w$ is tiled by graphs of the form $G_{k'}^{w'}$, with $w' \in \sigma^{k'}(\mathcal{E}_{1}^{\mathbb{Z}_-})$.
    \item $E_\infty$ is contained in a nested sequence of graphs
    \begin{equation}
      G_1^{w_1} \subset G_2^{w_2} \subset \dots \subset G_k^{w_k} \subset \dots ,
    \end{equation}
    each $G_k^{w_k}$ being an element of a tiling in the above item, moreover $w_k = \sigma^{k}(\dots, e_{-1}, e_0) = (\dots, e_{-(k + 1)}, e_{-k})$.
  \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.750795+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_545f5a43bbdba4473735b73e8c048fdca16c0929110b4c3698e04bdbfb85d91a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma eq:bound_Grad_kol of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] For any $n,m\in{\mathbb {N}}_0$ and $\gamma\in(0,1)$, and for any $\sigma,T>0$, there exists a positive constant $C=C(n,m, \gamma, T,\sigma,B)$ such that the upper bounds
\begin{equation}
\big| \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0} p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r,x,y)  \big|  \leq  \frac{C}{r^{\frac{m+n }{2}}}  p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r, x  , y) ,
\end{equation}
the regularity bounds in forward variables
\begin{align}
\big| \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0} p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r,x,y) - \nabla^{n}_{y^0}\nabla^{m}_{x^0} p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r,x,y') \big| & \leq C \, \frac{|y - y'|^{\gamma}_B}{r^{\frac{m+n + \gamma}{2}}} \big( p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r, x  , y) + p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r, x  , y') \big), \\
 \\
\big| \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0}  p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r,x,y) - \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0}  p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}\big(r',x, e^{(r'-r) B} y\big) \big| & \leq C \bigg(\frac{r'}{r} \bigg)^{Q/2}  \frac{(r'-r)^{\gamma}}{r^{\frac{m}{2}+\gamma}} \, p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r', x  , y),\\

\end{align}
and the regularity bounds in backward variables
\begin{align}
\big| \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0} p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r,x,y) - \nabla^{n}_{y^0}\nabla^{m}_{x^0} p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r,x',y) \big| & \leq C \, \frac{|x - x'|^{\gamma}_B}{r^{\frac{m+n + \gamma}{2}}} \big( p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r, x  , y) + p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r, x'  , y) \big), \\
\big| \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0}  p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}(r',x,y) - \nabla^{n}_{y^0} \nabla^{m}_{x^0}  p_{\mathscr{K}_{\sigma}}\big(r,e^{(r'-r) B}x,  y\big) \big| & \leq C \bigg(\frac{r'}{r} \bigg)^{Q/2}  \frac{(r'-r)^{\gamma}}{r^{\frac{m}{2}+\gamma}} \, p_{\mathscr{K}_{2\sigma}}(r', x  , y),
\end{align}
hold for any $r'>r>0$ and $x,x',y,y'\in\mathbb{R}^d$. Here, $p_{\mathscr{K},\cdot}$ is the Gaussian density in (equation eq:kol_dens of the paper) and $Q$ is the so-called intrinsic dimension, i.e.
\begin{equation}
Q := \sum_{j = 0}^{\mathfrak{r}} d_j (2j +1).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.310506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_54627e4bc1eafc5ca6877af9403176f2e205976bd8525a63d63a769a56f6d90b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2604.08127, 'Asymptotics of Brownian occupation measures \\ with unusually large intersections'] For any $q > 1$ and $0 < \gamma < \frac{2q}{q-1}$, consider the Gibbs measure
    \begin{equation}

        \mathrm{d} \widehat{\mathbb{P}}_{t} = \frac{1}{Z_t} \exp \left\{ t^{1 - \gamma} \beta([0, t]^q)^{\gamma/q} \right\} \mathrm{d} \mathbb{P}.
    \end{equation}
    Then, the Brownian occupation measures $\widetilde{L}_t$ under the law $\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_t \circ \widetilde{L}_t^{-1}$ converge to
    \[  
    \lim_{t \to \infty} \widetilde{L}_t = \widetilde{\mu}_{1, q, \gamma} \quad \text{in } \widetilde{\mathcal{M}}_1(\mathbb{R}),
    \]
    where $\mu_{1, q, \gamma}$ has density $\psi_{1, q, \gamma}^2$ and $\psi_{1, q, \gamma}$ is the unique (up to shifts) solution to the variational problem
    \begin{equation}

    \rho_{1, q, \gamma} = \sup_{\substack{\psi \in H^1(\mathbb{R}) \\ \| \psi \|_2 = 1}} \left\{\| \psi \|_{2q}^{2\gamma} - \frac{1}{2} \| \nabla \psi \|_2^2 \right\}.
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.251842+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5468506e387872f5781478f302fe045bf2409b3b49fa7b14a7f2118a9a50c4cb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 18 (\cite{KellerRose-22a}) of arXiv:2609.01098, 'Gaussian upper bounds for averaged heat semigroups on graphs'] For $b\geq a\geq 0$, $\kappa>0$ and $\omega\in\ell^\infty(X)$ $\kappa$-Lipschitz, we have 
\begin{align*}
\fint_{a}^{b}\Vert P_\tau^{\omega}\Vert_{2,2}^2 \drm \tau
\leq \euler^{-2\Lambda a+\gamma(\kappa S)b},
\end{align*}
and 
\[
h(\omega)\leq \gamma(\kappa S)
:=\frac{2}{S^2}\left(\cosh\left(\kappa S\right)-1\right).
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:35:19.877248+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5468fe10580ce0d3cbbfc050e47f3e6da324df0823b8e3c7e1880fb922286225","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor closed-ineq of arXiv:2608.27939, 'Entropy and domination for quasi-Hitchin representations'] For the curve $\gamma$ constructed in the above Proposition, we have the following strict inequalities of the Hilbert and translation lengths: 
