	where $N(h) = \E h(Z)$ with $Z \sim \mathcal{N}(0,1)$, $h \in \mathcal{H}_1$ and $f_h$ is the solution to the Stein's equation. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.418303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_54c0f731de5b54f39fcd0ee65180c468378ed2f1d65fbb136509677f08699a2f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 112 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Consider the variational datum \eqref{eq_ZW_data_1}-\eqref{eq_ZW_data_2} and suppose that the sextuple
 \[
 (\theta, \hat n, \hat A_1', \hat A_2', \hat B_1', \hat B_2')
 \]
 belongs to the domain $\mathcal D$ of Definition~\ref{Definition_ZW_set}. Then the simplified equilibrium measure $\widetilde \mu$ has a single band $(\alpha,\beta)$ and its density inside the band is given by
 \begin{equation}

 \forall x \in (\alpha,\beta)\qquad \widetilde \mu(x) =\frac{1}{\pi\theta}\,\arctan\Bigg(\hat D' \frac{\sqrt{(x - \alpha)(\beta - x)}}{q^+(x)}\Bigg),
 \end{equation}
 where $\arctan(z)$ takes values between $0$ and $\pi$ for $z\in [-\infty,+\infty]$ with $\arctan(\pm \infty)=\tfrac{\pi}{2}$,
 \begin{equation}

\begin{split} q^+(z) & = 2z^2 - (\hat{A}'_1 + \hat{A}'_2 + \hat{B}'_1 + \hat{B}'_2)z + \hat{B}'_1\hat{B}'_2 + \hat{A}'_1\hat{A}'_2 - \theta\hat{n}(\theta\hat{n} + \hat{D}') \\
\alpha & = \frac{1}{(\hat{D}')^2}\Big(-(\hat{A}'_1 + \hat{A}'_2 + \hat{B}'_1 + \hat{B}'_2)(\hat{D}' + \theta\hat{n})\theta\hat{n} + (\hat{B}'_1\hat{B}'_2 - \hat{A}'_1\hat{A}'_2)(\hat{D}' + 2\theta\hat{n})- \sqrt{\Delta} \Big), \\
 \beta & = \frac{1}{(\hat{D}')^2}\Big(-(\hat{A}'_1 + \hat{A}'_2 + \hat{B}'_1 + \hat{B}'_2)(\hat{D}' + \theta\hat{n})\theta\hat{n} + (\hat{B}'_1\hat{B}'_2 - \hat{A}'_1\hat{A}'_2)(\hat{D}' + 2\theta\hat{n}) + \sqrt{\Delta}\Big), \\
 \Delta & = 4\theta\hat{n}(\hat{D}' + \theta\hat{n})\hat{D}'_{1,1}\hat{D}'_{1,2}\hat{D}'_{2,1}\hat{D}'_{2,2} > 0.
\end{split}
\end{equation}
Besides, if the parameters are in $\mathcal{D}^{\tau}$ with $\tau \in \{\pm 1\}$, then
\begin{itemize}
\item[$\bullet$] if $\tau\,q^+(\hat{A}_1') > 0$, then $[\hat{A}'_1,\alpha]$ is a void;
\item[$\bullet$] if $\tau\,q^+(\hat{A}_1') < 0$, then $[\hat{A}'_1,\alpha]$ is a saturation;
\item[$\bullet$] if $\tau\,q^+(\hat{B}_1') > 0$, then $[\beta,\hat{B}'_1]$ is a void;
\item[$\bullet$] if $\tau\,q^+(\hat{B}_1') < 0$, then $[\beta,\hat{B}'_1]$ is a saturation.
\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:57:07.297879+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_54c96774055c061a3d59bd2987f240dbf7843a22927d29b2e7817d80862d12ad","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 4 (Lemma~A.1 in \cite{forien_stepping_2019}) of arXiv:2606.23242, 'Correction to ``The stepping stone model in a random environment'' - limit theorems for th'] There exists a constant $ C > 0 $ such that, for $ \mu $-almost every $ \omega \in \Omega $,
