{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.974716+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5503e1fcfdad9fb217bc7de253b19f37c13fa0e55e4a6e2c12e7287f28ee4b44","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eq:defA of arXiv:2411.07157, 'Rough differential equations in the flow approach'] For any ${\boldsymbol{\tau}} \in\text{$\widehat{\mathcal{T}}$}$ such that $r({\boldsymbol{\tau}})\geqslant1$, we set $k\equiv k({\boldsymbol{\tau}})\eqdef\min\{i\in[p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})]:r_i=1\}$. Moreover, to have notation for writing Leibniz rule for scale derivatives of cumulants, for ${\boldsymbol{\tau}} = \big( (\tau_{i},0,u_i) \big)_{i=1}^{p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})} \in \text{$\widehat{\mathcal{T}}$} $ (note that $r({\boldsymbol{\tau}}) = 0$) and $1 \leqslant k \leqslant p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})$, we define \textcolor{white}{$\mathbf d$}
\begin{equs}
    \mathbf{d}_{k} {\boldsymbol{\tau}} \eqdef \Big( (\tau_{i}, \mathbf{1}\{i = k\},u_i) : 1 \leqslant i \leqslant p({\boldsymbol{\tau}}) \Big) \in \widehat{\mathcal{T}}\,.
\end{equs}
Note that is holds $k(\mathbf{d}_k{\boldsymbol{\tau}})=k$.

Fix ${\boldsymbol{\tau}} \in\widehat\mathcal{T}$ such that $r({\boldsymbol{\tau}})\geqslant1$, and recall the definition of $\text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau_k)$ given in Definition~(def:defBmu of the paper). Moreover, fix $v_1,v_2\in\{0,1,2\}$ such that $v_1+v_2=u_k$. We combine $v_1,v_2$ and $(\sigma_1,\sigma_2)\in\text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau_k)$ in the forest ${\boldsymbol{\sigma}} = \big((\sigma_1,v_1),(\sigma_2,v_2) \big)$. 

We then define 
\begin{equs}
\text{$ {\mathrm{Ind}}$}(\text{${{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}$})\eqdef
\Bigg\{ 
\big({\boldsymbol{\sigma}}, \pi,\rho \big):
\begin{array}{c}
(\sigma_1,\sigma_2) \in \text{$\mathrm{Ind}$}(\tau_k)\,,\\
 v_1,v_2\in\{0,1,2\}:\,v_1+v_2=u_k\,,
\\
(\pi,\rho) \in \mathcal{Q}\big([p(\text{${{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}$})]\setminus\{k\},[2]\big)
\end{array}
\Bigg\}\;.
\end{equs}
The set $\mathrm{Ind}({\boldsymbol{\tau}})$ will appear as an indexing set for a sum in our cumulant equation. 
To motivate this notation, we note that upon fixing ${\boldsymbol{\sigma}}$, we are working with a cumulant of the form (suppressing time arguments) 

\[
\kappa_{p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})}
\Big( \big(\xi^{\tau_i}_{{\eps,\mu}}: i \in [p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})] \setminus \{k\} \big), 
\xi^{\sigma_2} \graft^{\mu} \xi^{\sigma_1}
\Big)
\]
We then generate a sum over $(\pi,\rho)$ using (equation eq:relcum of the paper), each term corresponding to a product of joint cumulants indexed by the $|\rho|$ different blocks of  $\rho$. 

We fix $({\boldsymbol{\sigma}},\pi,\rho)\in\mathrm{Ind}({\boldsymbol{\tau}})$. For each $1 \leqslant q \leqslant |\rho|$, we define ${\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_{q} \in \text{$\widehat{\mathcal{T}}$}$ via the concatenation of ordered forests ${\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_q \eqdef {{\boldsymbol{\tau}}}_{\pi_q}\sqcup{{\boldsymbol{\sigma}}}_{\rho_q}$, where we set  ${\boldsymbol{\lambda}}_q={\boldsymbol{\sigma}}_{\rho_q}$ if $p({\boldsymbol{\tau}})=1$. 

Now, in order to define our analogue of the r.h.s. of (equation eq:flowLabelled of the paper) for the cumulant flow, we fix some representative $\underline{\boldsymbol{\tau}}\in {\boldsymbol{\tau}}$ verifying (equation eq:restrictionforest of the paper). For $j\in[2]$, we also fix some representatives $\underline{\sigma}_j\in\sigma_j$, along with $f\in\mathrm{Aut}(\underline{\tau}_k)$ and $n\in N(\underline{\sigma}_1)$ such that $\underline{\tau}_k=\underline{\sigma}_2\graft_n\underline{\sigma}_1$.

Then, following Remark~(rem:2_24 of the paper), we introduce the short-hand notations
 \begin{equs}       
    \ell_{\underline{\sigma}_1,\underline{\sigma}_2,j}\eqdef \begin{cases}  
  0 & \text{for} \;j= 1\,, \\
   \ell_{\underline{\sigma}_1,\underline{\sigma}_2}& \text{for} \;j= 2\,, \\
\end{cases}
           \quad\text{and}\quad
           t_{\underline{\sigma}_i} \equiv t_{\underline{\sigma}_i}(t_{\underline{\tau}_k},w)\eqdef \begin{cases}  
    t_{\underline{\tau}_k} & \text{for} \;j= 1\,, \\
      w & \text{for} \;j= 2\,. \\
\end{cases}
   \end{equs}

Recall that $f$ was promoted to a map
$\text{$\mathbf{f}$}:\bigotimes_{j=1}^2\text{${\mcb{C}}$}[\underline{\sigma}_j+\ell_{\underline{\sigma}_1,\underline{\sigma}_2,j}]\longrightarrow{\mcb{C}}[\underline{\tau}_k]$. We further promote","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:16:18.654450+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5504f48df330a25e9a83ee3bbf6a2f13f1a54dd5e754e97d39419f22b53b02fc","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn 7 of arXiv:2607.24561, 'Range convergence and sharp one-arm asymptotics\\for the critical percolation cluster in h'] For $x \in \partial \mathcal Q_{z, i}$, we say that $x$ is $s$-good if $\mathcal T_{s}(i;x)$ holds. Otherwise, we say that $x$ is $s$-bad. For $x \in \partial \mathcal Q_{z, i}$, we call $x$ a $K$-regular point if the following two conditions hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item $x$ is $s$-good for $s\ge K$;
\item $x$ is connected to $0$ in $\mathcal Q_{z, i}^c$.
\end{enumerate}
Similarly, if $x$ is connected to $0$ in $\mathcal Q_{z, i}^c$, but it is not $K$-regular, then we say it is $K$-irregular. We write $X_{r,i}^{K-\mathrm{reg}}$ (resp.\ $X_{r,i}^{K-\mathrm{irr}}$) for the number of $K$-regular (resp.\ irregular) points on $\partial \mathcal Q_{z, i}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:41:03.269250+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_550652387292eceda2f1b8f8338abd7db08f30c7048863b20f2259513753a567","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Mydef gg1 of arXiv:2411.08430, 'The Restricted Isometry Property of Block Diagonal Matrices Generated by $\varphi$-Sub-Gau'] For an Orlicz $N$-function $\varphi$ satisfying $Q-$condition, then for $1 \leq p<\infty$ distribution-dependent Talagrand-type $\gamma$-functional 
	is defined by 
