{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:25:40.141291+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_58320815a7862bb14873d187947209a2846a3dc84a79cc1ded2dbce4f3acb8f4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition semigroup-representation of arXiv:2609.00425, 'Individual and Uniform Eventual Positivity of Self-Adjoint Semigroups'] The operator $A$ generates a self-adjoint $C_0$-semigroup $(\mathrm{e}^{tA})_{t \geq 0}$ on $\ell^2(\mathbb{Z})$. Moreover, one has
    \begin{align*}
        \mathrm{e}^{tA} = \mathrm{e}^{tA_0} + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} h_n(t)\,q_n\otimes q_n,
    \end{align*}
    where $h_n(t) \coloneqq \mathrm{e}^{-(n-\delta_n)t}-\mathrm{e}^{-nt}$ for all $t\geq0$ and $n\in\mathbb{N}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:25:44.584504+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_583312d1c134b434d88b083751487e723d14aaea089c8388aeffa632a04547c0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def:hyperunif of arXiv:2509.08659, 'Gap metrics for stationary point processes and quantitative convexity of the free energy'] A point process $\mathsf{P}\in \mathcal P_{s,1}(\Gamma)$ is  hyperuniform if \begin{align*}
       \frac{1}{n} \int (\xi(\Lambda_n)-n)^2\mathsf{P}(d\xi)\xrightarrow{n\to \infty}0.
    \end{align*}
    The process $\mathsf{P}$ is class-1-hyperuniform if  $ \int (\xi(\Lambda_n)-n)^2\mathsf{P}(d\xi)=O(1)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:27:25.803927+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5834321046582e7c89164b9cc618a65a60353bc4a584651b972372abbb160b06","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:stopping-criterion of arXiv:2605.17082, 'Spectral Purification in Reversible Markov Chains: Hidden Parameters, Observable Equivalen'] Define the \emph{online convergence indicator}
\begin{equation}
\Gamma_k := \frac{\widehat{V}_k}{\rho_k^2} = \frac{\rho_{k+1}}{\rho_k} - 1 \;\ge\; 0.
\end{equation}
Assume that a lower bound $\tau \le 1 - (\lambda_3/\lambda_2)^2$ is known (or estimated online; see (rem:tau-estimation of the paper)). Define the critical threshold
\[
\eta(\varepsilon) := \frac{\varepsilon^4}{8} \cdot \tau^2.
\]
Then the stopping rule ``stop at the first $k$ such that $\Gamma_k \le \eta(\varepsilon)$'' guarantees
\[
\|v_k - s_2 \phi_2\|_\pi \le \varepsilon,
\]
provided that $\alpha_2(k) \ge 1/2$ at termination (which holds for all sufficiently large $k$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.318170+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_58422738689619b7e8bcdff0e28147f6f63688aea2bdc8d7c9c83e64369209a2","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ITS-carac of arXiv:2411.14926, 'Convex combinations of random variables stochastically dominate the parent for a new class'] A random variable $X$ is {\rm InvSub}\ if and only if
