{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:32:35.588517+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5930d1b090876896fff1f8980df19aa0f8e8cdf12a88e5c5a27c402da6cfdd6f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:mainexplicit of arXiv:2606.06187, 'How to Study Reflected Brownian Motion in a Quadrant via Kernel Functional Equations?\\ \m'] For all $y\in \mathcal{D}_\mathcal{R}$, the Laplace transform $\widehat \pi_1$ is given by
