whose complements are pairwise disjoint.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:06:42.724102+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f815b9bb4fac3ff5498f3bca5a2ab0f692b0b5939a872f66d989c749b889bc4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary coro: levy1 bounded support of arXiv:2411.04238, 'Holomorphic jump-diffusions'] Fix $S\subseteq\mathbb R$ and let  $X=(X_t)_{t\in[0,T]}$ be an $S$-valued Lévy process with characteristics $(b,a,F)$. Assume that $F$ has bounded support and set
 \begin{align*}
    \mathcal{V}&:=\{h\in H(\mathbb C)\colon |h|_\mathbb R|,|h'|_\mathbb R|,|h''|_\mathbb R|\leq C\exp(a|\cdot|)\text{ for some }a\in \mathbb R, C>0 \}\\
    \mathcal V^*&:=\{\mathbf{u}\in \mathcal{S}^* \colon h_\mathbf{u}=h,  \ h\in \mathcal{V}\}.
\end{align*}
The following conditions hold true.
\begin{enumerate}
\item $X$ is an $S$-valued $\mathcal V$-holomorphic process.
    \item  The process $N^h$ given in equation (equation eq:localmart of the paper) is a true martingale for each $h\in \mathcal V$.
    \item  
    Fix $\mathbf{u}\in \mathcal V^*$. Assume that 

        $\int_\mathbb R |h_\mathbf{u}(x)|dx<\infty $ or $ \int_\mathbb R |h_\mathbf{u}(x)|^2 dx<\infty$ and  
     the map $\hat h_\mathbf{u}:\mathbb R\to\mathbb C$ given by 
        \begin{equation}
    \hat h_\mathbf{u}(u):=\int_\mathbb R h_\mathbf{u}(x)e^{-iux}dx
    \end{equation}
 satisfies
\begin{align}
        \int_\mathbb R \exp(|ux|)|\hat{h}_\mathbf{u}(u)|du<\infty,
    \end{align}
    for all $x\in \mathbb R$.  Then there exists a $\mathcal{V}^*$-valued solution $(\mathbf{c}(t))_{t\in[0,T]}$ of (equation eq: ODE pointwise of the paper) with initial condition $\mathbf{u}$ which satisfies condition~(moment formula ii of the paper) and~(moment formula iii of the paper) of Theorem~(th: moment formula holomorphic of the paper). In particular, the holomorphic formula holds true:
    \begin{align*}
    \mathbb E[h_\mathbf{u}(X_T)]=h_{\mathbf{c}(T)}(x_0).
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.646238+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5f868ca178700b8bc884184acb25378b851c0efbd4279252128f25b95aa31683","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:asympt lx of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $x>0$ and $\lambda_x$ be the function defined in~(equation eq:lx of the paper).
    Let $t_x$ be the minimizer of $\lambda_x$.
    Then, as $x$ converges to $0$, we have the following asymptotic behaviour.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item[$\mathbf{(1)}$] The minimizer $t_x$ of the function satisfies
        \begin{align*}
            t_x=\left(\frac{2\pi^2}{x}\right)^{1/3}\left[1+\frac{1}{9}\left(\frac{\pi x}{2}\right)^{2/3}+O\left(x^{4/3}\right)\right]=\left(\frac{2\pi^2}{x}\right)^{1/3}+O\left(x^{1/3}\right)
        \end{align*}
        \item[$\mathbf{(2)}$] The minimum of the function satisfies
        \begin{align*}
            \lambda_x(t_x)=3\left(\frac{\pi x}{2}\right)^{2/3}+\frac{1}{6}\left(\frac{x}{2\pi}\right)^{4/3}+O\left(x^2\right)\,,
        \end{align*}
        \item[$\mathbf{(3)}$] For any $\epsilon=\epsilon_x$ converging to $0$ as $x$ goes to $0$ (allowed to be negative), the asymptotic behaviour of $\lambda_x$ at $(1+\epsilon)t_x$ is given by
        \begin{align*}
            \lambda_x\big((1+\epsilon)t_x\big)-\lambda_x(t_x)&=3\left(\frac{\pi x}{2}\right)^{2/3}\epsilon^2+O\left(\epsilon^3x^{2/3}+\epsilon^2x^{4/3}\right)\,,
        \end{align*}
        and the $O(\cdot)$ term is uniform over any window of the form $[-\delta,\delta]$ for $\delta=\delta_x\ll1$.
    \end{itemize}","status":"unverified"}

