for every $n\geq 0$ and $1\leq j \leq n+1$. Then $\T^{(1)}(\mathbf{\lambda}) \in \mathcal{M}^{\infty}$ for any $\lambda\in\mathcal{Q}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:52:57.731208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5fd7cc3b991aaaa658a491b1f3a815f1266bbc716fb1959c23c0e3814ba9fb4e","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 14 of arXiv:2609.02732, 'Conservation of mass for solutions of Leibenson's equation on Riemannian Manifolds'] Suppose that the manifold $M$ admits the Sobolev inequality \begin{equation}\left(\int_{M}{|f|^{p\kappa} d\mu}\right)^{1/\kappa}\leq C\int_{M}{|\nabla f|^{p}d\mu}\quad \forall~f\in W^{1, p}(M),\end{equation}  with some Sobolev exponent $\kappa>1$. Let $\rho$ be a positive function on $M$ and let $u$ be a non-negative bounded weak solution of $\rho\partial _{t}u=\Delta _{p}u^{q}$ in $M\times [0, \infty)$, with initial function $u(\cdot, 0)=u_{0}\in L^{\zeta}(M, \rho d\mu)\cap L^{\infty}(M)$ for some $\zeta\geq1$. If $\rho$ is bounded and satisfies \begin{equation}\left|\left|\rho\right|\right|_{L^{\theta}(M, \mu)}<\infty,\end{equation} where \begin{equation}\theta=\left\{\begin{array}{ll}
			\frac{\kappa}{\kappa-1-\frac{D}{\zeta-D}}, & \text{if }\kappa\geq \frac{\zeta}{\zeta-D}~\textnormal{and}~\zeta> 1, \\ 
			\infty, & \text{if }\kappa< \frac{\zeta}{\zeta-D}.
		\end{array}
		\right.\end{equation} Then $u$ has a finite extinction time in $M$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:45:17.710720+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5fd7f8a20b3b61c7284d7b1aa056f644865f9f966a149284141cfc5dea77776c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theoremHP of arXiv:2604.11212, 'Sofic measures'] Let~$Y$ be a shift space over the alphabet~$A$.
An invariant probability measure on~$Y$ has a non-negative linear representation if and only if it is sofic.
In this case, the linear representation can be chosen such that
\begin{enumerate}[label=\rm(\roman*)]
\item the matrix~$M=\sum_{a\in A}\varphi(a)$ is stochastic;
\item the vector~$\lambda$ is stochastic and~$\gamma$ has all its components equal to~$1$;
\item the vector~$\lambda$ satisfies the relation~$\lambda M=\lambda$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:47:53.343765+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_5fdb645d118cc5acb90221febda406df809a5be664d298fe86b224a49c257072","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 24 of arXiv:2607.14948, 'Graph alignment in sparse inhomogeneous models via self-overlap'] Let $\rho \geq 0$; then, we have the following inequalities. For any $\sigma \in \mc{S}_n$, $X \in \Der \sigma$, 

