{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:50:48.854787+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6052b52401c8ccb92eb5d9d7d43d6f44e36f373634bcfe92dc604ebc9e44570a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem gms_survival of arXiv:2607.12061, 'Models for species evolution with random deaths'] {Bessel Link}}]
Let $f^*_c = \frac{q}{p}$, $f\in [0,1]$, and suppose $p>q$. If the number of species currently alive with fitness below $f$ is $k$, then the probability that a species with fitness $f$ survives forever is
\[
\begin{cases}
0 \qquad &\text{if } f < f^*_c, \\
1 - \left(\tfrac{f^*_c}{f}\right)^{k+1} \qquad &\text{if } f \geq f^*_c.
\end{cases}
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.316798+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60622eae747a086003eeec33599a62e3c0d44c4c6afbf052e10f5a92ebb06066","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 5 of arXiv:2604.09248, '\bfseries Limit laws for longest edges in empty region graphs'] Let $\eta_t$ be a stationary Poisson process on $\RR^d$ with intensity $t\geq 2$. To each pair of distinct points $x,y\in\RR^d$ we associate a compact set $S(x,y)$ such that $(x,y)\mapsto S(x,y)$ is measurable and the following assumptions for pairs of distinct points $(x,y),(u,v)\in(\RR^d)^2$ are satisfied:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] $\vol(S(x,y))=\gamma\|x-y\|^d$ for some $\gamma\in(0,\infty)$,
    \item[(ii)] $S(x,y)\subseteq B^d({x+y\over 2},\alpha\|x-y\|)$ for some {$\alpha\in(\frac{1}{2},\infty)$,}
    \item[(iii)] $\vol(S(x,y)\setminus S(u,v)) \geq \beta\|{x+y\over 2}-{u+v\over 2}\|\|x-y\|^{d-1}$ for some $\beta\in(0,\infty)$ if $\|x-y\|\geq\|u-v\|$ and $S(x,y)\cap S(u,v)\neq\varnothing$,
    \item[(iv)] $S(x,y)=S(y,x)$.
\end{itemize}
Define the point process
$$
\xi_t := {1\over 2}\sum_{(x,y)\in\eta_{t,\neq}^2}\mathbbm{1}\{(\eta_t-\delta_x-\delta_y)(S(x,y))=0\}\delta_{\big({x+y\over 2},\,\gamma t\|x-y\|^d-\log(t)-\log(\kappa_d/(2\gamma)),\,\mathrm{span}\{x-y\}\big)}
$$
on $\RR^d\times\RR\times\LL^d$, denote by $\zeta$ a Poisson process on $\RR^d\times\RR\times\LL^d$ with intensity measure $\vol\otimes\mu\otimes\nu$, and let $W\in\mathcal{B}(\RR^d)$ with $\vol(W)<\infty$ and $b\in\RR$. Then, for any $t\geq 2$,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{d}_{\mathrm{KR}}(\xi_t|_{W\times[b,\infty)\times\LL^d},\zeta|_{W\times[b,\infty)\times\LL^d}) \leq \begin{cases}
        c\log^{-(d-2)}(t), & d \ge 3,\\
        c\log^{-1/2}(t), & d=2,
    \end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $c\in(0,\infty)$ is a constant only depending on $d$, $\vol(W)$, $b$, $\alpha$, $\beta$, and $\gamma$. {Moreover, one has $\xi_t\overset{d}{\longrightarrow} \zeta$ as $t\to\infty$.} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.668740+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_60644b50e6006a3134d73f52e465657877f19fca203e423e6ef07fd8f634cbd4","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Def 18 of arXiv:2604.24189, 'Malliavin calculus and densities for chaos-driven stochastic differential equations'] The one-dimensional Hermite process $Z^{H,q}=\left(Z_t^{H,q}\right)_{t \in \R_+}$ of order $q\geq 1$ and self-similarity parameter $H \in\left(\frac{1}{2}, 1\right)$ is defined by
\begin{align}
	Z^{H,q}_t=I_q(L_t^{H, q}) \qquad \text{where} \qquad L_t^{H, q}(\xi)=c(H, q) \int_0^t \prod_{j=1}^q\left(s-\xi_j\right)_{+}^{H_0-\frac{3}{2}} ds,
\end{align}
$$
H_0=1+\frac{H-1}{q} \in\left(1-\frac{1}{2 q}, 1\right), \quad\text{and}\quad c(H, q)=\sqrt{\frac{H(2 H-1)}{q!\,\beta^q\left(H_0-\frac{1}{2}, 2-2 H_0\right)}}.
$$
Here $\beta$ stands for the Beta function and we use the convention, for each $(\theta, \alpha) \in \R^2$,
		\[\theta^{\alpha}_+:=\left\{ \begin{array}{rl}\theta^{\alpha}, & \text{if }\theta>0,\\ 0, &  \text{otherwise}. \end{array}\right. \]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.729279+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6069dae4e423e82268cb4b94ce40f5f6d534ecf8975135b79b54e79dc76b144e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm thm_1 of arXiv:2503.22334, 'Probabilistic analysis of optimal multi-pivot QuickSort'] Let $K\in {\mathbb N}$ and $\lambda\in \mathbb{R}$. For the normalization $Y_n:=(X_n-{\mathbb E}\left[X_n\right])/n$ as $n\to\infty$ we have 
\begin{align*}
   Y_n&\stackrel{d}{\longrightarrow} Z_K,\\
   {\mathbb E}\left[\exp(\lambda Y_n)\right] &\longrightarrow {\mathbb E}\left[\exp(\lambda Z_K)\right]<\infty,
\end{align*}
where the distribution of $Z_K$ is determined as the unique centered, square integrable probability measure such that
\begin{align}
Z_K \overset{\text{d}}{=} \sum_{i=0}^{K} \kl*{D_i Z_K^{(i)}+\alpha_K D_i \ln\left(D_i\right)}+l_{\mathrm{opt}}(D),
\end{align}
where $D,Z_K^{(0)},\dots,Z_K^{(K)}$ are independent and $Z_K^{(i)}$ has the same distribution as $Z_K$ for all $i=0,\dots,K$.
Moreover, we have moments convergence, i.e., all moments of $Y_n$ converge to the moments of $Z_K$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T10:13:21.261654+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_606bbe2f20bf6e5d22fc4bcaed75e2a6d41323909b5ba3bef92a1ea581531c7f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 28 of arXiv:2609.01473, 'Keller sequences of exact $\infty$-categories'] Given a nullcomposite sequence
	\[\begin{tikzcd}
		\cal{A} \ar[r] \ar[d] & \cal{C} \ar[d] \\
		0 \ar[r] & \cal{B}
	\end{tikzcd}\]
	of exact categories, the following are equivalent:
	\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
		\item $i$ is a Keller inclusion and $p\colon \cal{C} \to \cal{B}$ is the cofibre of $i$ in $\exact$;
		\item $p$ is a Keller projection and $i\colon\cal{A} \to \cal{C}$ is the kernel of $p$ in $\exact$.
	\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.633303+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6070a14d38a4f6b78a1f579a144fa236dd87de3f9ff33f1bc1e4373011722cb5","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition useful-prop of arXiv:2412.09087, 'General Markovian randomized equilibrium existence and construction in zero-sum Dynkin gam'] Suppose $B\subseteq {\cal I}$ is an interval with $g(x)<f(x)$ for all $x\in B$.
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]

\item  Let  
\begin{align*}
A_t:= \int_0^t \frac{1}{f(X_s)-g(X_s)}\mathbb{I}_{\{X_s\in B\}}dC_s^{-}(g), \enskip t \geq 0. 
\end{align*}
Then $A_t<\infty, t < \tau^{\partial B}$ a.s. (while it may occur that $A_{\tau^{\partial B}}=\infty$) and with $\tau_{A}:=\inf\{t\geq 0: A_t\geq E_1\}$ it holds, for all $x \in B$ and $\tau_2 \in \mathbb{T}_2$, that
\begin{align*}
& g (x)\\
& \leq \E_x\left(e^{-r(\tau_A \wedge \tau_2)} \left(
f(X_{\tau_A})\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_A<\tau_2\}} + g(X_{\tau_2})\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_A>\tau_2\}} + h(X_{\tau_2})\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_2=\tau_{A}\}}\right)\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_2 \wedge \tau_A < \tau^{\partial B}\}}\right)\\
& + \E_x\left(e^{-r\tau^{\partial B}}  g(X_{\tau^{\partial B}}) \mathbb{I}{\{\tau_A \wedge \tau_2 \geq  \tau^{\partial B}\}}\right).
\end{align*}
Moreover, if $g$ is twice continuously differentiable except on a countable set of separated points $x_j \in {\cal I},j \in J$ (where $J$ is an index set), then
\begin{align*}
A_t = \int_0^t\left(\frac{(\mathbb{L}_X-r)g(X_s)}{g(X_s)-f(X_s)}\right)_+\mathbb{I}_{\{X_s \in B, X_s \neq x_j,\forall j\}}ds + \frac{1}{2}\sum_{j} \mathbb{I}_{\{x_j \in B\}}\left(\frac{g'(x_j+)-g'(x_j-)}{{g(x_j)-f(x_j)}}\right)_+l_t^{x_j}, \enskip t \geq 0.
\end{align*}

\item Let 
\begin{align}
A_t:= \int_0^t \frac{1}{f(X_s)-g(X_s)}\mathbb{I}_{\{X_s\in B\}}dC_s^{+}(f), \enskip t \geq 0. 
\end{align}
Then $A_t<\infty, t < \tau^{\partial B}$ a.s. (while it may occur that $A_{\tau^{\partial B}}=\infty$) and with $\tau_{A}:=\inf\{t\geq 0: A_t\geq E_2\}$ it holds, for all $x \in B$ and $\tau_1 \in \mathbb{T}_1$, that
\begin{align*}
\begin{split}
& f (x)\\
& \geq \E_x\left(e^{-r(\tau_1 \wedge \tau_{A})} \left(
f(X_{\tau_1})\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_1<\tau_{A}\}} + g(X_{\tau_{A}})\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_1>\tau_{A}\}} + h(X_{\tau_1})\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_1=\tau_{A}\}}
\right)\mathbb{I}{\{\tau_1 \wedge \tau_{A} < \tau^{ \partial B }\}}\right)\\
& + \E_x\left(e^{-r\tau^{\partial B}}  f(X_{\tau^{\partial B}}) \mathbb{I}{\{\tau_1 \wedge \tau_{A} \geq  \tau^{\partial B}\}}\right).
\end{split}
\end{align*}
Moreover, if $f$ is twice continuously differentiable except on a countable set of separated points $x_i \in {\cal I}, i \in I$ (where $I$ is an index set) then
\begin{align*}
A_t =  \int_0^t\left(\frac{(\mathbb{L}_X-r)f(X_s)}{f(X_s)-g(X_s)}\right)_+\mathbb{I}_{\{X_s \in B, X_s \neq x_i,\forall i\}}ds + 
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i} \mathbb{I}_{\{x_i \in B\}} \left(\frac{f'(x_i+)-f'(x_i-)}{{f(x_i)-g(x_i)}}\right)_+l_t^{x_i}, \enskip t \geq 0.
\end{align*}



\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:28.955251+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_607114c613506f6e50faac4a950285e1fd4ada73e3d24baa0fe08e02c9645d02","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:freezing-four of arXiv:2608.23135, 'Graph distance and effective resistance of the random walk trace in four and five dimensio'] For every $p,q\ge1$, there exists $C=C_{p,q}<\infty$ such that
