{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:03:59.433195+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_624d8b429e1c8e4471d14b58c10300684e55e8b513720a436176c66cdedb1976","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:g400 of arXiv:2507.08757, 'Strong existence and uniqueness of the tilt-indexed Busemann process in the planar corner '] Assume weights are i.i.d.\ with $p>2$ moments.   Then there exists an event $\Omega_0$ of full probability on which the following holds. For each $\omega\in\Omega_0$ and every semi-infinite geodesic  $\pi_{k:\infty}$ in the environment $\omega$  such that  
$\pi_n\cdote_i\to\infty$  for both $i\in\{1,2\}$,   there exists a finite Busemann function 
\begin{equation}  A(\omega,x,y) =  \lim_{n\to\infty}  [L_{x,\pi_n}(\omega) - L_{y, \pi_n}(\omega) ] \quad \forall x,y\in\mathbb{Z}^2    \end{equation}
that recovers the weights $\omega$: 
