  \item For every $\varepsilon > 0$ there exists $\delta > 0$ so that $d(x,y) < \delta$ implies $d_Y(f(x),f(y)) < \varepsilon$ for all $(K,f) \in \mathcal {K}^W$.
 \end{enumerate}
 Then $\mathcal {K}^W$ is relatively compact with respect to the metric $d_\infty$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:08.987875+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_63a5e41c50c4df88d9362235c40dc3e5733d26e285daa2c5d0fa1297c6219e99","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor cor_SlepianPosCorr of arXiv:2608.17463, 'Persistence probabilities of fractional L\'evy fields indexed by hyperbolic space and othe'] Let $(E,d)$ be a metric space and let $(Y_t)_{t\in E}$ denote a real-valued, separable, centred Gaussian process such that $\mathbb{E}\left[Y_s Y_t\right] \geq 0$, $\forall s,t\in E$.
