{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:06:06.291313+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_659836c4f1bc4eb5a285e59e9195f942c17aceff381c5b5416322ff811cd8a2a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem theo:main of arXiv:2605.21265, 'The non-Hermitian minor process'] Let $X$ be a complex or real $N\times N$ i.i.d. matrix satisfying Assumption~(ass:chi of the paper). Fix (small) $c_0,\delta>0$ and an open bounded domain $\mathbf{D}\subset \Omega\subset \mathbf{C}$. Then the following holds.

\emph(i) The process of centered linear statistics $L^{(k)}(f)$ converges to a centered Gaussian process $L(x,f)$ in the sense of moments: 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{E} \prod_{i=1}^n \left(L^{(k_i)}(f_i)\right)^{p_i} \left(\overline{L^{(k_i)}(f_i)}\right)^{q_i} = \mathbf{E} \prod_{i=1}^n \left(L(x_i,f_i)\right)^{p_i}\left(\overline{L(x_i,f_i)}\right)^{q_i} + \mathcal{O}(N^{-c(\bm{p},\bm{q})})\,\,\,\text{with}\,\,\, x_i:=k_i/N,

\end{equation}
for any $N$-independent $n, p_i,q_i\in\mathbf{N}\cup\{0\}$, and for (possibly $N$-dependent) $x_i\in [c_0,1]$, for $i\in [n]$. The exponent $c(\bm{p},\bm{q})>0$ in (equation eq:main_macro of the paper) depends only on $\sum_{i=1}^n (p_i+q_i)$, and the implicit constant in $\mathcal{O}(\cdot)$ may depend only on $H^{2+\delta}$-norm of $f_i$, $i\in[n]$, and on the constants from Assumption~(ass:chi of the paper).

\emph{(ii)} The covariance structure of $\{L(x,f)\}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Cov}\left(L(x_1,f_1),(x_2,f_2)\right)=C_\beta\left((x_1,f_1),(x_2,f_2)\right),

\end{equation}
where the explicit sesquilinear form $C_\beta$ is defined below in (equation eq:C_beta_2 of the paper)--(equation eq:C_beta_1 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:35:57.051129+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_659dde2b3ed855f04b887a7d7030c30a17d113e91bd0629fc9220f9f24741361","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma dir_E[deg-edges]_asymp of arXiv:2604.05921, 'Simplicity of random hypergraphs'] If $\forall e \in E: \delta_e^{\textnormal{tail}} = \delta^{\textnormal{tail}}\in O(1)$ and $\delta_e^{\textnormal{head}}=\delta^{\textnormal{head}}\in O(1)$ and
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\mathds{E}[(d_U^{\textnormal{out}})^{\delta^{\textnormal{tail}}}], \mathds{E}[(d_U^{\textnormal{in}})^{\delta^{\textnormal{head}}}] \in o(n)$
    \item $\exists c>0: \lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} \mathds{P}(d_U^{\textnormal{out}}, d_U^{\textnormal{in}} \geq 1) \geq c$
\end{enumerate}
then 
\begin{align*}
    \mathds{E}[DH_n] = \Big(\frac{\delta^{\textnormal{tail}}-1}{\delta^{\textnormal{tail}}} \cdot \frac{\delta^{\textnormal{tail}} \mathds{E}[(d_U^{\textnormal{out}})^2] - 2 \mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{out}}]}{2 \mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{out}}]} + \frac{\delta^{\textnormal{head}}-1}{\delta^{\textnormal{head}}}  \cdot \frac{\delta^{\textnormal{head}} \mathds{E}[(d_U^{\textnormal{in}})^2] - 2 \mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{in}}]}{2 \mathds{E}[d_U^{\textnormal{in}}]}\Big)(1+o(1)).
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:26:40.357994+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_65a4b8d5400d47b46b7ee398dae56c367037b617fd911e311c900394becebe4f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 (Cannings tree) of arXiv:2511.09517, 'From Cannings model to Brownian motion conditioned on local time profile'] Given a population profile $q = (q(s))_{s \in \mathbb{N}_+}$ and offspring vectors $\nu^s = (\nu_1^s, \ldots, \nu_{q(s)}^s)$, we define the associated Cannings tree $\mathscr{T}$ as a subset of $\mathcal{U}$ constructed recursively as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\emptyset\in \mathscr{T}$;
    \item Suppose all elements \( u \in \mathscr{T} \) with \( |u| = s \) have been defined. Label them in lexicographic order as \( u^1 \prec u^2 \prec \cdots  \prec u^{q(s)} \). Then the elements \( u \in \mathscr{T} \) with \( |u| = s + 1 \) are defined as
    \[
    \left\{ u^i j : 1 \le i \le q(s),\, 1 \le j \le \nu^s_i \right\},
    \]
    where we set \( q(0) := 1 \) and \( \nu^0_1 := q(1) \).
\end{itemize}
The resulting set $\mathscr{T}$ is a prefix-stable subset of $\mathcal{U}$ representing a random rooted ordered tree.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.735746+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_65a89720649dcc2e39475dd833c2af1e74337df11d8510a2d2a0d104a42f32d7","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_LSI_fluct_measure_nonquad of arXiv:2503.24372, 'A criterion on the free energy for log-Sobolev inequalities in mean-field particle systems'] Let $\psi=(\varphi,\zeta)\in\bbH=\R^d\times\bbH_0$ (recall~(equation eq_def_Hilbert_space of the paper)). 
