{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.447768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66aad4786566b09b4d6d37507b08888ca5339442309fa92819593ceb7f0fbb5a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lemma: single vertex pinching cancellations of arXiv:2411.11676, 'Surface sums for lattice Yang--Mills in the large-$N$ limit'] Fix a blue face $B$ with disjoint vertices and a vertex $v$ of $B$. Then 
\begin{equation}
	w_{\infty}(B) + \sum_{B'\in \AF(B,v)}w_{\infty}(B') = 0,
\end{equation}
where if $B'$ is the pair of faces $\{B'_1,B'_2\}$, then $w_{\infty}(B'):=w_{\infty}(B'_1)\cdot w_{\infty}(B'_2)$.

As a consequence, for an embedded map $M\in \mathcal{M}(s,K)$ and a vertex $v$ on a blue face $B$ of $M$ with disjoint vertices, we have that
\begin{align}
	w_{\infty}(M) + \sum_{M'\in \AM(M,B,v)}w_{\infty}(M') = 0.
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.749276+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66b6077fd5ed6c51733f722cf39e011af792e312f6862c069b3ad315f7f5fbfc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:equivalence_nonlinear_mp_weak_sol of arXiv:2608.30493, '\bf McKean-Vlasov Differential Equations:\\ {An introduction and a focus on some kinetic m'] Let $\nu\in\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{R}^d)$, and let $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ be a probability measure on $(C_{\infty}, \mathcal{B} )$, such that
\begin{equation}
\big[(t,x)\mapsto {\bf b}(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big], \ \big[ (t,x)\mapsto {\boldsymbol\sigma} {\boldsymbol\sigma}^\top(t,x,{\boldsymbol\mu}_t)\big] \in L^{1}_{\text{loc}}([0,\infty) \times \mathbb{R}^d , \overline{\boldsymbol\mu}), 
\end{equation}

with ${\boldsymbol\mu}_t:= \mathbb{P}_{\nu}(x_t \in d y)$ and $\overline{\boldsymbol\mu}$ as in Notation (not:measure_dt_dx of the paper). 

Then $\mathbb{P}_{\nu}$ is a solution to NL-MP$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$ if and only if there exists a (weak) solution $X$ to MKV$({\bf b},{\boldsymbol\sigma};\nu)$ such that $[X] =\mathbb{P}_{\nu} $.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.417324+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66bcd32088862124e8912d0bd539a9a793dea0e3cf4330a9142b9c3194ededb2","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 103 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Take two variational data satisfying Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A_prime} and \ref{Assumption_B}, differing by their matrices of interactions $\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{\textnormal{I}}$ and $\boldsymbol{\Theta}^{\textnormal{II}}$, by their segment endpoints $(\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}},\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}})_{h=1}^{H}$ and $(\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}},\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}})_{h=1}^H$, by their potentials $(V^{\textnormal{I}}_h)_{h=1}^H$ and $(V^{\textnormal{II}}_h)_{h=1}^H$, by the right-hand sides $(r_e^{\textnormal{I}})_{e=1}^{\mathfrak{e}}$ and $(r_e^{\textnormal{II}})_{e=1}^{\mathfrak{e}}$ of the affine constraints \eqref{eq_equations_eqs}. There exists $M > 0$ depending only on the constants in the assumptions, such that for any measurable function $f$ with finite $|\!|f|\!|_{\frac{1}{2}}$ and $|\!|f|\!|_{\infty}$ we have
\begin{equation}

 \begin{split}
&  \bigg| \int_{\amsmathbb R} f(x)\dd(\mu^{\textnormal{I}}-\mu^{\textnormal{II}})(x)\bigg| \leq M \cdot \big(|\!|f|\!|_{\frac{1}{2}}+|\!|f|\!|_{\infty}\big)  \\
& \quad \times \Biggl[ \sum_{e=1}^{\mathfrak e} \bigl|r_e^{\textnormal{I}}-r_e^{\textnormal{II}}\bigr| + \sum_{g,h=1}^H \bigl|\theta^{\textnormal{I}}_{g,h}- \theta^{\textnormal{II}}_{g,h}\bigr| + \sum_{h=1}^H \Big(\bigl|\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}}-\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}}\bigr|+ \bigl|\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}}-\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}}\bigr| + \sup_{x} \bigl|V_h^{\textnormal{I}}(x) - V_{h}^{\textnormal{II}}(x)\bigr|\Big)\Biggr]^{\frac{1}{2}},
 \end{split}
 \end{equation}
 where the supremum ranges over $x \in [\max(\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}},\hat{a}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}}),\min(\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{I}},\hat{b}_{h}^{\prime\,\textnormal{II}})]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:32:15.351339+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_66c6c8e938ac9ff62daad05542ff06eccda72a7caf0809f68e62439e135ee988","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 18 of arXiv:2506.15736, 'Coming down from infinity for coordinated particle systems'] Suppose $(X(t))_{t \geq 0}$ is a $\Lambda$-coalescent and $(\widehat{X}(t))_{t \geq 0}$ is a $4\Lambda([0,1])\delta_0$-coalescent, both of which are started with infinitely many particles. Then there exists a random time $t^* > 0$ a.s. such that for all $s \leq t^*$,

