    u(\hat{t}, b_{\hat{t}}, \h\theta; t, \lambda) \leq C(d) \lambda A^2 (\log \hat{t})^{d/2} \, \hat{t}^{-A^2}.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.595920+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67370ebbcf827fdd9449f83ba81d58886b8d9139258aa314708c5a82d01565d0","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:scaleFunction of arXiv:2412.05030, 'Characterization of Subordinate Symmetric Markov Processes'] For constants $0 < \alpha_1 \le \alpha_2$, 
  we say a continuous function $\psi : [0,\infty) \to [0,\infty)$ satisfies $LU(\alpha_1,\alpha_2)$
  if $\psi$ is strictly increasing, $\psi(0) = 0$, $\psi(1) = 1$  and 
  there exists constants $C \ge 1$ such that
  \begin{align}
    C^{-1} \left(\frac{R}{r}\right)^{\alpha_1} \le \frac{\psi(R)}{\psi(r)} \le C \left(\frac{R}{r}\right)^{\alpha_2}, \qquad 0 < r \le R.
  \end{align}
  We call such function $\psi$ a scale function.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:14:57.707985+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_673a6d43b9c9c6c9917a0727ffc81db17d0eba93fb16d5c105355552cec83062","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop Kac-Rice Formula of arXiv:2605.29095, 'Asymptotics of the number of components of random polynomial lemniscates'] For the random polynomials $P_n$, we have almost surely 
    \begin{equation}
