{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:49:05.433866+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67c4f8b7a8359357d143a94f0fb161ea118a5f53821b4b1ad2dcfe4c77a088df","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Lemma on Steklov of arXiv:2601.09990, 'Remarks on the convex integration technique applied to singular stochastic partial differe'] Let $I \subset \mathbb{R}$ be any interval, $E$ any measurable set over $\mathbb{T}^{d}$, $1 \leq q, r \leq \infty$ and $h > 0$. Given $v \in L^{r} (I;  L^{q}(E))$, we define a Steklov average by 
\begin{align*}
v_{h} (t, \cdot) \triangleq \frac{1}{h} \int_{t-h}^{t} \tilde{v} (s, \cdot) ds \hspace{2mm} \text{ where } \hspace{2mm}  \tilde{v}(t, \cdot) \triangleq 
\begin{cases}
v(t, \cdot) &\text{ if } t \in I, \\
0 & \text{ if } t \in \mathbb{R} \setminus I.
\end{cases}
\end{align*}
Then 
\begin{enumerate}
\item (e.g. [BD25]) $\lVert v_{h} \rVert_{L^{q}(I; L^{q}(E))} \leq \lVert v \rVert_{L^{q}(I; L^{q}(E))}$ and $(\partial_{t} v_{h})(t,x) = \frac{\tilde{v}(t,x) - \tilde{v}(t-h,x)}{h}$. 
\item (e.g. [CDG17]) $v_{h} \in C(I; L^{q}(E)) \cap L^{\infty} (I; L^{q}(E))$. 
\item (e.g. [CDG17]) $v_{h} \to v$ in $L^{r} (I; L^{q}(E))$ as $h\searrow 0$. 
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:42:08.413487+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_67caba2fb808f89ab1081b80fb1e6abddd25ca40b3233d47453dcb198a9ea8b8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma Th+i of arXiv:2511.15893, 'On a Class of Dynamical Poisson-Voronoi Tessellations'] Let $\theta\in [0,\pi]$ and $\gamma\geq 0$. Define $\rho_{i}(\theta)^2:=v_i^2\cot^2\theta+1$, for $i=1,2$. For each $i\in \{1,2\}$, $\Theta^+_{0, i}$ and $\hat{H}_{0,i}^2$ we have 

