\noindent We will always work with values of $i$ for which $q_i>0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:10.192792+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69157b69ea6164c74396daabedd83004c44417a1764fcfb21b3c64129db3bd9f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 3 of arXiv:2605.03851, 'The Relay Random Tree:\\A stochastic geometry approach of multihop relay in an urban visib'] \textbf{Shade.}

Let $\landsc$ be a landscape, $(x_1, y_1)$ a point of $\Half$, and $(a, b)\in \landsc$ a building. We call the \textit{shade} of $(a, b)$ from $(x_1, y_1)$ the set of points right to $a$ that form a lower angle with $(x_1, y_1)$ than $(a, b)$ does:
\[S^{(x_1, y_1)}_{(a, b)} = \left\{ (x, y)\in \Half:x \geq a,\  \frac{y-y_1}{x-x_1}\leq \frac{b-y_1}{a-x_1}\right\}.\]","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.346127+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_691dda80cbb32897a7f4d0243d15fa2d6e71468da299cd67bd0fbf2bd0be4b25","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] We call an $ H $-valued, $ \mathbf{F} $-adapted, continuous stochastic process $ X(\cdot) $ a mild solution to (eqSEE of the paper) if   $F(\cdot, X(\cdot)) \in L^1(0, T ; H)$ a.s., $\widetilde{F}(\cdot, X(\cdot)) \in L_{\mathbb{F}}^{2}(0, T; H)$, and for any $t \in[0, T]$,
    \begin{align*}
    X(t)\!=\!S(t) X_0\!+\!\int_0^t\! S(t\!-\!s) F(s, X(s)) d s\!+\!\int_0^t\! S(t\!-\!s) \widetilde{F}(s, X(s)) d W(s),  \;\,  \text { a.s. }
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.813825+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69260b8a79a60e645aa57cdcea7397f95e6d107f23f8a9ed03465f7e7974b5d4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:sample_Gn of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Consider graphs $G_{i,j}$ defined as follows. If $(i,j)$ is $\rm BAD$, we simply let $G_{i,j}$ be a uniform random simple graph with degree sequence $\mathbf d_i(n)$ if $i=j$ and with degree sequence $\mathbf d_{(i,j)}(n)$ if $i \neq j$. If $(i,j)$ is $\rm GOOD$, we let $G_{i,j}$ be $CM_n^{i,j}$. Recall $G_n$ is a uniform multipartite random graph with given degree sequences, satisfying Assumptions (assump:part_i_size of the paper), (assump:degree_regularity of the paper), (assump:one_good_deg_greater_2 of the paper) and (assump:offspring_degree_reg of the paper). The following statements are true:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The random graph $\mathbb G_n:=\mathcal G(G_{i,j}:i,j\in [k])$ conditionally on the event $\{\mathbb G_n \textrm{ is simple}\}$ has the same law as $G_n$.
        \item Moreover,
        \begin{align*}
            \liminf_{n \to \infty}\Prob{\mathbb G_n \textrm{ is simple}}=\left(\prod_{\substack{i\in [k]:\\(i,i)\textrm{ is GOOD}}}e^{-\phi_i/2-(\phi_i)^2/4}\right)\left(\prod_{\substack{i\neq j \in [k]:\\(i,j)\textrm{ is GOOD }}}e^{-\phi_{i,j}} \right)>0.
        \end{align*}
        Thus, for any subset $A_n$ of simple graphs, $\mathbb{P}\left(G_n\in A_n\right)=o(1)$ if and only if $\mathbb{P}\left(\mathbb G_n \in A_n\right)=o(1)$.  
    \end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:24:47.663054+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6927fff262140bc1a64dd95140458f921cfe32364f558c6fde90ba0c189c6a6d","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 19 of arXiv:2605.09372, 'Sharp weighted norm estimates for martingale square functions'] Let $1<r<p<\infty$. Given a scalar weight $w\in A_p$, we have
