{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:07:17.286615+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_698eb663a87b94cc06710d5bd4d85af299541437f9a4026aa39fb440198ad2f7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:rwre of arXiv:2603.22652, 'A cohesive account on the ergodic behaviours and scaling limits of Random Walks in Cooling'] Let $\omega$ be sampled from $\mu$. The \emph{random walk in random environment}
(RWRE) is the Markov chain $Z=(Z_n)_{n\in{\mathbb {N}}_0}$ on ${\mathbb {Z}}$ with transition
kernel
\begin{equation}
P^{\omega}(Z_{n+1}=x+e\mid Z_n=x)=
\begin{cases}
\omega(x), & e=+1,\\
1-\omega(x), & e=-1,
\end{cases}
\qquad x\in{\mathbb {Z}},\ n\in{\mathbb {N}}_0.
\end{equation}
We write $P_x^{\omega}$ for the \emph{quenched} law of $Z$ started at $x$ and
\begin{equation}
P_x^{\mu}(\,\cdot\,):=\int_{(0,1)^{{\mathbb {Z}}}} P_x^{\omega}(\,\cdot\,)\,\mu(d\omega)
\end{equation}
for the corresponding \emph{annealed} law (with expectations $E_x^{\omega}$ and
$E_x^{\mu}$).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:47:21.610796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_699650c26766999bbdfbf692249eb8a4e5fb47e607141b3636d21723571dbe33","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:puremixedstoppingtimes of arXiv:2412.09087, 'General Markovian randomized equilibrium existence and construction in zero-sum Dynkin gam'] The stopping time in (equation def:admissST-eq of the paper) is said to be a Markovian randomized stopping time if $\Psi^{(i)}$ is on the form
\begin{align}
\Psi^{(i)}_t= A^{(i)}_t  +  \infty \mathbb{I}\{\tau^{D_i} \leq t \}, \enskip t  \geq 0, 
\end{align} 
where $(A_t^{(i)})_{t \geq 0}$ is a continuous additive functional of $X$ (see e.g., [revuz2013continuous]) and
\begin{align*}
\tau^{D_i}:= \inf \{t \geq 0:X_t \in  D_i\},
\end{align*}
where $D_i \subseteq {\cal I}$ is a measurable (stopping) set. 

The stopping time in (equation def:admissST-eq of the paper) is said to be a Markovian pure stopping time if it is on the form of a first entry time, i.e., in case $A^{(i)}_t = 0,t \geq 0$ so that $\tau_{\Psi^{(i)}} = \tau^{D_i}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T02:16:53.674325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_699a6364929501886775bf65a1269c4580f0b31be6e5d0d8713848431b9d7473","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem lem10 of arXiv:2412.05521, 'Random attractors for the stochastic Nernst-Planck-Navier-Stokes system with multiplicativ'] There exist two random variables  $R_4(\omega)$ and $R_5(\omega)$ depending on the initial data, $\varepsilon_0$, $\nu$, $D$, $f$ and universal constants, such that for every $E > 0$, there exists $t(\omega,E) \leq -1$, such that for all $t_0 \leq t(\omega,E)$ and $x_0=(u_0,\rho_0,\sigma_0) \in {H}^{1}$  with $|x_0| < E$, we have
		\begin{align}
			\|\nabla v^\varepsilon(0,\omega,t_0,z(t_0,\omega)u_0)\|_{L^2} \leq  R_4(\omega),~for~all~t \in [-1,0],
		\end{align}
		\begin{align}
			\|\nabla \sigma(0,\omega,t_0,\sigma_0)\|_{L^2} \leq R_5(\omega),~for~all~t \in [-1,0],
