{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:12:08.996325+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_699edcba160f49a7b4904b9c6f0e6e4bc320543534e2e81860aeb239b90c0542","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defi def_condNegSemiDef of arXiv:2608.17463, 'Persistence probabilities of fractional L\'evy fields indexed by hyperbolic space and othe'] Let $A$ be a set and let $f:A\times A\to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}$ be a symmetric function. Then it is called conditionally negative definite, if for any $n\in\mathbb{N}$, any $\lambda_1, \ldots, \lambda_n \in\mathbb{R}$ with $\lambda_1 + \ldots + \lambda_n=0$ and any $x_1, \ldots, x_n \in A$ it holds that
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{i,j=1}^n \lambda_i \lambda_j f(x_i, x_j) \leq 0.
    \end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:31:20.614523+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69a0c733a31004411142404d24eada5dce7a2b21784265bea52f96046ac1614a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 12 of arXiv:2608.25785, 'Proper Actions on Homogeneous Spaces of the Upper Triangular Matrix Group via Coarse Geome'] Let $S$ be a set.
    A family $\mathcal{B}\subset \mathcal{P}(S)$ of subsets of $S$ is called a \emph{bornology} on $S$ if it satisfies the following three conditions. 
    \begin{enumerate} 
        \item $\mathcal{B}$ is a cover of $S$ (i.e.~ $\bigcup_{B\in \mathcal{B}}B=S$).
        \item $B_1 ,B_2\in \mathcal{B}$ implies $B_1\cup B_2\in \mathcal{B}$. 
        \item $B\in \mathcal{B}$ and $B'\subset B$ imply $B'\in \mathcal{B}$. 
    \end{enumerate}
    The pair $(S,\mathcal{B})$ is called a \emph{bornological space}.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:50.733613+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_69aff35b9cc5395298ee10750d7d4860014c323d91237db8ba0b086b3f1298a4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop_T_c_convexity_GHS of arXiv:2503.24372, 'A criterion on the free energy for log-Sobolev inequalities in mean-field particle systems'] Suppose Assumption~(ass_doublewell of the paper) applies. 
