\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.061673+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6acbacdd3c6112e65f93c9371d5a28f43da3567ad424166892f5ee611ce44b18","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:Bernoulli-split of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $\Omega = \{0, 1\}^{E \times \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket} \times \{0, 1\}^{\mathbb Z^d \times \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket}$. We define, for $e \in E$ and $l \in \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket$,
  \begin{equation}
    Y_{e, l}: (y, z) \in \Omega \mapsto y(e, l) \in \{0, 1\}
  \end{equation}
  and, for $x \in \mathbb Z^d$ and $k \in \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$,
  \begin{equation}
    Z_{x, k}: (y, z) \in \Omega \mapsto z(x, k) \in \{0, 1\}.
  \end{equation}
  We call these Bernoulli variables base variables. We define for $l \in \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket$ the function
  \begin{equation}
    p_l: \beta \in \mathbb R_+^* \mapsto 1 - \frac {1 - h_l(\beta)} {1 - h_{l-1}(\beta)} \in [0, 1].
  \end{equation}
  Let $\mathbb P$ be the product probability measure on $\Omega$ such that
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(Y_{e, l} = 1) = p_l(\beta) \text{ for all $e \in E$,$l \in \llbracket 1, M \rrbracket$} 
  \end{equation}
  and
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(Z_{x, k} = 1) = \frac {q_\nu(\llbracket k, N \rrbracket)} {q_\nu(\llbracket k-1, N \rrbracket)}
    \text{ for all $x \in \mathbb Z^d$, $k \in \llbracket 1, N \rrbracket$.}
  \end{equation}
  We define
  \begin{equation}
    \nu_x = \min \{n \in \llbracket 0, N \rrbracket : \forall 1 \leqslant k \leqslant n, Z_{x, k} = 1 \}
  \end{equation}
  and we say that an edge $e = \{x, y\}$ is open if
  \begin{equation}
    \max\limits_{1 \leqslant l \leqslant \zeta(\nu_x, \nu_y)} Y_{e, l} = 1.
  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

