Then, for every $1 \leq i \leq  n$, define $I_i^{(M)}(\mathbf{x}) =I^{(M)}_{1}(x_i, x_{i+1}, \ldots, x_n, x_1, \ldots, x_{i-1})$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:02:38.605839+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6af776ded7502ef95f861ce42f6757f640c91ef5a9af8db4f68b211bdf1920dc","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 8 of arXiv:2607.08129, '2607.08129'] For every $f\in C[0,1]$,
\begin{equation}
 I_\theta(f)
 =\sup_{\mu\in\cM_{\mathrm{at}}[0,1]}
 \bigg\{
 \int_{[0,1]}f(t)\mu(\dd t)-\mathfrak L_\theta(\mu)
 \bigg\}.
\end{equation}
Equivalently,
\begin{align}
 I_\theta(f)
 =\sup_{m,\boldsymbol t,\lambda}
 \bigg\{
 &\sum_{j=1}^m\lambda_jf(t_j)
 \nonumber\\
 &-\int_0^1\log\bigg[
 q^{m(x)}+
 \sum_{k=1}^{m(x)}\pi_k
 \exp\bigg\{\sum_{j=1}^m
 \lambda_j\1_{\{xk\le t_j\}}\bigg\}
 \bigg]\dd x
 \bigg\},

\end{align}
where the supremum is over $m\ge1$, $0\le t_1<\cdots<t_m\le1$, and $\lambda\in\R^m$.

For a fixed atomic measure $\mu$, the pointwise optimizer in the finite-state Gibbs variational formula used in \eqref{eq:finite-gibbs} is the reduced kernel
\begin{equation}
 a_k^\mu(x)
 =\frac{\pi_k e^{\mu([xk,1])}}{Z_\mu(x)},
 \quad 1\le k\le m(x),
 \qquad
 a_0^\mu(x)=\frac{q^{m(x)}}{Z_\mu(x)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:06:39.354439+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6af7d85373473aea16896fd1479e48b533b0b8c55d168c94ea34a4fca1a0c110","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma l:sharp_poincare of arXiv:2607.15140, 'On the absence of blow-up in the 3D Navier-Stokes equations with transport noise'] For all $\sigma\in [0,1/2)$ and balls $B\subseteq {\mathbb R}^d$, there exists a constant $C>0$ independent of the center of $B$ such that, for all $f\in L^2(B)$ and $\nabla f \in H^{-1-\sigma}(B)$, 
\begin{align}

\Big\|f-\fint_{B} f\Big\|_{H^{-\sigma}(B)}
\leq C \|\nabla f\|_{H^{-1-\sigma}(B)}.
\end{align}
In particular, $
\big\|f-\fint_{B} f\big\|_{H^{-1}(B)}
\leq C \|\nabla f\|_{H^{-1}(B)}$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

