    \end{equation}
    Then, $\ds \operatorname*{pliminf}_{n \to +\infty} ov(\hat{\pi}, \pi^*) > 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.874399+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b06c182e6f11f50890b4c490d4572ec60c660ce5746f7d07998c01a77f6f104","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:main Birk of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},V,T)$ be a capacity preserving system, where $V$ is an  upper probability.  Then $V$ is ergodic with respect to $T$ if and only if there exists a unique $Q\in \mathcal{M}^e(T)\cap \operatorname{core}(V)$ such that for any  $f\in L^1(\Omega,\mathcal{F},Q)$,
	\begin{equation}
		\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=0}^{n-1} f(T^{i} \omega)=\int f dQ \text{ for }V\text{-a.s. }\omega\in\Omega.
	\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:26:26.735567+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6b078ba4b5bbb93b18c8638b7a56dee8390eb769e572e6efd5ad1fc4e0888136","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary cor:Push-TASEP-scalar of arXiv:2608.02772, 'Non-Abelian Hirota-Miwa Equations for the KPZ Universality Class'] For $t > 0$ and $a < y_{n+1} - 1$, the one-point Fredholm
