{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:36:16.061225+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c387b50cb562b102f9122ada3dfec2a41b192640355cd420a73955d4e3aa18f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem: Martingale of arXiv:2606.28031, 'Insurance risk models in a heterogeneous time-dependent population: scaling limits and rui'] Assume there exists $\overline{\alpha}>0$ such that both $\mathbb{E}[{\mathrm e}^{-\overline{\alpha} X(1)}]$ and $\mathbb{E}[{\mathrm e}^{-\overline{\alpha} Y(1)}]$ are finite. Then, for any $\alpha \in [0,\overline{\alpha}]$, the process $M_\alpha$ defined by
\[
M_\alpha(t) := \exp{{\left(- \alpha V(t) - k_\alpha(t)\right)}}
\] 
is a càdlàg $\PAR{\mathcal{F}_t}_{t\geqslant 0}$-martingale, with $M_\alpha(0)=1$. It is also an $\PAR{\mathcal{F}_t \vee \mathcal{F}^F}_{t\geqslant 0}$-martingale.

Moreover, if $\tau$ is a bounded $\PAR{\mathcal{F}_t \vee \mathcal{F}^F}_{t\geqslant 0}$-stopping time, then
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}\big[M_\alpha(\tau)\,\vert\, \mathcal{F}^F\big] = 1, \quad \text{almost surely.}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:41:29.055796+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c46a92bb89522e13eb4c3cea4bdaf95818cc282fd51de204513a95805a22e66","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 26 of arXiv:2608.26619, 'Uniqueness of Positively Curved Ancient Ricci Flows on Surfaces with Boundary'] If $v:\old\times(-\infty,0)\rightarrow\bR$ satisfies \eqref{eq_symmetry_v}  with $v(\cdot,t)\xrightarrow{C^\infty(\mathbb{D})} 0$ as $t\rightarrow -\infty$, and we define $\phi(x,t)=\frac{v(x,t)-v_R(x,t)}{x_1}$ as in Lemma \ref{lem_neumann}, then there exists some $T<0$ and $\gamma>0$ such that for any $-\infty<s<t\leq T$ we have
\begin{equation}
\|\phi(\cdot,t)\|_{L^\infty(\old)}\leq \gamma\left(\int_s^t \|v(\cdot,\tau)\|_{C^2(\old)}~d\tau\right)\|\phi(\cdot,s)\|_{L^\infty(\old)}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.244697+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c47cb430cc17028643aa57f940314d52d3debaca2e755ee19ff89576ada31c9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:gumbel of arXiv:2607.01463, 'Two Multi--Draw Coupon Collector models with different retention rules'] A collector receives at each run $d$ distinct coupons ($d$ is a positive integer) and keeps \textbf{all the new} observed coupons (\textit{Problem I}), while he chooses \textbf{the least--collected coupon} at each run (\textit{Problem II}). Let $T_{N,d}$ and $T_{N,1}$ be the random variables denoting, respectively, the number of trials for a complete set of $N$ different types of coupons, which are uniformly distributed. Then, as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{
        T_{N,d}
        -
        \frac{N}{d}\log N
    }{
        \frac{N}{d}
    }
  \Longrightarrow
    G, 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \frac{
        T_{N,1}
        -
        \frac{N}{d}\log N
        -
        N C_d
    }{
        \frac{N}{d}
    }
    \Longrightarrow
    G,

\end{equation}
where
\[
    C_d
    =
    \int_0^1
    \left[
        \frac{1}{1-(1-x)^d}
        -
        \frac{1}{dx}
    \right]\,dx,
\]
and $G$ is the standard Gumbel random variable, with distribution function
\[
    \mathbb{P}(G\le x)=\exp\{-e^{-x}\},
    \qquad x\in\mathbb{R}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:01:22.599406+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6c4a49ae8148db35138fe04543fd392427f1d2f6b27e072546ff38e1b33876e9","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:v<L(B_t|H) of arXiv:2606.10257, 'Downward conditional monotonicity gives survival and extinction for contact processes in r'] Assume that $T$ is irreducible, monotone, that $\alpha$ is increasing, and that the MMPP is DCM. Given any $t>0$ and any history of arrivals $\mathcal{H}_{t}$, $v^*\preceq\mathcal L(B_0)$ implies that 
	\begin{equation*}
		v^*\preceq\mathcal L(B_t\mid\mathcal{H}_{t} ).
