{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.829179+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_6e28308e7ee450b11b20f83b7624f40e8158b0ae49ab78382e9179e1494037d7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn propchaos of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] A sequence of random vectors ${\boldsymbol Y}^N=(Y^1,\dots,Y^N)$ in ${\mathscr Y}^N$ is said to be \emph{chaotic with law $\mu\in{\mathscr{P}}({\mathscr Y})$} if for every $k\in\mathbb N$ fixed, the $k$-marginals converge in law:
