{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:57:30.963145+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_707f90d80867561f350b830eaede2543b82f8755e819dcab7fe7172e1531c08c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 21 of arXiv:2608.12291, 'When should one stop the most exciting game?\\ {\tiny Sequential Inference for win-marting'] Let $T<\infty$. For every $t\in[0,T]$ the map $x\mapsto W_T(t,x)$ is $\llip{(0,1)}$. Specifically, for the optimal stopping time $\tau^* :=\tau^*_T(t,x)$, 
