holds for all $x\in\R_+$ and all $\th\in (0,1)$. Denote by ${\mathcal V}$ the class of all completely subscalable  distribution functions.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:42:47.764030+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70b57e4defef476e0995f1ba1ad53b8fc81ad709809ae94627b45b8bb47b1de8","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 14 of arXiv:2604.12334, 'On additive averaging kernels for finite Markov chains'] For any subset $S \in \mathcal{D}$ and $P \in \mathcal{L}(\pi)$, 
    \begin{align*}
        \|A_\alpha(S) - \Pi\|_{F,\pi}^2 &= \alpha^2\Tr(P^2) - 2\alpha(1-\alpha)g(S) + 1 - 2\alpha^2\\
