Then $\{X^{(n)}\}$ is exponentially tight in $D([0,T])$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:23:03.041343+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_70dbf42a9f597c320d27d7f816ccc7b5731a560a9bff8b4c451ef7188036cd47","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corollary 44 of arXiv:2605.03236, 'Stochastic It\^o Equations and Parabolic Second-Order Equations with singular Drift'] For  $\mu \in[0,1]$,   stopping time $\tau$,  and any $\rho\in(0,\rho_{b}]$  we have
