{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:11:02.370096+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7340b2dd62dfd5d72ed52bf42126fa6a749ce91cf7be28f26ffb7099be747182","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition max2 of arXiv:2507.10294, 'Guessing Strategies for Shelf-Shuffling Machines'] Let $n\geq 5$. Then, $I_2^*=I_{n-1}^*=\{2,3\}$, i.e. the player should guess card $2$ or card $3$ in positions $2$ and $n-1$.
    \begin{proof}
        Note that the $n=2$ case was covered in the proof of Proposition $(first card of the paper)$. One verifies that when $n=3$, $p_{2,2}=p_{3,2}=1/2$. When $n=4$, we have $p_{3,2}=p_{4,2}=p_{3,3}=p_{4,3}=3/8$, and Proposition $(doubly stochastic of the paper)$ implies that the player should guess card $3$ or $4$ in position $2$ and card $3$ or $4$ in position $3$.
        We will show that $p_{2,2}$ and $p_{3,2}$ are the unique maxima of column $2$. The column $n-1$ case will follow by Proposition $(guessing strategy symmetry of the paper)$. We have, for any $i\leq n$, $p_{i,2}=\frac{i-1+\binom{i-1}{n-2}}{2^i}$. The binomial coefficient vanishes when $i-1<n-2$, i.e. when $i\leq n-2$. For the other two cases, we have $p_{n-1,2}=\frac{n-1}{2^{n-1}}=p_{n,2}$ using Lemma $(last two rows are equal of the paper)$. So the problem has been reduced to finding
        \begin{equation}
        \max_{1\leq i\leq n-2}\frac{i-1}{2^i}
        \end{equation}
        and comparing this value with $p_{n,2}=\frac{n}{2^{n}}$. Some calculus shows that the maximum in equation $(max2 of the paper)$ is $1/4$ and is achieved only at $i=2$ and $i=3$. To compare this with $p_{n,2}$, we note that by the same calculus used above, for $n\geq 2$, the sequence $p_{n,2}=\frac{n}{2^n}$ is strictly decreasing with $p_{4,2}=1/4$. Hence, $p_{n,2}<1/4$ for all $n\geq 5$ and thus the maxima are unique.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:47:22.257979+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73456c86c9609a4806193c709a384e03137730be6a79d1e24c6d78b75b86f1e1","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 120 (Complex slope) of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] For any $u \in \mathbb{C}$, define 
		\begin{flalign}

                  	f_0 (u) = \displaystyle\frac{(\mathfrak{q}^{u-b - c} - 1) (\mathfrak{q}^{u} - 1)}{(\mathfrak{q}^{u+a} - 1) (\mathfrak{q}^{u - b} - 1)}; \qquad U(u) = \mathfrak{q}^{u} \cdot \displaystyle\frac{f_0 (u)}{1 - f_0 (u)}; \qquad V(u) = \displaystyle\frac{\mathfrak{q}^{u}}{1 - f_0 (u)}. 
		\end{flalign} 

		\noindent For any $(x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2$, define $u = u(x,y) = u_q (x, y)$ to be the unique solution in $\overline{\mathbb{H}}$ to the equation
		\begin{flalign}

                  	\mathfrak{q}^y =  V(u) - \mathfrak{q}^x \cdot U(u),
		\end{flalign}
		whenever \eqref{equationu}, which is quickly verified to be a quadratic equation in $\mathfrak q^u$, has at most one real root. In this case, define the \emph{complex slope} $f_x(y):=f (x, y) $ by
		\begin{flalign}

			f (x,y) = \displaystyle\frac{\mathfrak{q}^{u} - \mathfrak{q}^{y}}{\mathfrak{q}^{u} - \mathfrak{q}^{y-x}}.
		\end{flalign}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-09-07T12:18:08.278392+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7345c4b6d6feb744dc1104586a8616815a2d1a6046a665e03f064dd07f981513","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 of arXiv:2505.20799, 'A note on the improved sparse Hanson-Wright inequalities'] Assume that $\{\delta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ and $\{\zeta_{i}, 1\le i\le n \}$ are two independent sequences of random variables, where $\delta_{i}\sim\textnormal{Bernoulli}(p_{i})$ are independent and $\zeta_{i}$ are independent centered $\alpha$-sub-exponential random variables.
	Consider a random vector $\xi=(\xi_{1}, \cdots, \xi_{n})$  with $\xi_{i}=\delta_{i}\cdot\zeta_{i}$. Let $A=(a_{ij})_{n\times n}$ be a symmetric fixed matrix. Then for $0<\alpha\le 2$ and $t\ge 0$
	\begin{align}
		&\mathbb{P}\{\vert \xi^\top A\xi-\mathbb{E}\xi^\top A\xi\vert\ge L(\alpha)^{2}t  \}\nonumber\\
		\le& 2\exp\left(-c(\alpha)\min\left\{ \frac{t^{2}}{\sum_{k}a_{kk}^{2}p_{k}+\sum_{i\neq j}a_{ij}^{2}p_{i}p_{j}}, \left(\frac{t}{\Vert A\Vert_{l_{2}\to l_{2}}}\right)^{\alpha/2}  \right\}      \right),\nonumber
	\end{align}
where $L(\alpha)=\max_{i}\Vert \zeta_{i}\Vert_{\Psi_{\alpha}}$ and $c(\alpha)$ is a positive constant depending only on $\alpha$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:30:14.229090+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_734baaa05ac5bd03dc865cd4902422632a15cdd8ef90083a9a6b2da097d75c58","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma gap_convergence of arXiv:2608.23202, 'Locally finite Fixed points of Branching Brownian Motion'] For any fixed $\delta >0$ and $f$ of the form (Form of f of the paper) with $supp (f) \subset [-K_f, \infty), $ for some $K_f>0$, uniformly in $x\in[-(\mu -\lambda)t-\delta\sqrt{t},-(\mu -\lambda)t+\delta\sqrt{t}]$, we have
\begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
    &\lim_{t\to\infty}\mathbb{E}[1-e^{-\sum_{i = 1}^{n(t)} f(x_{i}+\chi_{k}^{i}(t) - \lambda t)}|x+M_{t} -\lambda t\ge -K_{f}]\\
    & = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\mu \mathbb{E}[1-e^{-\int f(y+z)\mathcal{D}^{\mu}(dz) }]e^{-\mu y}e^{-\mu K_{f}}dy. 
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:52:47.255297+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_73524c9e474601ef6d9300fddebd6f182d18b485145ff5371304d8c53a6a2a81","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 8 of arXiv:2609.01101, '2609.01101'] There is $A_0\ge1$ such that, uniformly for
\begin{equation*}
1\le\lambda\le\tfrac12\log T
\qquad\hbox{and}\qquad |b_i|\le T/2,
\end{equation*}
one has
\begin{equation}
(2+\lambda)^{-A_0}B(T,\mathbf b,\mathbf a)
