{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:02:08.131901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_74c3d5323fb581f61fe5ad684ed2bf663ba01402241cd3c6dabdc2d31ab86d01","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (Wasserstein Distance, \cite{peyre2019computational}) of arXiv:2608.27500, 'Optimal Transport for Network Comparison: \\ A Review with Machine Learning Applications'] Let $\mu=\sum_{i=1}^n a_i\delta_{x_i}$ and $\nu=\sum_{j=1}^m b_j\delta_{y_j}$ be probability measures in $(\mathbb{R}^d,\|\cdot\|)$. For $1\leq p<\infty$, the $p$-Wasserstein distance between $\mu$ and $\nu$ is defined as
