{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.498730+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_76ade1004838a23960d95f43d9c68cb259f6f446aac465ea404fcacaa554fe50","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defn radial link pattern of arXiv:2505.14762, 'Multiple radial SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Sutherland system'] Given $\boldsymbol{z}=\{z_1,z_2,...,z_n\}$ on the unit circle, a radial link pattern is a homotopically equivalent class of non-intersecting curves connecting pair of boundary points (links/arcs) or connecting boundary points and the origin (rays).

The link pattern in the unit disk with one interior point is known as the standard module $W_{n,m}$ over the affine Temperley-Lieb algebra $aTL_n$. The link patterns with $n$ boundary points and $m$ links are called $(n,m)$-links, denoted by ${\rm LP}(n,m)$. 

The number of radial $(n,m)$-links is given by $|{\rm LP}(n,m)|=C_{n}^{m}$.","status":"unverified"}

