{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:43:55.605733+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a95f77c6dfe52a6f797c943d8759df0cc91658fd333b2cd7c1eddf813c22f3b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theoremi thm_main_undirected of arXiv:2604.04672, 'Connected components and topological ends of stationary planar forests'] With probability one, every connected component of $G$ is a tree with at most $2$ topological ends. Let $N_0, N_1$ and $N_2$ respectively denote the number of finite, one-ended and two-ended connected components of $G$, taking values in $\mathbb{Z}_+\cup\{\infty\}$. Then, $(N_0,N_1,N_2)$ is constant on a full-probability event, and the following holds almost surely:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $N_0\in\{0,\infty\}$,
            \item $N_1\leq 2$, and
            \item $N_1=2\implies N_2<\infty$.
        \end{itemize}
    Moreover, this classification is optimal: every value for $(N_0,N_1,N_2)$ satisfying the above constraints is realized by some model on which the result applies. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.826208+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7a998fca4f89941591d7be7d628e048b1d2d3aaaf0a8c4d9d36cc5345d738e4c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm Informal statement of \autoref{lem:conv_muN_detailed} of arXiv:2606.24385, 'Gradient Mean-Field Dynamics with Measure-Valued States: Well-Posedness, Chaos, and Long-T'] Let $\mu^N(t)$ denote the empirical measure of the particle system and let $\mu(t)$ be the unique solution of the McKean--Vlasov equation. Then, as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{align}
\sup_{t\in[0,T]} \mathbb{E}\big[ {\mathcal W}_1(\mu^N(t),\mu(t)) \big]
\longrightarrow 0.
\end{align}
In particular, the particle system is chaotic with limit law $\mu(t)$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

