{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:34:33.539803+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d880b82022cb7ea1fe47d48f650f97fcd8889914caecc82a6bf370c70ac62e7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 2 of arXiv:2603.28734, 'Free energy analyticity of the disordered XY model and Debye screening in the 2D Coulomb g'] For any $n \geq 0$ and $-t < -s < 0$, define the following coupling events.
\begin{multline*} \mathsf{C}(n,t,s) := \{ R^{\Lambda_{n}, -t, -s}(\alpha^+)_0 = R^{\Lambda_n, -t, -s}(\alpha^-)_0  \} \\ \text{ and }~\mathsf{C}(n,t,\geq s) := \bigcap_{-r \in [-s, 0]} \mathsf{C}(n,t,r), \end{multline*}
and 
\begin{multline*} \lfloor \mathsf{C}(n,t,s) \rfloor_k := \{ \lfloor R^{\Lambda_n(v), -t, -s}(\alpha^+)_0\rfloor_k = \lfloor R^{\Lambda_n(v), -t, -s}(\alpha^-)_0 \rfloor_k  \} \\ \text{ and }~\lfloor\mathsf{C}(n,t,\geq s)\rfloor_k := \bigcap_{-r \in [-s, 0]}  \lfloor\mathsf{C}(n,t,r)\rfloor_k . \end{multline*}

Finally, when $s = 0$, we remove it from the notation \emph{e.g.} we write:
\[  \mathsf{C}(n,t,0) := \mathsf{C}(n,t).  \]

Observe that those events are the ones appearing in Theorem~\ref{thm:cftp}, which explains their name of ``coupling events''.
We will also name their complementary events by replacing $\mathsf{C}$ by $\mathsf{NC}$ (``non-coupling'') for the four events just defined.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.952768+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d8bd1069938ad1da73310fd26ef3903bdd80c91efa1dcdf3377cfd69658be44","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 13 of arXiv:2607.21759, 'Marchenko--Pastur law for tensor powers of exchangeable unconditional vectors'] Let $d = o(\sqrt n)$. Assume $(X_1,\dotsc,X_n) \sim \Inv_k(R)$ for some fixed $k \in (0,\infty]$. Define \[Q(k) = \begin{cases} \frac{\Gamma(3/k)}{\Gamma(1/k)} \cdot \bigl(\frac{n}{k}\bigr)^{-2/k} & \text{if }k<\infty, \\ 1/3 & \text{if }k=\infty. \end{cases}\] Then as $n \to \infty$, if \begin{enumerate}
    \item ${Q(k)}^d \cdot \E(R^{2d})\to 1$, and 
    \item ${Q(k)}^{2d} \cdot \bigl[\E(R^{4d}) - \bigl(\E R^{2d}\bigr)^2\bigr] \to 0$, 
\end{enumerate}
then the three conditions in \cref{thm:main} are satisfied, and we have $\ESD(K_{\PrincipalT}) \wkconv \mu_{\MP(c)}$ with probability $1$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:35:05.840901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_7d8df013f9f75a7f1f1aaa1f421d76b23269df8bcf81c4e59187d0ee61446659","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:general-row of arXiv:2606.27073, 'Retention Profiles and KL Contraction Bounds in Finite Markov Chains'] Let $P$ be a Markov kernel on a finite state space $S$ with $|S|\ge2$ and
