Then $M=M_c$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:32:19.897829+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_809a455f1d6687be3cbd3fcccc0c8f3b96630089ce684294f81dbf9ea72378dd","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Teo 12 of arXiv:2608.02103, '$p$-Adic Diffusion and Random Walks on $[0,1]$'] Let \(X_t\in [0,1]\) be the strong Markov process attached to the infinitesimal generator \(D_f\) with probability transition \(p_t(x,\cdot)\). Let \(P_f\) be the operator defined on \(L^2(\mathbb{Z}_p,\mu)\) defined by equation (\ref{eq:padicoperator}). Let \(X^{*}_t:=\rho^{-1}(X_t)\) be the pull-back of \(X_t\). 
    Then the following holds
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item The attached semigroups are unitarily equivalent:
        \begin{equation*}
            e^{tD_f}=\rho^{*-1} e^{tP_f}\rho^{*}
        \end{equation*}
        \item The pull-back \(X^{*}_t\in \mathbb{Z}_p\) is a strong Markov process on \(\mathbb{Z}_p\) with probability transition \(p_t^{*}(y,\cdot)\) satisfying
        \[p_t^{*}(x,B)=p_t(\rho(x),\rho(B)),\]for any Borel set \(B\subset \mathbb{Z}_p\).
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:47:01.667941+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80b3d5c2f354f0cfaf6fb78f5f96eef7a72f08447300dd403783aa1ce502e074","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop 10 of arXiv:2604.24189, 'Malliavin calculus and densities for chaos-driven stochastic differential equations'] Let $F=\{F_t=(I_q(f_t^1),\ldots,I_q(f_t^m))\,,\;t\in \R_+\}$ be an $\R^m$-valued chaos process on an abstract Wiener space $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},\P,\mathfrak{H})$, and assume that $F$ satisfies hypothesis $\mathbf{(H_2)}$ with $H\in(\tfrac{1}{2},1)$.
	Let $R=\{R^h_{t,\varepsilon}\,;\;t\in\R,\, \varepsilon\in\R,\, h\in\HHH\}$ and $S=\{S^{h}_{t,\varepsilon}\,;\;t\in\R,\, \varepsilon\in\R,\, h\in\HHH\}$ be the $\R^m$-valued stochastic processes defined, respectively, by
 \begin{align}
	R_{t,\varepsilon}^h=F_t(\bullet + \varepsilon\,j(h)) \quad \text{ and } \quad S^{h}_{t,\varepsilon} = \sum_{k=0}^{q}  \frac{\varepsilon^k}{k!}\langle D^k F_t,h^{\otimes k} \rangle_{\mathfrak{H}^{\otimes k}}.
 \end{align}
Then the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}[(a)]
	\item $S$ is a version of $R$;
	\item for every $h\in \HHH$, almost every $\omega\in\Omega$ and every $\varepsilon\in\R$, one has $S^{h}_{\star,\varepsilon}(\omega)\in W_{2}^{1-\alpha}\left(0, T ; \mathbb{R}^{m}\right)$;
	\item for every $\beta\in(0,H-\tfrac{1}{2})$ and $1-\alpha = H-\beta$, and for almost every $\omega\in\Omega$, the map from $\R$ into $W_{2}^{1-\alpha}\left(0, T ; \mathbb{R}^{m}\right)$ defined by $\varepsilon\mapsto S^{h}_{\star,\varepsilon}(\omega)$ is of class $\mathcal{C}^{\infty}$. Moreover, for every $h\in \HHH$, almost every $\omega\in\Omega$, every $\varepsilon\in\R$ and every $\ell \in \N$,
	$$
\frac{d^\ell}{d\varepsilon^\ell} S^{h}_{\star,\varepsilon}(\omega)= \left\{
\begin{array}{lcl}
\sum_{k=l}^{q}\frac{\varepsilon^{k-l}}{k!} \left<D^kF_\star(\omega) ,h^{\otimes k}\right>_{\HHH^{\otimes k}}, & \text{if }& 0 \leq \ell \leq q\\
0,&  \text{if }& \ell >q.
\end{array}\right.
$$
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:20:07.900625+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80d18c911ec26ebe2b50d6083130cb098a2c57e1f8c2f8df537e7e6abf817049","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma NSPl1min of arXiv:2608.23765, '\texorpdfstring{Tight Majorizations and Convergence Rates\\ of Nuclear Norm Minimization I'] Assume that the measurement operator $\mathcal{A}: \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2} \longrightarrow \R^m $ satisfies the NSP of order $r$ of (def:NSP:statement of the paper) for some $\eta_r<1$. Then  for all $\mathbf{Z},  \mathbf{X}  \in \mathbb{R}^{d_1 \times d_2}$ such that $\mathcal{A} \left( \mathbf{Z} \right) =  \mathcal{A} \left(\mathbf{X} \right)  $ it holds that

	\begin{equation*}
	\nucnorm{\mathbf{Z} - \mathbf{X} }  \leq \frac{1+\eta_r}{1-\eta_r}\left(    \nucnorm{\mathbf{X}} -  \nucnorm{\mathbf{Z}}  + 2\besterrNuc{\mathbf{Z}}{r}\right).
	\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.650365+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80d9d00ff89614f490c397f24f984619be176c48e58d84ff7e4ed2bc049c7282","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma delay_rough of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] Let $\mathbf{X}$ be a \emph{$\pi$-delayed $\gamma$-rough path with delay $r > 0$}. Suppose $\frac{1}{3}<\beta\leq\gamma$ , $r_1\leq r$ $(\zeta,\zeta^\prime)\in\mathscr{D}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\beta}([-r,0],\mathbb{R}^d) $, $(\xi_1,\xi_{1}^{\prime})\in\mathscr{D}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\beta}([-r,0],\mathbb{R}^d)$ and $(\xi_2,\xi_{2}^{\prime}),(\eta,\eta^{\prime})\in \mathscr{D}_{\mathbf{X}}^{\beta}([0,r_1],\mathbb{R}^d) $. Assume $F:\mathbb{R}^{d}\times\mathbb{R}^d\times\mathbb{R}^d\rightarrow \mathcal{L}(\mathbb{R}^m,\mathbb{R}^n)\in C^{2}_{b}$. Let $\xi: [-r,r_1]\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^d$ is defined by
			\begin{align}
				\xi_{t}:=\begin{cases}
					\xi_{1}(t), & \text{for } -r\leq t<0\\
					\xi_{2}(t), & \text{for } 0\leq t\leq r_1
				\end{cases} \ \ 
			\end{align}
			and
			\begin{align}
				\xi_{t}^{\prime}:=\begin{cases}
					\xi_{1}^{\prime}(t), & \text{for } -r\leq t<0\\
					\xi_{2}^{\prime}(t), & \text{for } 0\leq t\leq r_1
				\end{cases} \ \ 
			\end{align}
			Then for every  $s,t\in [0,r_1]$ and $A_{s}:=\big(\eta_{s},\zeta_{s-r},\int_{-r}^{0}\xi_{\theta+s}\pi(\mathrm{d}\theta)\big)$, following statement is well defined
			\begin{align}
				\begin{split}
					&\int_{s}^{t}F\big(\eta_{\tau},\zeta_{\tau-r},\int_{-r}^{0}\xi_{\theta+\tau}\pi(\mathrm{d}\theta)\big)\circ\mathrm{d}\mathbf{X}_{\tau}:=\quad\lim_{\vert\Pi\vert\rightarrow 0}\bigg[\sum_{t_j\in\Pi}F(A_{t_j})\circ X_{t_{j},t_{j+1}}\\&\quad+\sum_{t_j\in\Pi} \frac{\partial F}{\partial Y_{1}}|_{A_{t_j}}\circ (\eta^{\prime}_{t_j}\circ\mathbb{X}_{t_j,t_{j+1}})+\sum_{t_j\in\Pi}\frac{\partial F}{\partial Y_{2}}|_{A_{t_j}}\circ(\zeta^{\prime}_{t_j-r}\circ \mathbb{X}_{t_j,t_{j+1}}(-r))\\ &\qquad+\sum_{t_j\in\Pi} \frac{\partial F}{\partial Y_{3}}|_{A_{t_j}}\circ \big(\int_{-r}^{0}\xi_{\theta+t_j}^{\prime}\circ \mathbb{X}_{t_j,t_{j+1}}(\theta)\pi(\mathrm{d}\theta)\big)\bigg{]}.
				\end{split}
			\end{align}
			Where by $\frac{\partial F}{\partial Y_{1}}|_{A_{\tau}}, \frac{\partial F}{\partial Y_{2}}|_{A_\tau}$ and $ \frac{\partial F}{\partial Y_{3}}|_{A_\tau}$, we mean the derivative of $F$ respect to first, second and the third arguments at $A_\tau$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.643914+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80dd3ce37a9c01129c9a6c29d1703d3f7f0ad395acdfccf4f0115d2d18efaa37","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:gartner_ellis of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Consider a sequence of random variables $(X_n)_{n \geq 1}$ and a sequence $(a_n)_{n \geq 1}$ with $a_n \gg 1$. Assume for every $\lambda \in \mathbb R$ the limit
    \begin{align*}
    \lim_{n \to \infty}\frac{1}{a_n}\log \mathbb{E}\left[\Big.\exp\big(\lambda a_nX_n\big)\right]= \Lambda(\lambda),  \addtocounter{equation}{1}\tag{\theequation} 
\end{align*}
    exists point-wise as a convex function of $\lambda$, possibly assuming an extended real value for some $\lambda$, with the origin lying inside the interior of the domain $\mathcal D_{\Lambda}:=\{\lambda \in \mathbb R:\Lambda(\lambda)<\infty\}.$ Let $\Lambda$ further satisfy the following:
    \begin{itemize}
    \item Interior of $\mathcal D_{\Lambda}$ is non empty.
    \item $\Lambda$ is differentiable at all points of the interior of $\mathcal D_{\Lambda}$.
    \item $\Lambda(\cdot)$ is steep, namely, $\lim_{n \to \infty} |\Lambda'(\Lambda_n)|=\infty$ whenever $\lambda_n$ is a sequence of interior points of $\mathcal D_{\Lambda}$ converging to some boundary point of the interior of $\mathcal D_{\Lambda}$.
\end{itemize}
Then the sequence $(X_n)_{n \geq 1}$ satisfies a LDP with speed $a_n$ and with good rate function $\Lambda^*$, the \emph{Fenchel-Legendre} transform of $\Lambda$, defined as,
\begin{align*}
    \Lambda^*(x)=\sup_{\lambda \in \mathbb R}\Big\{\lambda x -\Lambda(\lambda)\Big\}\,.
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:32:29.002666+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80dd87909c5ba5a89c6bfad02a161fea5db5146f1dd736d9e5f0deb7c8fc982a","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem typT poly UB of arXiv:2608.25471, 'Exact Hausdorff measures and fine geometry of collisions of independent Markov processes'] For any $\ell \in \mathbb{N}$ and $a \in (0,\infty)$,
