  \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:33:43.760490+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80e2d0f15fa30e1cd1da9b3787aab34c6b27cbecbc4593b77802f27686e358bb","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Corr thisgivesthat of arXiv:2609.02682, 'LDP for tensor forms'] Given a sequence of graphs $G_n$ and a fixed graph $H$ above, set $Q_n$ by (equation eq:333 of the paper) and set $\widetilde{G}_n:=G_n/\lVert G_n\rVert_1$. Let $W^{(2)}_{\widetilde{G}_n}\in L^1([0,1]^2)$ be defined as in (equation eq:funext of the paper). Suppose the following assumptions are satisfied: 
\begin{align}
    \limsup_{n\to\infty}\,\lVert W^{(2)}_{\widetilde{G}_n}\rVert_{q\Delta} < \infty \qquad \mbox{and} \qquad \lVert W^{(2)}_{\widetilde{G}_n}-W_\infty^{(2)}\rVert_{\square^*}\overset{n\to\infty}{\to} 0, 
\end{align}
for some $q>1$ and $W_\infty^{(2)}\in L^1([0,1]^2)$. Then for any ${\boldsymbol \alpha}\in \mathbb{R}^c$, the  statistic
${\boldsymbol\alpha}^\top {\boldsymbol N}(H,G_n,{\bf{X}})$ satisfies an LDP with speed $n$ and the good rate function from (equation eq:i2 of the paper), with $G_{W_{\infty,\mathbf{1}_r}}$ replaced by $G^{(2)}_{{\infty},r}$, $1\le r\le c$ defined as
\begin{align}
    G^{(2)}_{{\infty},r}(\boldsymbol{f}):=\int_{[0,1]^v}\prod_{\{i,j\}\in E(H)} W^{(2)}_{\infty}(x_{i},x_{j})\prod_{a=1}^v f_r(x_a)\, d x_a.
\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.801547+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80f0bdfea66b63e8307dea397322ecede188d44952d2d416d69ea2bf83f41b07","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2609.03070, 'Variants of order semicontinuity in Banach lattices'] $F$ is $r$-semicontinuous if and only if the common order and uo closure of $\Ba_{F}$ is contained in $r\Ba_{F}$. Moreover, the following conditions are equivalent:
\item[(i)] $F$ is topologically semicontinuous;
\item[(ii)] The norm topology is semicontinuous;
\item[(iii)] The common order and uo closure of $\Ba_{F}$ is norm bounded;
\item[(iv)] Order closure preserves norm bounded sets;
\item[(iv')] Uo closure preserves norm bounded sets.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T05:57:28.231560+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80f3f00da76d82e11457a4f824bfdec4a1f521a0ef7ca9a3c719f1710c395c3f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 29 of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] For all $m_0>0$, there exist constants $\theta_0,c_1,C_1,L_0>0$, depending only on $\mathfrak{a}$ and $m_0$, such that for all $m=m(n) \in [m_0,m_0^{-1}]$, $\theta \geq \theta_0$, for all $x  \leq \theta$, and $L=L(\np) \in \lbr \np \rbr$ with $m L \geq L_0$, 
\begin{equation}
\Pfull\Big( \TF_L\big(\gamma_{(0,0),(\np,m\np)}\big) \geq x L^{2/3} \Big) \leq C_1\exp({-c_1 x^{2}}) 
\end{equation} for all $n$ large enough. Similarly, consider a family of $(N,k)$-periodic environments such that \eqref{eq:NonDegeneratedEnvironment} and \eqref{eq:CorrectPeriodicScale} hold with respect to some $\mathfrak{a}>0$. Then we get that for all $ x  \leq \theta^{1/3}$, and $L \in \lbr \np \rbr$ with $L \geq L_0$ that we find constants $c_2,C_2>0$ so that
\begin{equation}
\Pper\Big( \TF_L\big(\gper_{(0,0),(\np,m\np)}\big) \geq x L^{2/3} \Big) \leq C_2\exp({-c_2 x^{2}}) 
\end{equation} for all $N$ and $\np=\np(N)$ large enough. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:37:51.456558+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_80fdf5337e182cb059598b4b09a671969f7585bf417ef6e1261e6b9ffba99d5b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem 18 of arXiv:2604.03777, 'Equilibrium fluctuations for a multi-species particle system with long jumps'] For every $n \in \mathbb{N}_+$, it holds
\begin{align} 
 \sup_{r \in [0,T]} \Bigg\{ \frac{1}{n} \sum_{x} \big[  \widehat{\mathbb{L}}_{n, \lambda}^{ \gamma} H ( \tfrac{x + q_n^r }{n}  ) \big]^{2}  \Bigg\} \leq | \lambda| K_n C(\gamma,H).
\end{align}
Moreover, if $H \in C_c^{2}(\mathbb{R})$, then the role of $C(\gamma,H)$ above is fulfilled by some constant $C(H)$ satisfying \eqref{Cinvshift}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:31:57.858073+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8104f716ed330a4a47b235ba6b39299e4cc53e02d399351257ff144080ecdb2d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:IB of arXiv:2510.10299, '\Large Sparse graphs and their Benjamini-Schramm limits: a spectral tour'] Let $G = (V, E)$ be a finite graph, and let $A$, $B$ and $D$ be its adjacency, non-backtracking and degree diagianol operators ($D \varphi(v) = \deg(v) \varphi(v)$). For all $z \in \mathbb{C}$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\det(z 1_{E} - B) = (z^2 - 1)^{\chi - 1} \det(z^2 1_V - z A + D - 1_V),
\end{equation}
where $1_V$ and $1_{E}$ are the identity operators on $\mathbb{C}^V$ and $\mathbb{C}^{E}$, respectively, and $\chi = |E|/2 - |V| + 1$ is the Euler characteristic of the graph. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:48:45.306527+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_81071533925e27592790529f29396671dc7c33e7ca28a51c4a5f2e92e7a6f8cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition eq:seat_psi of arXiv:2411.14818, 'Scaling limits of solitons in the Box-Ball system'] Suppose that $\eta \in \Omega$. Then, for any $k, \ell \in {\mathbb{N}}$, $\sigma \in \{\uparrow, \downarrow\}$ and $x \in {\mathbb{Z}}$, we have 
                    \begin{align}
                        \Psi_{k}\left( \eta \right)^{\sigma}_{\ell}(x) = \eta^{\sigma}_{ k + \ell}\left(s_{k}\left(\eta, x + \xi_{k}\left(\eta, 0\right) \right) \right).
                    \end{align}
                In addition, we have 
                    \begin{align}
                        \Psi_{k }\left( \Psi_{\ell}\left(\eta\right) \right)\left( \ \cdot \ \right) =  \Psi_{k + \ell }\left( \eta\right)\left( \ \cdot \ \right),
                    \end{align}
                and 
                    \begin{align}
                        \zeta_{k}\left(\Psi_{\ell}\left( \eta \right), \ \cdot \ \right) = \zeta_{k+\ell}\left(\eta, \ \cdot \ \right) .
                    \end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:31:20.861167+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8109d193f3c60c2fefa54e5626df00c1392d4870811c703d3a0f34d0c84ca6f9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2506.10817, 'On the Weak Error for Local Stochastic Volatility Models'] Let $X^h, Y$ be defined as above, let Assumption \ref{Aspt_new} hold and assume that $f:\mathbb{R}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ is $l$-times weakly differentiable with derivatives which are functions of polynomial growth. Then, for $h \in (0,1)$, 
    \begin{align*}
