    \] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:54:12.630514+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_828e68b3354f5c390e293aed397cefebdf972c3431ccbfdbb838daabc98d9c85","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop P:two-point-bd of arXiv:2604.10020, 'The directed landscape in half-space'] Take $n \in \mathbb{N}$, parameter $\alpha \ge 1/2 - n^{-1/6}$, and $x, y, z_0 \in \llbracket0, n/100 \rrbracket$, $a > 0$ satisfying
\begin{equation}

C_0 \max \left(\frac{z_0^2}{n}, \frac{z_0 |y-x|}{n}, z_0(1/2 - \alpha)\vee 0 \right) < a < C_* z_0,
\end{equation}
where $C_0>0$ is a universal constant, and $C_*>0$ is any large enough number.
Then for a constant $c>0$ depending only on $C_*$, we have
$$
\mathbb{P}\left(\max_{z \in\llbracket0,z_0 \rrbracket} \left|\bar X[x, 1; y + z, n]_\textsf{L} - \bar X[x, 1; y, n]_\textsf{L} \right| \ge a \right) \le 2\exp(- c a^2/z_0) + 2 \exp(-c \sqrt{n}).
$$ (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:18:27.580241+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_82911cc718d1dd3ec2b3a7cc0659daa8fb1a768b34f04ba1ee1a140ea2bd2e53","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 ($K_\infty$ is generically not Hilbert--Schmidt) of arXiv:2608.15987, 'Threshold cascades of interacting diffusions on unimodular random trees: a conditional bra'] Let $k_\infty(\xi,\eta)$ be the limiting boundary kernel of the operator programme of Section 1.1, so that $k_\infty(\xi,\eta)\asymp\nu_h(B_{\xi\wedge\eta})^{-\beta}$ for some $\beta>0$ determined by the transmission/Martin exponents, where $\xi\wedge\eta$ is the confluence depth. Then
\begin{align*}
\|K_\infty\|_{\mathrm{HS}}^2&=\iint_{\partial T\times\partial T}|k_\infty(\xi,\eta)|^2\,d\nu_h(\xi)\,d\nu_h(\eta)\\
&\asymp\sum_{n\ge0}\br(T)^{2n\beta}\cdot\nu_h\otimes\nu_h\big(\{|\xi\wedge\eta|=n\}\big)\asymp\sum_{n\ge0}\br(T)^{n(2\beta-1)}
\end{align*}
whenever $2\beta\ge1$ and $g$ is supercritical ($\br(T)>1$). For the Martin-kernel construction one has $\beta\ge1$, since $K(x,\cdot)\asymp\nu_h(B_{|x|})^{-1}$ on the cylinder of $x$ (the Martin kernel is the harmonic-measure density $d\nu_h^x/d\nu_h$). Hence $K_\infty$ is not Hilbert--Schmidt, and the Krein--Rutman route through Hilbert--Schmidt compactness is unavailable.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:46:26.253600+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8291a39b841a1c0ca6eee5654912d01d093cfbe35914f8a921be5394b647bc2e","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 3 of arXiv:2605.23308, 'Convergence rate estimates for semigroups and heat kernels associated with resistance form'] Suppose that \cref{mainassump} is satisfied. 
  We regard $F_n,\;n\in\N,$ and $F$ as subsets of a common $\bcm$ space $(M,d^M)$.
  Let $x\in F$ and $x_n\in F_n$. Take $r_{\infty}\ge r_0$ satisfying 
  $x\in B(\rho,r_{\infty})$ and $x_n\in B(\rho_n,r_{\infty})$.
  Then there exist a constant $E=E(s,\theta,\kappa)$ and a polynomial $C_n(r)=C_n(\mu_n(F_n^{(r)}),\mu(F^{(r)}),\diam F^{(r)}\cup F_n^{(r)},\diam F^{(r)})$ (see \cref{noncpt} for explicit formulae),
  and it holds for any $f:M\to\R,\;\kappa\in [1/2,1],$ and $t>0$ that
  \begin{equation}
    \begin{aligned}
   \MoveEqLeft \Big|E_{x}[f(X_t)]-E^n_{x_n}[f(X^n_t)]\Big|\\
    \lesssim&\|f\|_{\BL^{\kappa}(M,d^M)}
    \inf\left\{\Psi_1(n,r)+C_n(r)\Psi_2(n,r)^E : r\ge r_{\infty}, \Psi_2(n,r)\le \frac{1}{2}\right\},  
    \end{aligned}
  \end{equation}
  where
  \begin{equation*}
    \Psi_1(n,r)=P_{x}(\sigma_{B(\rho,r)^c}\le t)\vee P_{x_n}^n(\sigma^n_{B(\rho_n,r)^c}\le t),
  \end{equation*}
  \begin{equation*}
    \Psi_2(n,r)=d_{\rm{H}}^M(F^{(r)},F_n^{(r)})^{\kappa}+d_{\BL^{\kappa}}^M(\mu^{(r)},\mu^{(r)}_n)+d^M(x,x_n),
  \end{equation*}
  Moreover, the time-dependence of the constant in $\lesssim$ is $t^{-[\kappa-\frac{s}{2(1+s)\theta}(2\kappa-1)]},\;t\in (0,1]$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

