{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:45:32.074452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_846865dfb3fd4e3a88354cf7586c7084f9dffecf76f3644066b52fced526b29a","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:branching-number:growth of arXiv:2411.14111, 'Stochastic processes on preferential attachment models'] The \emph{branching number} of a tree $\Gamma$, denoted by $\rm{br}(\Gamma)$, is defined as
	\eqan{
		\rm{br}(\Gamma)=&~\inf\Big\{ \lambda>0:\liminf\limits_{\Pi\to\infty}\sum\limits_{u\in\Pi} \lambda^{-|u|}=0 \Big\}\\
		=&~\sup\Big\{ \lambda>0:\liminf\limits_{\Pi\to\infty}\sum\limits_{u\in\Pi} \lambda^{-|u|}=\infty \Big\}\nn\\
		=&~\inf\Big\{ \lambda>0:\inf\limits_{\Pi}\sum\limits_{u\in\Pi} \lambda^{-|u|} \Big\}
		~,\nn}
	whereas the \emph{growth} of $\Gamma$, denoted by $\rm{gr}(\Gamma)$, is defined as
	\eqn{
		{\rm{gr}}(\Gamma)=\inf\Big\{ \lambda>0:\liminf\limits_{n\to \infty} {M_n}\lambda^{-n}=0\Big\}=\liminf\limits_{n\to\infty}M_n^{1/n}
		~,}
	where $M_n$ is the size of the $n$-th generation in the tree $\Gamma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:10.886694+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8468a1b565ccbf6e51c3f29104515cd235ce0b3e9cfa01352de829767eb04751","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 4 of arXiv:2504.02435, 'Poisson-Voronoi percolation in higher rank'] A countable group~$\Gamma$ has the {\em FIID sparse unique infinite cluster property} if there exists a Cayley graph ${\rm Cay}(\Gamma)$ of~$\Gamma$ such that
    \begin{equation} 
    \inf \bigg\{ \int_{\omega\in \mathcal U(G)} {\rm deg}_\omega(1_{\Gamma}) \, d \mu(\omega) \colon \mu\in F_{{\rm IID}}(\Gamma,\mathcal U({\rm Cay}(\Gamma))) \bigg\} = 0,
    \end{equation}
    where $\mathcal U({\rm Cay}(\Gamma))$ is the set of subgraphs of ${\rm Cay}(\Gamma)$ with a unique infinite cluster, $F_{{\rm IID}}(\Gamma,\mathcal S(\Gamma))$ is the set of $\Gamma$-invariant probability measures on $\mathcal U({\rm Cay}(\Gamma))$ which are factors of iid~processes on $\Gamma$ and $1_\Gamma$ denotes the identity of $\Gamma$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:17:13.076105+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8469b6e5899ce90d9becb63f741fe5dc9ae71cbc4ae587f544ad88638f5a77c0","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theo thm:quantitative-convergence-h of arXiv:2509.08928, 'The near critical random bond Ising model via embedding deformation'] Consider an s-embedding $\mathcal{S}^{(t)}_{\omega}=\mathcal{S}^{\delta_n}$ constructed by Lemma (lem:SDE-embedding of the paper) and $(\Omega^{\delta},a^{\delta},b^{\delta},\omega,t) \subset \mathbb{C}$ a discrete bounded simply connected domain drawn over $\mathcal{S}^{\delta_n}$, equipped with Dobrushin boundary conditions. For a realisation $\omega$ under the probability measure $\mathbf{P}$, set $F^{(t)}_{\delta_n,\omega}$ the $s$-holomorphic function associated to the FK-Dobrushin observable $X^{(t)}_{(\Omega^{\delta_n},\omega)}$. Denote by $h^{(t)}_{\mathrm{int}(\eta),\omega} $ the harmonic continuation of $H_{F^{(t)}_{\delta_n,\omega}}$ in $\Omega^{\delta}_{\mathrm{int}(\eta)} $. Then, with $\mathbf{P}$-probability at least $1-O(n^{-4})$, there exist an exponent $\gamma=\gamma(\alpha)>0$ and constant $O=O(\alpha,\omega)>0$ such that for any $0\leq t \leq t^{(\alpha)}_n$ and uniformly in $\Omega^{\delta}_{\mathrm{int}(\eta)} $, one has
	\begin{equation}
		|H_{F^{(t)}_{\delta_n,\omega}} -h^{(t)}_{\mathrm{int}(\eta),\omega} | = O(\delta_n^{\gamma}).
	\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:51:15.030797+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_84726c75700c92f4a34c6ce4acb71a8c8c09a566a64b5763d6412bf3e499ee5b","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem existencUnicit of arXiv:2609.03909, 'Conic Pseudo-Finslerian Mechanical Systems'] Let $\mathcal{L}$ be a regular Lagrangian. 
Then a smooth curve $\gamma = (x_1, \ldots, x_n)$ is an $\mathcal{L}$-geodesic if and only if it satisfies
\begin{equation}
\frac{d^2 x_i}{dt^2} 
+ \sum_j g^{ij}(v) 
\left(
  \frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{L}}{\partial v_j \partial x_i} \frac{d x_i}{dt} 
  - \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial x_j}
\right) = 0,
\end{equation}
where $g^{ij}(v)$ denotes the inverse matrix of 
$g_{ij}(v) = g_v^{\mathcal{L}}\!\left(\frac{\partial}{\partial x_i}, \frac{\partial}{\partial x_j}\right)$.
In particular, for each $v \in \mathcal{U}$ there exists a unique maximal $\mathcal{L}$-geodesic $\gamma_v : I_v \to M$ such that $\gamma_v'(0) = v$ and $0 \in I_v$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:29:00.770216+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8476e3ecd289eac8841d44adbbb0335cb4f61d4e32bc3ded2b7f287636bda171","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 1 (\cite{KirkpatrickMeckes2013,KirkpatrickNawaz2016,FangKoike2022}) of arXiv:2512.05340, 'The rate of convergence of the critical mean-field $O(N)$ magnetization via multivariate n'] Fix $N \geq 2$ and let $W_{n} := \sqrt{N-\beta}\,S_{n}/\sqrt{n}$ and $Z \sim N\left(0, \id\right)$, where $\id\in\RR^{d\times d}$ is the
