where $U_x$ is uniform on $\{0,\ldots,x\}$ and $X_t'$ is independent of $U_x$ with the law of $X_t$. Null changes are allowed. The process is increasing and is sent to $\infty$ when its graphical range becomes infinite.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:33:01.760211+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_87ff225469142040bcdee1470b5b650e872814d10068b5fab4da72abbdee90e4","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 of arXiv:2605.14254, '2605.14254'] The infinitesimal generator $A^{\lambda,z}$ of $\zeta^{\lambda,z}$ is given by
		\begin{align*}
			\begin{array}{ll}
				A^{\lambda,z}h(y_1,y_2)=\begin{cases} 0 & y_1=0, y_2\in \mathbb{R} \\
					-\lambda y_1\,\dfrac{\partial h}{\partial y_2}(y_1,y_2) +  \dfrac{y_1}{2}\,\dfrac{\partial^2 h}{\partial ^2y_2}(y_1,y_2) &  y_1=1, z<y_2 \\
					\dfrac{y_1}{2}\,\dfrac{\partial^2 h}{\partial ^2y_2}(y_1,y_2) &  y_1=1, y_2=z \\
					\lambda y_1\,\dfrac{\partial h}{\partial y_2}(y_1,y_2) +  \dfrac{y_1}{2}\,\dfrac{\partial^2 h}{\partial ^2y_2}(y_1,y_2) &  y_1=1, z>y_2
				\end{cases}
			\end{array}
		\end{align*}
		Moreover, the domain of $A^{\lambda,z}$ is given by
		\begin{equation*}
			\mathcal{D}(A^{\lambda,z})=\left\{h\in C_0(\mathcal{S}); \dfrac{\partial h}{\partial y_2}, \text{ and } \dfrac{\partial^2 h}{\partial ^2y_2} \text{ exist,  continuous, }h(0,z)=h(1,z)=\dfrac{\partial h}{\partial y_2}(1,z)=0 \right\}.
		\end{equation*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.828540+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_880365d81f0da38f2610b2375bcb774b4040a426ce2134f5b9965971372a2d39","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:ito-sums of arXiv:2606.28869, '\bfseries A General Theory of Paths:\\ \large Signatures, Jump Lifts, and Expected Signatu'] Let $X:[0,T]\to E$ be a finite-variation pure-jump c\`adl\`ag{} path, constant between jumps, with jumps $z_a=\Delta X_{\tau_a}$ at $0<\tau_1<\cdots<\tau_n\le T$, and let $\mathrm{Sig}^I(X)$ be its forward lift
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{Sig}^I_{0,t}=\mathbf 1+\int_{(0,t]}\mathrm{Sig}^I_{0,u^-}\otimes\,\mathrm{d} X_u .
\end{equation}
Then for every word $w=(i_1,\ldots,i_k)$,
\begin{equation}
\inner{\mathrm{Sig}^I(X)_{0,T}}{w}
=\sum_{1\le a_1<\cdots<a_k\le n}z_{a_1}^{i_1}\cdots z_{a_k}^{i_k}
=\inner{\mathrm{Sig}^{\mathrm{sum}}(z)}{w},
\qquad
\mathrm{Sig}^{\mathrm{sum}}(z)=\overrightarrow{\textstyle\prod}_{a=1}^n(\mathbf 1+z_a).

\end{equation}
In particular $\mathrm{Sig}^I(X)$ is a character on the quasi-shuffle Hopf algebra.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.490931+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8804f12d2d60e78b9c5a40edfb2b50e40cd41331bfe427c58df8aec815988144","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop uniqueness of solution for single-location master loop equation in the 't Hooft limit of arXiv:2411.11676, 'Surface sums for lattice Yang--Mills in the large-$N$ limit'] Given any $L\geq 1$, there exists $\upbeta_*(L,d)>0$ such that if $|\upbeta|\leq \upbeta_*(L,d)$, then there is a unique function $\phi_{\infty}:\mathcal{S}\to \mathbb{R}$ such that 
