{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:42:16.235719+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8c398c2724d148d1e6829e761f9fe5a95d403243b2ed9f7dbddee0c0c4bc9cd8","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem prop:uniform-time-avg-poc of arXiv:2607.00149, '\bf Uniform-in-time Propagation-of-Chaos for\\ Stein Variational Gradient Descent'] Let Assumption~(ass:poc of the paper) hold. Recall the constant $c_0$ from Lemma~(lem:finite-time-poc of the paper). Then, there exists a finite constant $C>0$ such that, for any $\delta>0$ and all sufficiently large $N$,
\begin{equation}
\sup_{T>0}
\mathbb E\,\left[\mathsf{KSD}_\pi\bigl(\overline\mu_T^N,\overline\mu_T\bigr)\, \mathbf{1}_{\{\int \|x\|^2 \mu^N_0(dx) \le R\}}\right]
\le C N^{-\beta/2}\exp\{c_0(1+R)(\log N)^{c_0\delta}\} + C(\delta\log\log N)^{-1/2}.
\end{equation}
Consequently,
\begin{equation}
    \lim_{M \to \infty} \, \limsup_{N \to \infty}\,\sup_{T>0}\mathbb{P}\left[\mathsf{KSD}_\pi\bigl(\overline\mu_T^N,\overline\mu_T\bigr) \ge M (\log \log N)^{-1/2}\right] = 0.
\end{equation}
Moreover, suppose that the empirical initial second moments satisfy the uniform sub-Gaussian bound
\[
R_N:=\int_{\mathbb R^d}|x|^2\,\mu_0^N(dx),
\qquad
\sup_{N\ge1}\mathbb E\exp\{\lambda R_N^2\}<\infty
\]
for some \(\lambda>0\). Then there exists \(C<\infty\) such that, for all sufficiently large \(N\),
\begin{equation}
\sup_{T>0}
\mathbb E\,\mathsf{KSD}_\pi(\overline\mu_T^N,\overline\mu_T)
\le
C(\log\log N)^{-1/2}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:38:23.574506+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8c3b378b62e995fef0628a3bff5b96dd12c596f47924d701e481767d7e77c103","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:equiv of arXiv:2511.01851, 'Percolation on graphs of polynomial growth is local:\\analyticity, supercritical sharpness'] The following statements are equivalent:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item every graph in $\mathfrak{A}$ satisfies $\theta(p_c)=0$,
        \item for every $\mathscr{G}\in \mathfrak{A}$, the map $p\longmapsto \theta_\mathscr{G}(p)$ is continuous from $[0,1]$ to $[0,1]$,
        \item for every $p\in[0,1]$, the map $\mathscr{G}\longmapsto \theta_\mathscr{G}(p)$ is continuous from $\mathfrak{A}$ to $[0,1]$,
        \item the map $(\mathscr{G},p)\longmapsto \theta_\mathscr{G}(p)$ is continuous from $\mathfrak{A}\times [0,1]$ to $[0,1]$,
