{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T22:57:49.911888+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d8900aae92570782965669c28d78730561fa62db3227ad55cdeeb8d0722332a","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop prop1 of arXiv:2411.00663, 'Ergodicity and Mixing of invariant capacities and applications'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},P,T)$  be a measure preserving system. Then the following three statements are equivalent:
	\begin{longlist}
		\item $P$ is weakly mixing;
		\medskip
		\item for any $r>0$, $\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P^{r}(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P^{r}(B)P^{r}(C)$ for any $B,C\in\mathcal{F}$;
		\medskip
		\item    for any $B,C\in\mathcal{F}$, $
		\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P(B)P(C),$	 and there exists $r=r_{B,C}\in(0,1/2]$ such that $\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P^{r}(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P^{r}(B)P^{r}(C)$.
	\end{longlist}
	In particular, $P$ is weakly mixing  if and only if $P$ is ergodic and $\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}P^{1/2}(B\cap T^{-i}C)=P^{1/2}(B)P^{1/2}(C)$ for any $B,C\in\mathcal{F}$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:38:36.894628+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8d8e12ae790995bd27ca2b8e7956e88bfbf5f4025a37c6bde609028404fcdd78","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop biliearesti of arXiv:2606.31155, 'The well-posedness of stochastic Korteweg--de Vries equations revisited'] Let $3/8<b<1/2$, $0<T<1$. Then for $\varepsilon<2b-3/4$, we have 
