{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:09:23.952893+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8eba379d383402c005eedfb0a4e24a3048b3e0c62eb807d555db9a06c8ad08d3","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 15 ({\cite[Theorem~2.1) of arXiv:2607.21759, 'Marchenko--Pastur law for tensor powers of exchangeable unconditional vectors'] {Yaskov_2016_necessary_sufficient}\cite[Theorem~5.2]{yaskov2025spectra}}] 
    For each $p \in \N$, let $\mathbf x^{(1)}_p,\dotsc,\mathbf x^{(m)}_p$ be $m = m(p)$ i.i.d.\ random vectors in $\R^p$ (with no assumption on the mean and covariance matrix). Consider the sample covariance matrix $K_{p} = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{k=1}^m\mathbf{x}_p^{(k)}{\mathbf{x}_p^{(k)}}^\trp$. As $p \to \infty$, suppose the aspect ratio $p/m \to c$ for some fixed $c > 0$, and \begin{equation}\frac{\mathbf x^\trp_p A_p \mathbf x_p - \tr A_p}{p} \to 0 \quad \text{in probability} \end{equation} for any sequence of positive semidefinite (PSD) matrices $A_p \in \R^{p\times p}$ with $\opnm{A_p} \leq 1$, then \[
        \ESD(K_p) \wkconv \mu_{\MP(c)}
    \] with probability $1$, where $\mu_{\MP(c)}$ is specified in \eqref{eq:MP}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:13:49.966793+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ebcac27e62255096abd9cc2572c8aa52d032a4b6c4082c9612f74ab235e1b8f","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem_gamma.from.K of arXiv:2411.07113, '\begin{LARGE}On differentiability and mass distributions of topologically typical multivar'] Consider $C \in \mathcal{C}_{ar}^d$ and let $\psi$, $\gamma$, and $F_K^d$ denote the 
corresponding generator, Williamson measure and Kendall distribution function, respectively. 
Then the following identity holds for all $z \in [0,\infty)$:
      \begin{equation}
     \gamma([0,z]) = \begin{cases}
        F_K^d(\psi(\frac{1}{z})),& \text{ if } z \in [\frac{1}{\varphi(0)},\infty)\\ 
        0,& \text{ if } z \in [0,\frac{1}{\varphi(0)}). 
     \end{cases}
    \end{equation}
Moreover, the non-decreasing transformation $h: [0,\infty] \rightarrow \mathbb{I}$, defined by $h(z)=\psi(\frac{1}{z})$ 
fulfills $h(0)=0$ and is non-singular in the sense that for every $F \in \mathcal{B}([0,\infty))$ we have that 
$\lambda(F)=0$ implies $\lambda^h(F)=0$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:05:09.629292+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8ec4f983e4ad69b72391ebbe6c860b9d66ba28070f635f5ab3fd350796afdf68","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:bounds_h_2_multidim of arXiv:2411.03609, 'Asymptotically optimal Wasserstein couplings for the small-time stable domain of attractio'] Under Assumption~(\nameref{asm:C}), there exists a function $\widetilde{h}:(0,\infty)\times \mathbb{S}^{d-1}\to(-1,\infty)$ and a constant $K_{\widetilde{h}} \geqslant 0$, such that, for all $x>0$ and $\bm{v} \in \mathbb{S}^{d-1}$, 
