{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:49:02.087452+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_8fb75ab9aeec8743d0e237220a79b29f12c2d4ce4333ba4985e24a98b124f330","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 3 of arXiv:2604.25769, 'The conformal dimension of the Brownian tree is one'] Let $(\SCT, d_\SCT, \SM_\SCT; o)$ be an infinite Brownian tree. Let $\mathop{\mathrm{proj}} \colon \BR \to \SCT$ be the canonical projection mapping. Let $T > 0$. Given $\SCT$ and $\mathop{\mathrm{proj}}$, let $\{x_n\}_{n \in \BN}$ be conditionally independent samples from $\SM_\Phi|_{\mathop{\mathrm{proj}}([-T, T])}$ (renormalized to be a probability measure). Then for each $\zeta > 0$, almost surely on the event that $B_1(o; d_\SCT) \subset \mathop{\mathrm{proj}}([-T, T])$, there exists $\varepsilon_\ast \in (0, 1)$ such that 
