{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:43:04.725952+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_981eff4e65307f1bc0654eca49afc3c21b9f268f1bdde4d7d76773eb35bec278","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 (FKG inequality) of arXiv:2605.17494, 'Generalized intersection exponents and local cut points for three-dimensional Brownian loo'] A function $f$ on the space of loop configurations is said to be \emph{increasing} if, for any realizations $\Lc'\subseteq\Lc''$ of the BLS, we have $f(\Lc') \le f(\Lc'')$. Then, for any two increasing functions $f$ and $g$,
