{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:21:06.856187+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_991a9cd649f6360b40c3683d97005f767dea9582e8d7fbc1f6b5b61801903c44","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 23 of arXiv:2608.21014, 'The stochastic Cahn--Hilliard equation in critical spaces'] Let $d=4$ and suppose that the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:localWP_weakened} hold. Moreover, let $u_0\in L^0_{\F_0}(\Omega;H^1(\sO))$ and $T>0$. Let $(u,\sigma)$ be the unique maximal solution to \eqref{eq:SCHtorus} and $\mu$ the chemical potential of Definition~\ref{def:chemPotential}. Then, we have
	\begin{equation}
	       \sup_{t\in[0,T]\cap [0,\sigma)}\|u(t)\|_{H^1}+\int_0^{\sigma \land T} \|\grad \mu(t)\|_{L^2}^2 \dt < \infty  \qquad \mathbb{P}\text{-a.s.}
