{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:37:28.986403+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b5f1bbf3cdc2f8df2f14a5a4631a1443351453e256a46106f5103f3b7b34a34","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma petites vitesses of arXiv:2412.00865, 'On the spectral problem and fractional diffusion limit for Fokker-Planck with(-out) drift '] \item 1.  For all $\gamma \in(\frac{d+1}{2},\frac{d+4}{2})$,  the following estimate holds
\begin{equation} \int_{\{|v_1|\leqslant R\}}  \bigg|\frac{  M_{0,\eta} - c_\eta M}{\langle v \rangle}\bigg|^2 \mathrm{d}v \lesssim \eta . 
\end{equation}
2.  For all $\gamma \in (\frac{d}{2},\frac{d+1}{2})$, one has
\begin{equation}
\underset{\eta \to 0}{\mathop{\rm lim}} \ \eta^{1-\alpha}  \int_{\{|v_1|\leqslant R\}} v_1 M_{0,\eta}(v)M(v) \ \mathrm{d}v = 0 .
\end{equation}
3.  For all $\gamma \in [\frac{d+1}{2},\frac{d+4}{2})$, one has
\begin{equation}
\underset{\eta \to 0}{\mathop{\rm lim}} \ \eta^{1-\alpha}  \int_{\{|v_1|\leqslant R\}} v_1 [M_{0,\eta}(v)- c_\eta M(v)]M(v) \ \mathrm{d}v = 0 .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:04:39.788567+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b601c16a6de8fb0cacf8aad4ab9e9a22e11196bbbc031c3faf75545611019ec","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:tailrl-harmonic-decomposition of arXiv:2609.02987, 'Tail Likelihood Reinforcement Learning'] The population-level TailRL{} objective decomposes as a harmonic mixture of Best-of-$k$ objectives
\begin{align}
J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}(\theta;x)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
\frac{\text{Best-of-}k(\theta;x)-1}{k}.

\end{align}
And under (lem:tailrl-regularity of the paper) the gradient $\nabla_\theta J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}(\theta;x)$ is a harmonic combination of Best-at-$k$ gradients,
\begin{align}
\nabla_\theta J_{\mathrm{TailRL{}}}(\theta;x)
=
\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}
\frac{1}{k}
\nabla_\theta\text{Best-of-}k(\theta;x).

\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:03:39.771674+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b627cff22ef32ebb7f2f44b7ee3c541ade48233e61ce644ff0355df90e62fe5","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Defin Modified admissibility of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] For $d\geq1$ and $s\in\{0,1,\dots,d-1\}$, we call $(d,s)$-\textit{admissible} the $(d,s)$-non-degenerate weight pairs $({\mathbf{a}},{\mathbf{b}})$ for which the random compositions $(C_{nd+s})_{n\geq 0}$  with distributions $(\PComp{{\mathbf{a}},{\mathbf{b}}}{nd+s})_{n\geq 0}$ can be coupled in such a way that
	\begin{align*}
	C_s\covered[d] C_{d+s}\covered[d] C_{2d+s}\covered[d] \dots\covered[d] C_{nd+s}\covered[d] C_{(n+1)d+s}\covered[d]\dots.
	\end{align*}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:27:08.852635+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b69b17d56d34eaeba3272a61e57245bbeb67c147601f81f1d46fe0eaab356e4","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 15 of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] Fix \(E\in(-2,2)\) and, for each \(N\), let \(i_N(E):=\min\{k:\,\lambda_k^{(N)}\ge E\sqrt{N}\}\).
For fixed offsets \(u\in\mathbb{Z}\), set \(i:=i_N(E)+u\).
Assume that the random family \(\bigl(\mu_{j}^{(N)},\,g_{j}^{(N)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\) converges almost surely, as \(N\to\infty\), to \(\bigl(\mu_{j}^{(0)},\,g_{j}^{(0)}\bigr)_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\).
Then, almost surely,
\begin{equation}
\lim_{N\to\infty}
\left(\frac{\Delta_{i,i}^{(N)}}{\upsilon_{i}^{(N)}}\right)^{2}
=
\bigl(\mu_i^{(1)}-\mu_i^{(0)}\bigr)^{2}
\sum_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}
\left(\frac{|g_j^{(0)}|}{\mu_i^{(1)}-\mu_j^{(0)}}\right)^{2},
\end{equation}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:as-convergence}, the variables \(\{g_j^{(0)}\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\) are independent Gaussian random variables (independent of \(\{\mu_j^{(0)}\}_{j\in\mathbb{Z}}\)). \(\mu_i^{(1)}\) denotes the \(i\)-th solution \(z\) of the secular equation
\(
S_{\mu^{(0)},\,(g^{(0)})^{2}}(z)\;=\;-\frac{E}{2\sqrt{4-E^{2}}}\,.
\) (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:19:47.225005+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9b6e38ab282cc6d01ca6a10bd06d29e5ca4412f4636a8ad683ab791a08ca03d7","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition of arXiv:2512.06640, 'Existence and sharpness of the phase transition for the frog model on transitive graphs'] A graph $G=(V,E)$ satisfies the \textbf{volume doubling} condition if there exists $C > 0$ such that for all $x\in V$ and $n\geq1:$
