{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T08:44:39.745715+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9c9673a4744b0a884d2442ff13a44375c68412f5b9d15a67406aafb1d59c7778","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 12 (Rigidity of eigenvalues) of arXiv:2608.29872, 'Local Laws and Outlier Eigenvalues for Spiked Separable Covariance Matrices'] Suppose the assumptions of Theorem \ref{local_law} hold. Then, outside the spectrum, we have the following stronger averaged local law:
\begin{equation}
|m_1(z)-m_{1c}(z)|+|m_2(z)-m_{2c}(z)|+|m(z)-m_c(z)|\prec
\frac{1}{N(\kappa+\eta)}
+\frac{1}{(N\eta)^2\sqrt{\kappa+\eta}},
\end{equation}
uniformly in
\(
z\in S(c_0,C_0,\varepsilon)
\cap \{z=E+\ii\eta:E\geq \lambda_+,\, N\eta\sqrt{\kappa+\eta}\geq N^\varepsilon\}
\)
for any fixed $\varepsilon>0$. 

The local laws \eqref{average} and \eqref{eq:averaged_outside} imply the following rigidity estimate for any constant $0<c_1<c_0$. Define the classical location $\gamma_j$ of the $j$-th eigenvalue of $\mathcal Q_1$ by
\begin{equation*}
\gamma_j:=\sup_x\left\{\int_x^{+\infty}\rho_c(x)\dd x>\frac{j-1}{p}\right\}.
\end{equation*}
In particular, $\gamma_1=\lambda_+$. Then, for any $j$ such that $\lambda_+-c_1\leq \gamma_j\leq \lambda_+$, we have
\begin{equation}
|\lambda_j(\cal Q_1)-\gamma_j|\prec j^{-1/3}n^{-2/3}.
\end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T21:00:30.486678+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9c9aa84f5e0e34511ab3895d7e81142327aaef3eb20270e5bb02b1373bc24fe9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 2 (\rm{\cite[Theorem 5.10) of arXiv:2607.03360, 'Smoluchowski-Kramers Approximation Meets Khasminskii Averaging Principles in Nonequilibriu'] {KP91}}]
Assume that the following conditions hold:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(KP1)] (Convergence condition): $(\CU_t^m, \CH_t^m) \to (0,\CH)$ in probability with respect to the sup-norm in $C([0,T]; \rr^{d_1} \times \rr^n)$, i.e., for all $\dl>0$,
\beq{cond-1}
\PP\bigg[\sup_{s\in [0,T]}\Big(|\CU_s^m-0|+ |\CH_s^m - \CH_s|\Big) > \dl \bigg] \to 0, \quad \text{as } m \to 0.
\eeq
\item[(KP2)] (Tightness condition): the total variations $\{\CV_t(\CA^m)\}$ are stochastically bounded for each $t>0$, i.e., $\PP[\CV_t(\CA^m) > K] \to 0$ as $K \to \infty$, uniformly in $m$.
\end{itemize}
Suppose that there exists a unique global solution $x$ to \eqref{xt-fH}. Then as $m\to 0$, $x^m$ converges to $x$ in probability with respect to $C([0,T];\rr^{d_1})$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T20:49:17.503627+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9c9ba45369b6bbdaff1a05054a38086becbe6f5a4237d24ed5a2c1dbf130d8c6","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 34 of arXiv:2605.30030, 'Critical exponents for planar random-cluster model with cluster-weight~$q=4$'] Fix~$\calF$ a well-separated inner flower domain on~$\Lambda_{2|y|}(x)$ and let~$\xi < \xi'$ be a boosting pair of boundary conditions.
Then 
