{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:13:18.533261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e5be83e1f00b76a430a7a37e5cb6676874533c5124c3a419337f97b4883d35a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 10 of arXiv:2608.30668, 'Recurrence and capacity of stable branching random walks'] Assume that \(d> \frac{2\alpha}{\alpha-1}\) and \(\theta\) has a finite
moment of order \(d-1\), then we have

\begin{equation*}{
G(x) \sim_{x \to \infty} C_{\mu,\theta} \lVert x \rVert^{d} g_{\theta}(x)^{\alpha}L(g_{\theta}(x)^{-1}),
}\end{equation*}
where \(C_{\mu,\theta} = \bcp(\{ 0 \})^{\alpha-1}\times  \int_{\R^d} \frac{1}{\Vert z\Vert^{d-2}} \frac{1}{\Vert u-z\Vert^{(\alpha-1)(d-2)}}\dd z\)
with \(u\) any unitary vector (for \(\lVert \cdot \rVert\)). In particular, \(G\) is \((2\alpha-d(\alpha-1))\)-varying. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:39:41.409222+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e63c1232b72d33c21f616b687a77988c772fdf86c9abfedf14dac18d7fa5e80","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 1 (SRRW on a discrete group) of arXiv:2604.07207, 'Mixing times of step-reinforced random walks'] Let $(G,\cdot)$ be a discrete group with $|G|\geq 2$ and let $\mu$ be a probability measure on $G$. Let $(\xi_n)_{n\geq 2}$ be i.i.d.\ Bernoulli random variables with success parameter $\alpha\in [0,1]$, and let $(u_n)_{n\geq 2}$ be independent random variables where each $u_n$ is uniformly distributed on $\{1,2,\ldots,n-1\}$. Define a walk $(S_n)_{n\in \NN}$ and its the step sequence $(X_n)_{n\geq 1}$ recursively as follows:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
  \item Set $S_0:=x \in G$ and at time $n=1$, sample $X_1$ from $\mu$, set $S_1:=X_1$;
  \item For $n> 1$, given $X_1,X_2,\dots,X_{n-1}$: 
  \begin{itemize}
    \item If $\xi_{n}=1$, set $X_{n}:=X_{u_{n}}$;
    \item If $\xi_{n}=0$, sample $X_{n}$ independently from $\mu$.
  \end{itemize}
 Update $S_{n}:=S_{n-1}\cdot X_{n}$.
\end{enumerate}
The process $S=(S_n)_{n\in \NN}$ is called a step-reinforced random walk (SRRW) on $G$ started at $x$, with step distribution $\mu$ and reinforcement parameter $\alpha$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:26:52.666880+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_9e7aeff45438574ba2d8499f844f2a263b7b35995b53188950165ecbf358764b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 21 of arXiv:2609.03736, '2609.03736'] The joint typical subspace contains asymptotically all of the
tensor powers:
\begin{equation}
 \Tr\bigl(\mathcal A_n(I-p_n)\bigr)=o(1).

