{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T01:41:54.375889+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a0deff50edfc29d239731c1db0918d3446d69f3175e45310e5d9ca0759b611c7","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:viscosity of arXiv:2412.01995, 'Exciting games and Monge-Amp\`{e}re equations'] The convex value function $v:[0,1) \times \Delta \to \mathbb R$ satisfies the dynamic programming principle, i.e., for any $(t,x) \in [0,1) \times \Delta $
    \begin{align*}
v(t,x)=&\textstyle  \inf_{\mathbb Q \in \mathcal{M}_{t,x}^{win}} \inf_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t^1} \mathbb E_{\mathbb Q}\left[ \int_{t}^{\tau} - \log \det \Sigma_u \, du + v(\tau,X_{\tau}) \right] \\
=& \textstyle \inf_{\mathbb Q \in \mathcal{M}_{t,x}^{win}} \sup_{\tau \in \mathcal{T}_t^1} \mathbb E_{\mathbb Q}\left[ \int_{t}^{\tau} - \log \det \Sigma_u \, du + v(\tau,X_{\tau}) \right],
    \end{align*}
    where $(X_t)_{t \in [0,1]}$ is the canonical process defined on the path space, and $\mathcal{T}_t^1$ denotes the set of stopping times with respect to the canonical filtration taking values in $[t,1]$. Moreover, the value function $v(t,x)$ is a viscosity solution to (equation eq:HJB of the paper).","status":"unverified"}

