{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:56:11.133525+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a229e25ef55c5db4892bbc1cd2125032ffed22958e46c5fbc5b9681abf9d0f19","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 6 (\cite{Chen2013}*{Lemma~5}) of arXiv:2608.10515, 'Limiting Law of Local Fields for the Mean Field Ghatak-Sherrington Model'] Fix an integer $m\ge1$. Let $\psi$ be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. For a binary word $\mathbf s=(s_1,\ldots,s_n)\in\{0,1\}^n$, let $\psi_{\mathbf s}$ be continuous on $[0,1]$ and differentiable on $(0,1)$. Suppose that all the expressions below are integrable at zero, that $\psi(0)=0$, and that
\begin{equation}
  \psi'(t)=\frac12\psi_{(0)}(t)+\frac1{2\sqrt t}\psi_{(1)}(t),
  \qquad
  \psi_{\mathbf s}'(t)=\frac12\psi_{(\mathbf s,0)}(t)
  +\frac1{2\sqrt t}\psi_{(\mathbf s,1)}(t)
\end{equation}
for $t\in(0,1)$ and $1\le |\mathbf s|\le m$. If
\[
  \psi_{\mathbf s}(0)=0,\qquad 1\le |\mathbf s|<m,
\]
then, writing
\[
  \Delta_n(t):=\{(t_1,\ldots,t_n):0<t_n<\cdots<t_1<t\},
\]
we have
\begin{align}
\psi(t)
={}&\frac1{2^m}\sum_{\mathbf s\in\{0,1\}^m}
\int_{\Delta_m(t)}
\left(\prod_{j=1}^m t_j^{-s_j/2}\right)\psi_{\mathbf s}(0)\,d\mathbf t \\
&+\frac1{2^{m+1}}\sum_{\mathbf s\in\{0,1\}^{m+1}}
\int_{\Delta_{m+1}(t)}
\left(\prod_{j=1}^{m+1}t_j^{-s_j/2}\right)
\psi_{\mathbf s}(t_{m+1})\,d\mathbf t.\notag
\end{align}","status":"unverified"}

