{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:13:31.943247+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a433fbe2b60e8236654b4f8ffd822c3b1bafc77efa39125e28e403fee97f3047","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Prop hall_projection of arXiv:2506.12171, 'The multinomial dimer model'] Let $G$ be a finite bipartite graph with bipartition $(B,W)$. Label every vertex $v\in G$ with a multiplicity $N_v$ such that 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v\in W} N_v = \sum_{v\in B} N_v.
    \end{align*}
    There exists an ${\mathbf N}= (N_v)_{v}$-dimer cover of $G$ if and only if there is no set $C\subset W$ such that 
    $$N(C) = \{v\in B : \exists\, c\in C, (c,v)\in E\}$$
    satisfies 
    \begin{align*}
        \sum_{v\in N(C)} N_v < \sum_{v\in C} N_v.
    \end{align*}
    We call $U:=C \cup N(C)$ satisfying these conditions an \emph{${\mathbf N}$-counterexample} in $G$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

