{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T07:10:05.795718+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a653a491bcbaa7b5cfea613decc88627c33135c9a0b9b5d3ab063895834168ec","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.17509, '2608.17509'] Let \(a>1\). The limiting log-moment generating function \(\Lambda_a\) belongs to \(C^\infty(\mathbb{R})\), with
\[
\Lambda_a'(\theta)
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_0^{\infty}
\frac{e^{\theta}r_a(\xi)}
{1+(e^{\theta}-1)r_a(\xi)}
\,\mathrm{d}\xi,
\]
\[
\Lambda_a''(\theta)
=
\frac{1}{2\pi}
\int_0^{\infty}
\frac{e^{\theta}r_a(\xi)(1-r_a(\xi))}
{\left(1+(e^{\theta}-1)r_a(\xi)\right)^2}
\,\mathrm{d}\xi .
\]
Moreover, \(\Lambda_a\) is strictly increasing and strictly convex on \(\mathbb{R}\), and
\begin{equation}

\lim_{\theta\to-\infty}\Lambda_a'(\theta)=0,
\qquad
\lim_{\theta\to+\infty}\Lambda_a'(\theta)=+\infty.
\end{equation}
Consequently, the Legendre--Fenchel transform 
\(
\widetilde{J}_a(x):=\sup_{\theta\in\mathbb{R}}\{\theta x-\Lambda_a(\theta)\}
\)
is finite precisely on \([0,\infty)\), and \(J_a:=\widetilde{J}_a|_{[0,\infty)}\) is a good rate function on \([0,\infty)\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T17:43:53.644992+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a657a76be598f67777bd69d3dc1a5b17b6f4ed1fbfd86f1204242b7a390cdd64","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition prop:bridge_subg_tail of arXiv:2512.17747, 'On exponentially height-penalized random trees'] Let $(B^{(n),x}_t;0\leq t \leq n)$ be a \textcolor{green!50!black}{uniform bridge} of length $n$ ending at $x$, and for fixed $C>0$, define the functional
    \begin{align*}
        Y_n(C,x):=L_{n}(B^{(n),x})\ind{-C\sqrt{n}\leq B^{(n),x}_t \leq C \sqrt{n}\;\forall\;t\in[0,n]}.
    \end{align*}
    Then uniformly over $x \in [-C\sqrt{n},C\sqrt{n}]$, for all sufficiently large $T>0$, we have
    \begin{align*}
        \Prob{\frac{Y_n(C,x)}{\sqrt{n}}>T}\leq R_1\exp{(-R_2T^2)},
    \end{align*}
    where $R_1,R_2>0$ are constants depending only on $C$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:09:15.344579+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a65bba3aa74721b7a7e8ed277582dbd3df5346ec18364c844098e755c1ae6227","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition eqSimplefuction of arXiv:2411.05534, 'Inverse problems for stochastic partial differential equations'] Let $ f: \Omega \rightarrow \mathcal{X} $ be an $ \mathcal{X} $-valued function.
    \begin{enumerate}[(i)]
        \item The function $ f(\cdot ) $ is called an $ \mathcal{F} $-simple  function (or simple function when $ \mathcal{F} $ is clear) if 
        \begin{align}

            f (\cdot) = \sum_{j=1}^{k} \chi_{E_{j}}(\cdot) u_{j},
        \end{align}
        for some $ k \in \mathbb{N} $, $ u_{j} \in \mathcal{X} $, and $ \{E_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k} \subset \mathcal{F} $.
        Here, $ \chi_{E}(\cdot) $ is the characteristic function of $ E $.

        \item The function $ f(\cdot ) $ is said to be strongly $ \mathcal{F} $-measurable with respect to $ \mu $ (or simplify strongly measurable) if there exist simple functions $ \{ f_{k} \}_{k = 1}^{\infty} $ converging to $ f $ in $ \mathcal{X} $, $ \mu $-a.e.
    \end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:41:11.636028+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a66166b45a6d6bf7c63154cb56f8486656d32ee46e4e4c90d0a34f9d86fa1148","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma terms_in_signature_second of arXiv:2411.13707, 'High-degree cubature on Wiener space \\ through unshuffle expansions'] \[
\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big (4(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij})+(\xi_i,\xi_i,\xi_j,\xi_j)\Big ) .
\]
\begin{proof}
Representation (equation eq:expansion of the paper) combined with (cor:parity of the paper) gives,
\begin{equation}
\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)=\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)\Big )+\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)\Big ).
\end{equation}
Expanding the symmetric product as a pure tensor and applying linearity we get,
\[
\mathrm{e}^{\star k}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}(\epsilon_i^2,\epsilon_j^2)\Big )=\mathrm{e}^{\star k}\Big (\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2\Big )=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\mathrm{e}^{\star k} (\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2 ).
\]
For \(k=2\):
\[
\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}(\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2)=2(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_j,\xi_{iij})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij}) .
\]
Substituting \(\xi_{iij}=\xi_{jii}\) (reversal property) before re-indexing the middle term \((i,j)\mapsto (j,i)\) we get,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\mathrm{e}^{\star 2}(\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2)&=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big (2(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_j,\xi_{jii})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij})\Big )\\
&=\sum_{1\leq i,j\leq d}\Big (4(\xi_i,\xi_{ijj})+2(\xi_{ij},\xi_{ij})\Big ) .
\end{aligned}
\end{equation}
For \(k=4\):
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{e}^{\star 4}(\epsilon_i^2\epsilon_j^2)=(\xi_i,\xi_i,\xi_j,\xi_j) .
\end{equation}
Substituting (equation eq:k2_iijj of the paper) and (equation eq:k4_iijj of the paper) into (equation eq:proof_ei2_ej2 of the paper) we obtain the required result.
\end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:37:20.358682+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_a664b9dd185b2413fb7cad46b46c92d559ddec93a4c21d6fb1ca9da4d455e56f","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem clt for negative graphons of arXiv:2411.13062, 'Graphon-Theoretic Approach to Central Limit Theorems for $\epsilon$-Independence'] Let $(Q_n)_{n \ge 1}$ be a sequence of symmetric $n\times n$ matrices with entries in $[-1,1]$. Assume that $w_{Q_n} \to w$ in the graphon sense. Let $s_1,\ldots,s_n \in \mathcal{A}$ be a mixed $q$-Gaussian system, for every $n \ge 1$. Then 
