{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T14:09:34.472636+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab99600fb97892ea2875a3b8cee2f07868ca4cf94a8fa5e41182b80fc18de55d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:flowpoincare of arXiv:2505.12187, 'Speeding up quantum Markov processes through lifting'] Let $P_t$ be a hypocoercive $C_0$-semigroup on $\mathcal{H}$ with the equilibrium subspace $\mathcal{F}$ as defined in (equation eq:mixsubspace of the paper). Suppose that Assumptions (assp:locoercive of the paper), (assmp:decomposition of the paper), and (assump:est1 of the paper) hold. Then, for any time horizon $T > 0$, and any $x_0 \in \mathcal{F}^\perp \cap \dom(\mathsf{L})$, there holds: 
\begin{align} 
  \alpha_T  \lVertx_t\rVert_{T, \mathcal{H}}^2 \le \mathcal{E}_{T, \mathsf{L}_S}(x_t)\,, \quad \alpha_T := \frac{1}{\gamma^2 C_0(T) + C_1(T)}\,, 
\end{align}
where $x_t: = P_t x_0$, and 
\begin{align*}
    & C_0(T): = 2 c_3^2 K_0^2\,, \\  & C_1(T): = 2 \left(s_{\rm M}^{1/2} \lambda_S^{-1/2} c_4  + K_3 c_2  +  \lambda_S^{-1/2} (K_1 c_1 + K_2 c_3) \right)^2 + \lambda_S^{-1}\,,
\end{align*}
where the constants $\{c_i\}_{i = 1,2,3,4}$ are given in (divergencelemma of the paper), $s_{\rm M}$ is defined in (equation eq:eigss of the paper), and constants $\{K_i\}_{i = 0,1,2,3}$ are given in (equation asspc0 of the paper)--(equation asspc2 of the paper) from (assump:est1 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:55:42.223901+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab9adfdcb63d083d0ec63bb07296c77010d713d998912c354783f45b90334d13","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm conv-DL-infty of arXiv:2411.16376, 'Higher order approximations in arcsine laws for subordinators'] Under the assumptions of Theorem $(Thm:potential-infty of the paper)$ with  $\alpha_0=\alpha\in[0,1]$, let us define a function $\varepsilon_2:(0,+\infty)\to\ensuremath{\mathbb{R}}$ by
\begin{align}
    \Pi((x,+\infty))=
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{\alpha>0\}}
    \frac{c_0 x^{-\alpha}}{\Gamma(1-\alpha)}+\varepsilon_2(x),
    \quad 0<x<+\infty.	
\end{align}
Set
\begin{align}
   \varepsilon_3(s,x)
   =
   s\Pi((s-sx,+\infty))\varepsilon_1(sx),
   \quad 0<s, x<+\infty.	
\end{align}
Then, for $s\in(0,+\infty)$, the finite measure 
\begin{align}
\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}\bigg(\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}\in dx,\; 0<\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}<1\bigg),
\quad
0<x<1,
\end{align}
 admits a density expressed in the form
\begin{align}
   s\Pi(&(s-sx,+\infty))u(sx)
   \\
   =\,&\frac{\sin(\pi \alpha)}{\pi}x^{-1+\alpha}(1-x)^{-\alpha}
   +
    \mathbbm{1}_{\{\alpha>0\}}
   \sum_{k=1}^n
   \frac{c_0 s^{\alpha_k-\alpha}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}x^{-1+\alpha_k}(1-x)^{-\alpha}
   \\
   &+\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{s^{\alpha_k}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)}
   x^{-1+\alpha_k}\varepsilon_2(s-sx)
   +
   O(\varepsilon_3(s,x)),
   \quad
   \text{as $s\to+\infty$,}

\end{align}
uniformly in $x\in I$ for any closed interval $I\subset (0,1)$. 
Therefore, for any closed interval $I\subset (0,1)$,
\begin{align}
	\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}\bigg(&\frac{X_{T(s)-}}{s}\in I\bigg)
	\\
	=
	\,&\frac{\sin(\pi \alpha)}{\pi}\int_I x^{-1+\alpha}(1-x)^{-\alpha}\,dx
	\\
	&+
	 \mathbbm{1}_{\{\alpha>0\}}
	\sum_{k=1}^n
   \frac{c_0 s^{\alpha_k-\alpha}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)\Gamma(1-\alpha)}
   \int_I x^{-1+\alpha_k}(1-x)^{-\alpha}\,dx
   \\
   &+\sum_{k=0}^n\frac{s^{\alpha_k}}{c_k\Gamma(\alpha_k)}
   \int_I
   x^{-1+\alpha_k}\varepsilon_2(s-sx)\,dx
   +
  O\bigg(\sup_{x\in I}\varepsilon_3(s,x)\bigg),
   \quad
   \text{as $s\to+\infty$}.

\end{align}

\begin{Rem}
By Remark (Rem:Pi-infty of the paper), if $0<\alpha<1$, then $\varepsilon_2(x)=o(x^{-\alpha})$, as $x\to+\infty$.	
\end{Rem} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:25:39.086243+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ab9b5c00a6992a03392b0b80b827190b431c59e0e963a6a317cc6c670390a69c","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition cor:non_cross of arXiv:2606.12143, 'Continuous stochastic flows driven by white noise and their duals'] Suppose that $(\sigma,b)$ satisfy Assumption~(assu:H of the paper) and conditions (item:1a of the paper), (item:1b of the paper), (item:2c of the paper), and (item:2d of the paper). Then, almost surely, for all $(a_1,r_1), (a_2,r_2) \in \mathbb D^2$ and all $z \ge y \ge r_1\vee r_2$,
