{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:25:41.601366+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b0fe3fcda99d777af99b0d5aae5b4989f7bca8c7789b8578a906588d1d2ae53a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 43 of arXiv:2608.12947, 'Branch-stationary max-stable fields on rooted trees'] Assume (equation 42 of the paper) and define the law of a random field
\(Z^{Q,\pi}\) on \(\mathcal T\) by
\begin{equation}
  \Ehat{H(Z^{Q,\pi})}
  =
  \frac1{c_Q}
  \sum_{g\in\mathbb G}
  \E*{
    \rho_\pi(Q^{[g]})^\alpha
    H\left(
      \frac{Q^{[g]}}{\rho_\pi(Q^{[g]})}
    \right)
  }, \qquad H \in \mathfrak H.

\end{equation}  On
\(\{\rho_\pi(Q^{[g]})=0\}\), the ratio in
(equation 44 of the paper) may be defined arbitrarily and the
corresponding summand is understood to be zero. If
\(\widehat{\mathbb P}_\pi\) stands for the resulting probability measure,
then:
\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
\item (equation 44 of the paper) is a probability law,
\[
  \rho_\pi(Z^{Q,\pi})=1
  \quad\text{almost surely},
  \qquad
  \Ehat{(Z_v^{Q,\pi})^\alpha}=1,
  \quad v\in\mathcal T;
\]
in particular, \(Z^{Q,\pi}\neq\mathbf 0\) almost surely;
\item \(Z^{Q,\pi}\) is almost surely \(\alpha\)-summable, i.e.,
\[
  \sum_{v\in\mathcal T}(Z_v^{Q,\pi})^\alpha<\infty;
\]
\item for every \(F\in\mathfrak H_\alpha\),
\begin{equation}
  \Ehat{F(Z^{Q,\pi})}
  =
  \frac1{c_Q}
  \sum_{g\in\mathbb G}\E{F(Q^{[g]})}.

\end{equation}
Consequently, \(Z^{Q,\pi}\) is branch-BR stationary, and its
homogeneous class does not depend on \(\pi\);
\item its tail measure is
\begin{equation}
  \nu_Q(A)
  =
  \frac1{c_Q}
  \sum_{g\in\mathbb G}
  \int_0^\infty
  \mathbb P\{rQ^{[g]}\in A,\ Q^{[g]}\neq\mathbf 0\}
  \alpha r^{-\alpha-1}\mathrm d r,
  \qquad A\in\mathcal B(\mathcal F),

\end{equation}
and satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \nu_Q\bigl((B^u)^{-1}A\bigr)
  =
  \nu_Q(A),
  \qquad
  A\in\mathcal B(\mathcal F^\circ),\quad u\in\mathcal T;

\end{equation}
\item the root spectral tail field \(\Theta^Q\) is independent of
\(\pi\) and satisfies
\begin{equation}
  \E{H(\Theta^Q)}
  =
  \frac1{c_Q}
  \sum_{g\in\mathbb G}
  \E*{
    \mathbf 1_{\{Q_g>0\}}
    Q_g^\alpha
    H\left(
      \left(\frac{Q_{gv}}{Q_g}\right)_{v\in\mathcal T}
    \right)
  }, \qquad F\in\mathfrak H.

\end{equation}
The summand is understood to be zero on \(\{Q_g=0\}\). In particular, $
  \mathbb P\{\sum_{v\in\mathcal T}(\Theta_v^Q)^\alpha<\infty\}=1$.
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:56:37.657623+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b0ff9a97167e36d28b831910328362c124e8b7657c44601c87cb6c524094c3ce","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:cascade of arXiv:2608.24830, 'Motion helps the contact process: survival below\\ the percolation threshold on Poisson Br'] Let $C_{\mathrm{ent}}=(2d+1)\,C_{\mathrm{box}}(d)^2$ with $C_{\mathrm{box}}$ as in
