{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T12:10:07.360708+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3974bb75bb68d6156dc32159252580d8d96a3accb3a4b00a1d07ebd7b95fa77","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem th:off-critical-decay of arXiv:2606.11503, 'Percolation on hierarchical lattices'] For any directed graph $G$ satisfying (e:hypothesis of the paper), we have
  \begin{enumerate}[\quad a)]
  \item 
    (Existence of correlation length and surface tension) The function $\xi: (0, p_\star) \to (0, \infty)$ given by
    \begin{equation}

      \xi(p) = - \lim_k \frac{\log f_k(p) }{\d_{1}(a_1, b_1)^k}
    \end{equation}
    is well-defined and non-decreasing.
    The non-increasing function $\eta: (p_\star, 1) \to (0, \infty)$,
    \begin{equation}

      \eta(p) = - \lim_k \frac{\log \big( 1- f_k(p) \big)}{\cut(G_{1})^k}
    \end{equation}
    is well-defined.
  \item 
    (Critical exponents for the correlation length and surface tension) There exist constants $\uc{c:corr_exp_1}, \uc{c:corr_exp_2} > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation}

      \uc{c:corr_exp_1} (p_\star - p)^{-\nu}
      \leq \xi(p)
      \leq \uc{c:corr_exp_2} (p_\star - p)^{-\nu},
      \text{ for $p \in (p_\star/2, p_\star)$}
    \end{equation}
    where $\nu = \log_\zeta \d_{1}(a_1, b_1)$.
    Moreover, there exist constants $\uc{c:surf_exp_1}, \uc{c:surf_exp_2} > 0$ such that
    \begin{equation}

      \uc{c:surf_exp_1} (p - p_\star)^{-\mu}
      \leq \eta(p)
      \leq \uc{c:surf_exp_2} (p - p_\star)^{-\mu},
      \text{ for $p \in (p_\star, 1 - (1-p_\star)/2)$}
    \end{equation}
    with $\mu = \log_\zeta \cut(G_{1})$.
  \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:30:24.615481+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b39dab285075b791d637b19afd9c7f58bd2254b2c32b272062e58fdfe2ad8780","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition 5 of arXiv:2506.09592, 'Extremal process of the local time of simple\\random walk on a regular tree'] Given a locally-finite random point measure $\eta$ on~$\R$, a Branching Random Walk with step distribution~$\eta$ started at $x\in\R$ is a sequence
$\{\xi_k \}_{k \ge 0}$
of random locally-finite point measures on~$\R$ such that $\xi_0=\delta_x$ a.s.\ and, denoting
$\scrF_k:=\sigma(\xi_j\colon j\le k)$,
\begin{equation}

E\bigl(\texte^{-\langle\xi_{k+1},f\rangle}\,|\,\scrF_k\bigr) =\texte^{-\langle\xi_k,\tilde f\rangle}\quad\text{where}\quad\tilde f(h):=
- \log E \bigl(\texte^{-\langle \eta, f(h+\cdot) \rangle} \bigr)
\end{equation}
holds for all $k\ge0$ and all continuous $f\colon\R\to[0,\infty)$  with compact support.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T19:59:45.008575+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3a7afe1075755dd847af9ce734bb7c4a262224e33e497bf690a7ba0c567a9e8","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma inlarge of arXiv:2605.12777, 'Mesoscopic Rates of Convergence for Complex Wishart Matrices at the Leftmost Spectrum Edge'] For any $r\ge0$ and $t\in\left[\frac{e_0\tilde{\mu}_{n,N}}{\tilde{\sigma}_{n,N}},\infty\right)$, when $N$ is sufficiently large, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        |G_{\tau}(t)|,~|H_{\tau}(t)|\le N^{-r}C(\gamma,r)e^{-\frac{t}{2}}
    \end{equation*}
    where $C(\gamma,r)$ is a constant determined by $\frac{N}{N+a}\rightarrow\gamma\in(0,1)$ and $r$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T23:07:56.653341+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3ab19686875729537e94e99effca2e437fad8efec9f734e2375a92209c2b00b","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition ASsSCAAS of arXiv:2411.04590, 'Singular stochastic delay equations driven by fractional Brownian motion: Dynamics, longti'] Assume \( \frac{1}{3}<\beta< \gamma< H\) and let \(\mathbf{B}\) be the delayed \(\gamma\)-rough path cocycle constructed in Theorem (thm:B_delayed_cocycle of the paper). Consider the same conditions on \(A\), \(\pi\), and \(G\) as specified in Proposition (BBSTAB of the paper). We consider the following linear rough delay equation:
			\begin{equation}
				\begin{aligned}
					\mathrm{d}y_t &= A \left( y_t, \int_{-r}^{0} y_{\theta+t} \, \pi(\mathrm{d}\theta) \right) \mathrm{d}t + \epsilon G(y_t, y_{t-r}) \, \mathrm{d}\mathbf{B}_t(\omega), \\
					y_{\theta} &= \xi_{\theta} \quad \text{for} \quad \theta \in [-r, 0], \quad \xi \in \mathscr{D}_{\mathbf{B}(\omega)}^{\beta, \gamma}([-r,0]),
				\end{aligned}
			\end{equation}
			where \( y^{\xi, \epsilon}_t(\omega) \) denotes the solution. Let \( \lambda_1 \) be as defined in (equation TRFVV of the paper), and assume \( \lambda_1 < 0 \). Then, there exists \( \epsilon_0 > 0 \) such that for every \( 0 < \epsilon < \epsilon_0 \), there exists \( \lambda^\epsilon < 0 \) such that for any \( \xi \in  \mathscr{D}_{\mathbf{B}(\omega)}^{\beta, \gamma}([-r,0]) \), the following holds:
			\begin{equation}
				\limsup_{t \to \infty} \frac{1}{t} \log | y^{\xi, \epsilon}_t(\omega) | \leq \lambda^\epsilon.
			\end{equation}
			In particular, our stability result holds for every \(\xi \in C^{2\gamma}([-r, 0], \mathbb{R}^n)\), where \(\frac{1}{3} < \gamma < H\) and \(\gamma\) can be chosen arbitrarily close to \(\frac{1}{3}\).","status":"unverified"}

