{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:53:00.814480+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3c1882c84e616d2a1680abab08b4a5e55fb99576a80cbd6824a36bd965be20f","kind":"definition","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Definition def:type_path_induced of arXiv:2607.16911, 'Multipartite random graphs with given degrees: local limit, revisiting the giant, distance'] Fix $s_0,s_1 \in S$. Consider any rooted tree $\mathbb T$ with vertex types in $S$. For any vertex $v \in \mathbb T$ with $T_{p(v)}=s_0, T_v=s_1$, and a type path/ray $\pi=(s_0\rightarrow s_0s_1 \rightarrow s_0s_1s_2\rightarrow \dots)$, we define the subtree $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ of $\mathbb T$ to be spanned by the vertices
    \begin{align*}
        \{v\}\cup \left(\cup_{i=2}^{|\pi|}C(e;\pi_{(i)})  \right),
    \end{align*}
    where $e$ is the directed edge $(p(v),v)$. We naturally think of $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ to be a rooted tree, rooted at $v$. We call $\mathbb T(v;\pi)$ the subtree of $v$ in $\mathbb T$ induced by $\pi$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T09:36:31.240104+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3d0a7f39964791233e061afe4581b13ce13d67c271dbb0e0049a9096ab3708c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 65 of arXiv:2609.00567, 'Hodge--Tate splitting and Akizuki--Nakano vanishing in positive characteristic'] Let $k$ be a perfect field and let $X$ be a smooth projective scheme over $W(k)$.
If the closed fiber $X_{\F_p}$ is HT-split, then for every ample line bundle $L$ on $X$, the following vanishings hold;
\begin{align*}
    H^i_{(p)}(X,\Omega^j_{X/W(k)} \otimes L^{-1})=0 & \quad (i+j<\dim X), \\
    H^i(X_{\F_p}, \Omega^j_{X_{\F_p}} \otimes L_{\F_p}^{-1}) = 0 & \quad (i + j < \dim X - 1), \\
    H^i(X, \Omega^j_{X/W(k)} \otimes L^{-1}) = 0 & \quad (i + j < \dim X - 1 ),
\end{align*}
where \(H^i_{(p)}(X, -)\) is the \(i\)-th cohomology of \(R\Gamma_{(p)}(X, -)\).","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T18:51:26.726287+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3d2f68305c0dfd0f27ff4fc9f15130e22c9bf03150ce3eb4d723b0876b3100a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Cor quadratic-log of arXiv:2606.08325, 'Digit Mixing under Polynomial Maps'] There exists an absolute constant \(A_0>0\) such that if, for all sufficiently large \(n\),
			\begin{equation}
				v_n\ge A\left(\frac{\log n}{n}\right)^{1/2}
			\end{equation}
			with \(A\ge A_0\), then \(X^2\) is almost surely absolutely normal. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T11:09:49.780882+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3d5686f35ae7ebe0e4bb572a2778579ab7d2883724f6e6959ff80e60bd28d6d","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 1 of arXiv:2609.03054, 'The classes of bivariate Schur and Herglotz matrix-valued rational functions: realizations'] Let $F\in  \Cmats{m}{n}(z_1,z_2)$ be rational matrix function. Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}[\rm (i)]
    \item $F$ has no poles in  $\DD^2$ and 
$\norm{F(z_1,z_2)}\leq 1$ for all $z_1,z_2\in\DD$;
    \item there exists a contractive matrix
$T =\matr{A&B\\C&D}$ such
that for all $z_1,z_2\in \DD$
\begin{equation}
F(z_1,z_2)= D+ C(z_1P_1+z_2P_2)\big(I - A(z_1P_1+z_2P_2) \big)^{-1}B
\end{equation}
where $P_1, P_2$ are complementary orthogonal projections.
\end{enumerate} 
Additionally, if $F\in\mathbb R^{m\times n}(z_1,z_2)$ then the matrices $A,B,C,D,P_1,P_2$ in {\rm (ii)} may be chosen as real matrices.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:48:43.533733+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3e856695a76143674a56a386ed96174dcc44f6434bf824ca521d04bc478b6cd","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 5 of arXiv:2608.06646, 'Three-dimensional stochastic wave equation with non-Lipschitz coefficients'] Suppose the initial values $u_0$, $v_0$ and the spatial covariance function $f$ satisfy the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{T:MainTheorem}. Let $u$ be the solution to~\eqref{E:SWE}. Then, there exist constants $c,C>0$ such that for all $p\ge 2$, $T>0$, $0\le t\le  t'\le T$, $t'-t<1$ and $x\in\R^3$,
    \begin{align}
    \Norm{u (t',x)-u (t,x)}_p^2\le& C\left[1+\lr{L_{b}^2+pL_{\sigma }^2}Me^{c\beta t'}\right]|t'-t|^{2\mu}\,,
\end{align}
where $0<\mu<\overline{\mu}=\frac{1}{2}\min \{2\mu_2,\nu+1,2\overline{\gamma},\overline{\gamma}+\mu_1\}$ with $\bar\gamma = \min\{{\alpha}_1, \alpha_2, \alpha_3, \gamma_1, \gamma_2\}$, $\beta={\sqrt{L_{b }}+p^{\frac{1}{\overline{\nu}+1}}L_{\sigma }^{\frac{2}{\overline{\nu}+1}}}$ with $\bar{\nu} = \min\{\nu, \nu_1, \nu_2\}$,   and $L_b, L_\sigma$ are the Lipschitz constants of $b$ and $\sigma$ respectively, $M$ is defined in \eqref{E:DefM}. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:35:10.799739+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_b3ea39e4d36c6a33963ee097ea31de27351b82c6678d8d5cbb80e10bc33b6a70","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemme 71 of arXiv:2604.00635, 'Large deviations of the periodic Toda chain'] If $\ups_j = -\vsg $, $\vsg \in \{\pm\}$, then one has the upper bound

