{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.590830+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba4e71fe9298e30fbc18337259a78f7c26ca512088e2cf15e8cb06bba12047b9","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition WPD-NP-main of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] For any $N \in \left\{ {0,1, \cdots ,10} \right\} $, $n \in {\mathbb{Z}^ + } \cup \left\{ 0 \right\}$ and $t \in \mathrm{supp}_t\ w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}$, there exists a real number $\alpha \in \left( {\frac{\beta }{{b}},\beta } \right)$ such that the $\left( {n + 1} \right)$-step Newtonian perturbation $w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}\left( {t,x} \right)$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}} \right\|_N} \lesssim \mu _{q + 1}^{ - 1}{\delta _{q + 1}}\lambda _q^{N + 1}l_q^{ - \alpha }  ,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
{\left\| {{{\bar D}_{t,q}}w_{q + 1,n + 1}^{(n)}} \right\|_N} \lesssim {\delta _{q + 1}}\lambda _q^{N + 1}l_q^{ - \alpha }  .
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T13:57:59.849820+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba52067442f60514910b75b819f47ab1b451c70411d7cecba8da946208061b6a","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:conv_in_hidaspace of arXiv:2606.11951, 'On Skorokhod Problems for Reflected and Singular Stochastic Heat Equations'] Let $(\Phi_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a sequence in $(\mathcal{S})'$. If the corresponding sequence of $U$-functionals $(U_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}} \coloneqq (\mathrm{S}^{-1}\Phi_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ satisfies both of the following two conditions
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $(U_n(\varphi))_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$ is a Cauchy sequence for all $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})$\text{, and}
		\item there exist $p$, $N\in\mathbb{N}$ and $A$, $B\in\mathbb{R}_{\geq0}$ s.th.\
		\begin{equation*}
			|U_n(z\varphi)| \leq A \exp(B|z|^2\|\varphi\|_p^2)\quad \text{for all $z\in\mathbb{C}$, $\varphi \in \mathcal{S}(\mathbb{R})$ and $n\geq N$}.
		\end{equation*}
	\end{enumerate}
	Then, the point-wise limit $U\coloneqq \lim_{n \to \infty} U_n$ is a $U$-functional, and we have $\Phi_n \to \mathrm{S}^{-1}(U) \eqqcolon \Phi$ strongly in $(\mathcal{S})'$ as $n\to\infty$. The analogous statement holds if the $\mathrm{S}$-transform is exchanged for the $\mathrm{T}$-transform. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-28T00:26:20.702581+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba5311f7880178932663902ae16b025eb40cc51178d0194a83ad435764ffbd7d","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lem laplace of arXiv:2411.12149, 'Airy limit for $\beta$-additions through Dunkl operators'] There exists an increasing sequence of positive integers $N_{1}<N_{2}<\cdots$, such that as $k\to\infty$, $\{\lambda_{i}'(N_{k})/\mu_{+}(N_{k})\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}}$ converges weakly to a point process $\eta_{1}\ge\eta_{2}\ge \cdots$ in $\mathbb{R}\cup \{-\infty\}$, and for any $\mathbf{k}_{1},\ldots,\mathbf{k}_{l}>0$,
\begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}\left[\prod_{j=1}^{l}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\exp\left(\frac{\mathbf{k}_{j}\eta_{i}}{\mu_{+}}\right)\right)\right]=\mathbf L_\beta(\left(2P_{-1}\sigma\right)^{-2/3}\vec\mathbf{k}, \vec\boldsymbol{\tau}=0).
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:56:36.591702+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba536723ecd34bfcd1c9c18787fad4d9433b68713f409d1905a5c6748605ec97","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma STN-HCS-A of arXiv:2608.30582, 'The flexible part of intermittent Onsager theorem for the two-dimensional stochastic incom'] Assume that $f\left( x \right)$ and $g\left( x \right)$ are in ${C^{N + \alpha}}\left( {{\mathbb{T}^2}} \right)$ for $N \in {\mathbb{Z}^ + } \cup \left\{ 0 \right\} $ and $\alpha \in \left[ {0,1} \right)$. If $f\left( x \right)g\left( x \right)$ is in ${C^{N + \alpha}}\left( {{\mathbb{T}^2}} \right)$, we then have
\begin{equation*}
{\left\| {fg} \right\|_{N + \alpha }} \lesssim {\left\| f \right\|_{N + \alpha }}{\left\| g \right\|_0} + {\left\| f \right\|_0}{\left\| g \right\|_{N + \alpha }}  .
\end{equation*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:45:07.268355+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba540918dbc1ec23e59a2007e0ea81edd04415e2ca8bd9388a26899435c0bd6c","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Thm 12 of arXiv:2604.20435, 'Stochastic Extinction with Relaxed Boundedness Conditions'] Suppose 
	\begin{equation}
		\lambda_I= -\int_{[0,\infty)\times\mathcal S} \left(f(s, 0,\alpha)-c_2(\alpha)-\frac{\sigma_2^2}2\right)\pi(ds,d\alpha)>0.
	\end{equation}
Then, $$\PP_{s, i, r, \alpha}\left\{\lim_{t\to\infty} \frac{\ln I(t)}t=-\lambda_I<0, \lim_{t\to\infty}\frac{\ln R(t)}t=-\lambda_R\wedge\lambda_I<0\right\}=1, \text{ for all } (s,i, r,\alpha)\in(0,\infty)^3\times\mathcal S.$$
where
	$$\lambda_R=\sum_{\alpha\in\mathcal S}\left(c_3(\alpha)+\frac{\sigma_3^2(\alpha)}2\right)\pi(\{\alpha\})$$","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:40:21.310973+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba586e2b96a3cd675e07e96b8c88f3840ea30d5ff8fd9e0192d7190cd0cbc0f1","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem 9 of arXiv:2604.08127, 'Asymptotics of Brownian occupation measures \\ with unusually large intersections'] For any $d \ge 1$, $p \ge 2$ such that $d(p-1) < 2p$ and any $0 < \gamma < \frac{2p}{d(p-1)}$, the distribution $\widehat{\mathbb{P}}_t^{\otimes p} \circ (\widetilde{L}_t^{\otimes p})^{-1}$ under the Gibbs measure \eqref{eq:mutual-gibbs-measure} satisfies an LDP in $\widetilde{\mathcal{X}}^{\otimes p}(\mathbb{R}^d)$ with good rate function
    \[
    \mathcal{I}_{d, p, \gamma}^{\mathrm{Gibbs}}(\xi^{\otimes p}) = \begin{cases}
    \displaystyle \mathcal{I}(\xi^{\otimes p}) - p\biggl(\sum_{i \in I} \Big\| \prod_{j=1}^p \psi_i^j \Big\|_2^2 \biggr)^{\gamma/p} + p \cdot \rho_{d, p, \gamma} & \text{if } \mathcal{I}(\xi^{\otimes p}) < \infty\\
    \infty & \text{otherwise},
    \end{cases}
    \]
    where $\mathcal{I}(\cdot)$ and $\rho_{d, p, \gamma}$ are defined in \eqref{eq:rate-function} and \eqref{eq:mutual-tilted-var}, respectively. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T21:12:46.874261+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_ba5b6410cc45b516cf82d6e5f1928a95e668611d55fea73366c793e6d5ea15bc","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Theorem thm:hill-moment of arXiv:2608.08375, 'Algebraic formulas for first-passage times of Markov processes in the linear framework: ge'] With the same assumptions as at the start of this section, choose $z \in Z$. Let $W$ be as defined in the statement of Lemma~(cor:hill-2 of the paper) and let $\Omega$ be as defined in the statement of Theorem~(thm:hill-moment-Z of the paper). Then the $r$-th moment of the conditional FPT from $u$ to $z$ is given by,
