\item[(ii)] $p_n \sim q_n \sim \pi_n$.
\end{enumerate}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-26T19:21:52.417155+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd68f23a4addeafe71df959b5d180df899fc4c09866cb91087b0c494d6896ae6","kind":"theorem","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 101 of arXiv:2601.16377, '2601.16377'] Consider a variational datum satisfying Assumptions~\ref{Assumption_A}, \ref{Assumption_B} and \ref{Assumption_C} with $H=1$ and admitting a single band --- we drop the indices $h$ from the notations. Let $\Delta V(z)$ be a holomorphic function in a complex neighborhood of $[\hat a', \hat b']$. Consider the family of variational data parameterized by a real $\epsilon$ in a small enough neighborhood of $0$ and differing from the original variational datum only by the potential $V^t(x) = V(x) + \epsilon \Delta V(x)$. Then, denoting with exponent ${}^{\epsilon}$ all the associated quantities, $\epsilon \mapsto \I^\epsilon[\boldsymbol{\mu}^{\epsilon}]$ is differentiable at $\epsilon = 0$ and its derivative can be computed as
 \begin{equation}

\partial_{\epsilon}\I^\epsilon[\mu^\epsilon]\big|_{\epsilon =0}=-\int_{\hat a'}^{\hat b'} \Delta V(x)\,\mu^0(x)\dd x.
 \end{equation}","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T06:28:08.698219+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bd6921690e18784baed97a3e72463788f3e5903f2d6a9b4726d3a98ebd6f4608","kind":"concept","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Proposition 16 (Static and pair-specific score weighting) of arXiv:2607.29560, 'Tractable Relaxations of Multivariate Stochastic Dominance via Optimal Transport and CVaR'] For every $0\le\gamma\le1$,
