 admits a unique weak solution,	where $A^*$ stands for the transpose of matrix $A$, $x_0=(z_0,z_0)$, and $\beta$ is a 2-dimensional standard Brownian motion. The corresponding   law of the solution coincides with the unique minimiser $P^*$ of the \sch problem $\mathcal{S}_E(\mu_0,\mu_1)$ in (equation P:unconstrainedeps of the paper). In particular, for every $t\in [0,1]$, $\frac{dP^*}{dR}|_{\cF_t}=h(t,X_t)$.","status":"unverified"}

{"assumptions":[],"created_at":"2026-10-27T20:51:52.061138+00:00","domain":"probability theory","id":"claim_bfdb098bf1b9be86a6fa11d58bf85ace310a38cb13bb75087a04109b6c28b667","kind":"lemma","schema_version":1,"statement":"[Lemma lem:ineq-pivot-site-bond of arXiv:2607.14993, 'Sharp phase transition for percolation with short-range dependencies'] Let $0 < p, q < 1$ and $n \in \mathbb N^*$. Then for $e \in V_1$ with neighbours $x, y$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(e \text{ pivotal for } A_n) \leqslant \frac qp (\mathbb P(x \text{ pivotal for } A_n) + \mathbb P(y \text{ pivotal for } A_n))
  \end{equation}
  and, for $x \in V_0$,
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb P(x \text{ pivotal for } A_n) \leqslant \frac {(2d-1)p} {q (1-p)^{2d-1}} \sum\limits_{e \in \mathcal N(x)} \mathbb P(e \text{ pivotal for } A_n),
